TOPICS.push(...[{"id":"gtap-L02","name":"Day 2: signaling, binding and affinity","prof":"Gottlieb","lecture":"gtap-L02","cite":"Day 2 (9/23) — Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf","subs":[{"id":"gtap-review","name":"Day 1 review (ion channels)","cite":"transcript 9/23"},{"id":"gtap-gprot","name":"G proteins and tyrosine kinase receptors","cite":"slides ~3–~11"},{"id":"gtap-nuc","name":"Nuclear hormone receptors","cite":"slides ~12–~14"},{"id":"gtap-bond","name":"Drug–receptor binding forces","cite":"slides ~15–~17"},{"id":"gtap-site","name":"Orthosteric vs allosteric binding","cite":"slides ~18–~24"},{"id":"gtap-signal","name":"Signal transduction and cross-talk","cite":"slides ~25–~30"},{"id":"gtap-class","name":"Affinity, efficacy and drug classes","cite":"slides ~31–~44"},{"id":"gtap-kd","name":"Kd and affinity","cite":"slides ~45–~51"},{"id":"gtap-drc","name":"Potency and dose–response curves","cite":"slides ~52–~59"}]},{"id":"gtap-L04","name":"Day 4: inverse agonists, allosterics, antagonists","prof":"Gottlieb","lecture":"gtap-L04","cite":"Day 4 (9/28) — Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf (Part 1)","subs":[{"id":"gtap-recap","name":"Two-state recap: full and partial agonists","cite":"Day 3 deck slides ~31–~65 (lectured 9/28); Day 4 deck slide ~3"},{"id":"gtap-ia","name":"Inverse agonists","cite":"slides ~4–~24"},{"id":"gtap-allo","name":"Allosteric agonists and antagonists","cite":"slides ~25–~34, ~36–~42"},{"id":"gtap-antag","name":"Kinds of antagonism","cite":"slides ~35–~37"},{"id":"gtap-comp","name":"Competitive antagonist","cite":"slides ~43–~50, ~55"},{"id":"gtap-irrev","name":"Irreversible antagonist and spare receptors","cite":"slides ~51–~56"}]},{"id":"gtap-L10","name":"Day 10: adrenergic receptors, indirect-acting drugs, α1 and α2","prof":"Gottlieb","lecture":"gtap-L10","cite":"Day 10 (10/6) — PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf (slides 1–32 taught)","subs":[{"id":"gtap-rec","name":"Adrenergic receptors and their G proteins","cite":"slides 2–3"},{"id":"gtap-map","name":"Heart, circulation and the MAP equation","cite":"slides 4–7"},{"id":"gtap-ne","name":"Norepinephrine synthesis, release and removal","cite":"slide 8"},{"id":"gtap-class","name":"Direct, mixed and indirect agonists","cite":"slide 9"},{"id":"gtap-indirect","name":"Cocaine, amphetamines and methylphenidate","cite":"slides 10–12"},{"id":"gtap-mao","name":"MAO inhibitors and the cheese effect","cite":"slides 13–15"},{"id":"gtap-a1","name":"α1 receptors: phenylephrine, oxymetazoline","cite":"slides 16–19"},{"id":"gtap-a2","name":"α2 receptors: clonidine","cite":"slides 20–24, 26"},{"id":"gtap-a1ant","name":"α antagonists: prazosin and the -osins","cite":"slides 27–30"},{"id":"gtap-mirt","name":"Mirtazapine (α2 antagonist)","cite":"slides 31–32"}]}]);
QUESTIONS.push(
{id:'gtap-L02-001', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-review","skill":"tell","concept":"voltage-gated-opens-positive","tags":["poll"],"source":"transcript","stem":"Which of the following opens when a membrane potential becomes more positive?","options":[{"t":"Passive (always-open) ion channels","correct":false,"why":"Passive channels are always open; nothing has to change for them to stay open."},{"t":"Ligand-gated ion channels","correct":false,"why":"Ligand-gated channels open when a hormone, neurotransmitter or ingested substance binds them, not in response to membrane potential."},{"t":"Ion pumps","correct":false,"why":"Pumps use energy (ATP) to move ions back to homeostasis; they reset the system rather than open with depolarization."},{"t":"Voltage-gated calcium channels","correct":true,"why":"Voltage-gated calcium channels depend on membrane potential and open only when the membrane becomes more positive."}],"teach":"Ion channels are classed by what opens them. Passive channels (for example the funny (If) channel in the sinoatrial node) are always open; ligand-gated channels open when a ligand binds; voltage-gated channels open when the membrane potential becomes more positive; pumps use ATP to restore ion balance.","quote":"And then we have calcium channels, which are voltage-gated calcium channels that only opens when the membrane becomes more positive, right?","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Signaling Mechanisms & Drug Action — Ion Channels (p. 9–10 of 18)","t":"Ligand-gated ion channels open when a ligand binds them. Voltage-gated ion channels do not bind neurotransmitters directly but are controlled by membrane potential, through a charged domain that serves as the voltage sensor for channel opening."}],"cite":"Day 1 material reviewed in class (no Day 2 slide); transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-002', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-gprot","skill":"tell","concept":"g-alpha-effector-messenger","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which pairing of G protein α subunit → effector → second messenger is correct? (AC = adenylate cyclase; PLC = phospholipase C; cAMP = cyclic AMP; IP3 = inositol phosphate 3)","options":[{"t":"Gαs → PLC → ↑ IP3, Ca++","correct":false,"why":"Gαs acts on AC and increases cAMP; PLC and IP3 belong to Gαq."},{"t":"Gαi → AC → ↑ cAMP","correct":false,"why":"Gαi is inhibitory; it acts on AC but suppresses cAMP."},{"t":"Gαq → PLC → ↑ IP3, Ca++","correct":true,"why":"Gαq activates PLC, which makes IP3; IP3 releases Ca++ from the endoplasmic reticulum."},{"t":"Gαq → AC → ↑ cAMP","correct":false,"why":"AC and cAMP go with Gαs (increase) and Gαi (decrease), not Gαq."},{"t":"Gαi → PLC → ↓ IP3 and Ca++","correct":false,"why":"Gαi acts on AC, not PLC."}],"teach":"The three α subunits are Gαs (stimulation), Gαi (inhibition) and Gαq (positive, through calcium). Gαs increases cAMP through AC, Gαi suppresses cAMP through AC, and Gαq increases IP3 and Ca++ through PLC. Adenylate cyclase pairs with cyclic AMP; phospholipase C pairs with IP3.","quote":"The alphai, which is the opposite, also is going to be associated with the AC, but in this case it's going to suppress the camp. And then the alpha Q is going to increase IP3 and calcium.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"G Proteins & Second Messengers; Table 2–1 (p. 10–11 of 18)","t":"Gs stimulates adenylyl cyclase, which converts ATP to cAMP, while Gi proteins decrease adenylyl cyclase and lower cAMP. Gq stimulates phospholipase C, which raises IP3, diacylglycerol and cytoplasmic calcium."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~3; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-003', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-gprot","skill":"apply","concept":"alpha2-gi-decreases-camp","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Norepinephrine binds an α2 adrenergic receptor in the central nervous system (CNS). The receptor is coupled to Gαi. What happens to cyclic AMP (cAMP) in that cell?","options":[{"t":"cAMP decreases","correct":true,"why":"Gαi inhibits adenylate cyclase, so less cAMP is made, giving less signaling and less excitation."},{"t":"cAMP increases","correct":false,"why":"An increase in cAMP is the Gαs (stimulatory) result, as with β1 receptors."},{"t":"cAMP is unchanged","correct":false,"why":"An agonist at a Gαi-coupled receptor activates the inhibitory pathway; it does not leave cAMP unchanged."},{"t":"IP3 (inositol phosphate 3) and Ca++ rise","correct":false,"why":"IP3 and Ca++ are the Gαq second messengers (for example α1 receptors), not Gαi."}],"teach":"An agonist activates whatever pathway the receptor is coupled to. The α2 adrenergic receptor is coupled to Gαi, which inhibits adenylate cyclase; less cAMP means less signaling, and drugs that activate α2 suppress sympathetic outflow. The pathway is reversed when the GTP is hydrolyzed back to GDP and norepinephrine unbinds.","quote":"What does the I stand for? Inhibitory, which means that it's going to cause an inhibition of the AC, and I'm going to have less camp being produced.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Table 2–1 G proteins and their receptors and effectors; Variation in Drug Responsiveness (p. 11, 17 of 18)","t":"α2-Adrenergic receptors couple to the Gi family of G proteins. Gi decreases adenylyl cyclase activity and therefore decreases cAMP."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~3; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-004', prof:'Gottlieb', "dupOf":"gtap-L02-049","lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-gprot","skill":"recall","concept":"gtp-exchange-dissociation","tags":["poll"],"source":"transcript","stem":"During activation of a G protein–coupled receptor (GPCR), which step triggers the α subunit to separate from the β and γ subunits?","options":[{"t":"GDP is replaced by GTP","correct":true,"why":"Removal of the diphosphate (GDP) and replacement with the triphosphate (GTP) triggers the subunits to separate."},{"t":"GTP is hydrolyzed to GDP","correct":false,"why":"Hydrolysis of GTP to GDP is the reversal signal that lets α, β and γ reassociate."},{"t":"RGS protein binds the α subunit","correct":false,"why":"The RGS protein speeds removal of the extra phosphate, which ends signaling."},{"t":"cAMP binds the receptor","correct":false,"why":"cAMP is a downstream second messenger made by adenylate cyclase; it does not bind the receptor."},{"t":"The agonist unbinds the receptor","correct":false,"why":"Agonist unbinding is part of reversing the pathway, not activating it."}],"teach":"Sequence: the agonist binds, the receptor is activated and changes shape, the G protein (transducer) senses the change, GDP comes off and GTP comes in, and the α subunit separates from βγ to act on an effector (adenylate cyclase or phospholipase C). Reversal: GTP is hydrolyzed to GDP naturally or faster with the RGS protein, the subunits reassociate, and the drug unbinds.","quote":"The GDP, as in diphosphate, has to come off, and the GTP, as in triphosphate, has to come in, right?","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"G Proteins & Second Messengers; Figure 2–10 and Figure 2–11 (p. 10–11 of 18)","t":"Agonist binding reduces the nucleotide affinity of the G protein, so GDP dissociates and GTP replaces it. This exchange switches the G protein from its inactive GDP-bound form to its active GTP-bound form."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~3; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-005', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-gprot","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"gprotein-reversal","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which events end or reduce signaling after a Gαs-coupled receptor has been activated? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"GTP is hydrolyzed to GDP","correct":true,"why":"Going from GTP to GDP is the signal for α, β and γ to reassociate."},{"t":"RGS protein removes the extra phosphate","correct":true,"why":"The RGS protein speeds up removal of the extra phosphate from GTP."},{"t":"PDE enzyme breaks down cAMP","correct":true,"why":"The PDE enzyme breaks down cyclic AMP (cAMP), reducing second-messenger signaling and amplification."},{"t":"The agonist unbinds the receptor","correct":true,"why":"Unbinding of the receptor is a trigger for things to reassociate back to where they were."},{"t":"GDP is replaced by GTP","correct":false,"why":"GDP-for-GTP exchange is the activating step, not a reversal step."},{"t":"The α subunit activates adenylate cyclase (AC)","correct":false,"why":"Activating AC makes more cAMP; it propagates the signal rather than ending it."}],"teach":"Every pathway has a way forward and a way backward. G protein signaling stops when GTP is hydrolyzed to GDP (naturally, or faster with the RGS protein), the subunits reassociate and the agonist unbinds. The PDE enzyme lowers cAMP, which decreases amplification and the physiological response.","quote":"I have enzymes over here, for example, that the phosphorusase enzyme, the PDE, is gonna come and chew up that camp, and if I don't have camp, I can't activate the next one and the next one and the next one, and that's going to decrease the second messenger signing.","note":"Lecture and textbook differ. The lecture lists the agonist coming off the receptor as one of the steps that ends the signal. The textbook says the G protein stays active for seconds after the agonist has left, so what ends the signal is the G protein splitting its GTP. Use the lecture list on the exam.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"G Proteins & Second Messengers; Well-Established Second Messengers — cAMP (p. 10, 13 of 18)","t":"G proteins hydrolyze GTP to GDP, and this hydrolysis inactivates the G protein. cAMP itself is degraded to 5′-AMP by cyclic nucleotide phosphodiesterases (PDEs)."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~3, ~29; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-006', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-gprot","skill":"recall","concept":"rtk-dimerization","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"An inactive receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK) is a monomer. What happens next when a ligand binds its extracellular domain?","options":[{"t":"Dimerization","correct":true,"why":"Ligand binding induces two receptors to join (dimerize), the active form."},{"t":"Tyrosine phosphatase activation","correct":false,"why":"Tyrosine phosphatase removes the phosphate at the end of signaling; it is the reversal step."},{"t":"GDP is exchanged for GTP","correct":false,"why":"GDP/GTP exchange belongs to G protein–coupled receptors, not RTKs."},{"t":"Cross-phosphorylation","correct":false,"why":"Cross-phosphorylation of the kinase domain follows dimerization; it is step 3, not step 2."},{"t":"Transfer to the nucleus","correct":false,"why":"RTKs cross the membrane once and signal through their intracellular kinase domain."}],"teach":"RTK activation steps: (1) the inactive receptor is monomeric, (2) ligand binding induces dimerization, (3) the kinase domains cross-phosphorylate, (4) phosphorylation forms docking sites and signaling complexes, (5) a tyrosine phosphatase removes the phosphate and resets the process.","quote":"1. Inactive receptor is monomeric state 2. Ligand binding induces Dimerization (active) 3. Cross-phosphorylation of the kinase domain 4. Phosphorylation forms docking sites & signaling complexes 5. Tyrosine Phosphatase (TP)","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Ligand-Regulated Transmembrane Enzymes Including Receptor Tyrosine Kinases; Figure 2–7 (p. 8 of 18)","t":"Ligand binding to the extracellular domain causes two receptor molecules to bind to one another, converting the receptor from its inactive monomeric state to an active dimeric state. Dimerization then activates the tyrosine kinase activity of the cytoplasmic domains."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~7–~9; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-007', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-gprot","skill":"apply","concept":"ras-block-downstream","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"In the tyrosine kinase pathway Grb2 → GEF → RAS → RAF → MEK → ERK, a drug X binds RAS and stops it from functioning. What is the expected result?","options":[{"t":"Reduced gene transcription and cell proliferation","correct":true,"why":"With RAS blocked, RAF, MEK and ERK are not activated, so cells do not grow or divide."},{"t":"Increased RAF, MEK and ERK signaling","correct":false,"why":"RAF, MEK and ERK are downstream of RAS; blocking RAS removes their activation."},{"t":"Increased gene transcription and cell proliferation","correct":false,"why":"That is the effect of the active pathway, the opposite of blocking RAS."},{"t":"Increased cyclic AMP production","correct":false,"why":"Cyclic AMP is a G protein (Gαs) second messenger, not part of this RAS pathway."}],"teach":"Receptor tyrosine kinases for insulin and growth factors signal through Grb2, GEF, RAS, RAF, MEK and ERK to gene transcription and cell proliferation. About 20–25% of cancers contain a RAS mutation, so these kinases are a good drug target in cancer. A drug that blocks RAS suppresses the cascade and behaves as an indirect antagonist.","quote":"Now, I don't have the RAF, I don't have the Mac, I don't have the ERC, my cells don't grow, my cells don't divide anymore, right?","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Receptor Classes & Drug Development; Phosphorylation: A Common Theme (p. 14 of 18)","t":"Inhibitors can target the GTP-binding transducer proteins (Ras) that function downstream of tyrosine kinase growth factor receptors. Activated Ras stimulates growth factor signaling pathways, so a Ras inhibitor blocks that signaling."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~10–~11, ~41; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-051', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-gprot","skill":"recall","concept":"rtk-activation-sequence","tags":["rtk"],"source":"both","stem":"Which statement is CORRECT about the activation of a receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK)?","options":[{"t":"Ligand binding induces dimerization, then cross-phosphorylation","correct":true,"why":"Steps 2 and 3 on his slide: dimerization is the active form, and cross-phosphorylation of the kinase domain follows it."},{"t":"Ligand binding exchanges GDP for GTP on the α subunit","correct":false,"why":"GDP/GTP exchange happens on a G protein; an RTK has no G protein."},{"t":"The inactive receptor is a dimer that splits into two separate monomers when the ligand binds to it","correct":false,"why":"This is the reverse: the inactive receptor is the monomer, and ligand binding brings two together."},{"t":"A tyrosine phosphatase adds the phosphates that start the signal","correct":false,"why":"The tyrosine phosphatase removes phosphates; it is step 5, the step that ends the signal."},{"t":"The receptor crosses the membrane seven times","correct":false,"why":"Seven transmembrane segments describe a GPCR; an RTK crosses the membrane once (1-TM)."}],"teach":"RTK activation steps: (1) the inactive receptor is a monomer, (2) ligand binding induces dimerization, the active form, (3) the kinase domains cross-phosphorylate each other, (4) the phosphorylated sites form docking sites and signaling complexes, (5) a tyrosine phosphatase removes the phosphates and ends the signal. An RTK is a 1-transmembrane receptor: binding pocket outside, enzyme (kinase) activity inside. It has no G protein.","quote":"1. Inactive receptor is monomeric state 2. Ligand binding induces Dimerization (active) 3. Cross-phosphorylation of the kinase domain 4. Phosphorylation forms docking sites & signaling complexes 5. Tyrosine Phosphatase (TP)","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~7–~9; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-052', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-gprot","skill":"recall","concept":"rtk-activation-order","tags":["rtk"],"source":"both","stem":"Put the activation of a receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK) in order, starting from the inactive receptor before the ligand binds.","options":[{"t":"Monomer → dimerization → cross-phosphorylation → docking sites → tyrosine phosphatase","correct":true,"why":"This is the five-step order on his slide."},{"t":"Monomer → cross-phosphorylation → dimerization → docking sites → tyrosine phosphatase","correct":false,"why":"Cross-phosphorylation needs two kinase domains side by side, so it follows dimerization."},{"t":"Dimer → split into two monomers → cross-phosphorylation → docking sites → tyrosine phosphatase","correct":false,"why":"The inactive receptor is the monomer; the ligand brings two receptors together."},{"t":"Monomer → dimerization → tyrosine phosphatase → docking sites → cross-phosphorylation","correct":false,"why":"The kinase domains add the phosphates; the tyrosine phosphatase removes them at the end."},{"t":"Monomer → GDP off, GTP on → α subunit leaves → second messenger","correct":false,"why":"These are G-protein steps from a GPCR, not RTK steps."}],"teach":"RTK activation steps: (1) the inactive receptor is a monomer, (2) ligand binding induces dimerization, the active form, (3) the kinase domains cross-phosphorylate each other, (4) the phosphorylated sites form docking sites and signaling complexes, (5) a tyrosine phosphatase removes the phosphates and ends the signal. An RTK is a 1-transmembrane receptor: binding pocket outside, enzyme (kinase) activity inside. It has no G protein.","quote":"1. Inactive receptor is monomeric state 2. Ligand binding induces Dimerization (active) 3. Cross-phosphorylation of the kinase domain 4. Phosphorylation forms docking sites & signaling complexes 5. Tyrosine Phosphatase (TP)","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~7–~9; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-053', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-gprot","skill":"recall","concept":"rtk-docking-sites","tags":["rtk"],"source":"both","stem":"After the kinase domains of an activated receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK) cross-phosphorylate, what do the phosphorylated sites form?","options":[{"t":"Docking sites and signaling complexes","correct":true,"why":"Step 4 on his slide: phosphorylation forms docking sites and signaling complexes, which carry the signal on."},{"t":"Cyclic AMP (cAMP)","correct":false,"why":"cAMP is made by adenylate cyclase in a Gs pathway, not by an RTK."},{"t":"A new binding pocket for the ligand outside the cell","correct":false,"why":"The ligand binds the extracellular domain; phosphorylation happens on the intracellular kinase domain."},{"t":"An open ion channel pore","correct":false,"why":"An RTK is an enzyme-linked receptor, not an ion channel."},{"t":"A free α subunit","correct":false,"why":"α subunits belong to G proteins; an RTK has none."}],"teach":"RTK activation steps: (1) the inactive receptor is a monomer, (2) ligand binding induces dimerization, the active form, (3) the kinase domains cross-phosphorylate each other, (4) the phosphorylated sites form docking sites and signaling complexes, (5) a tyrosine phosphatase removes the phosphates and ends the signal. An RTK is a 1-transmembrane receptor: binding pocket outside, enzyme (kinase) activity inside. It has no G protein.","quote":"1. Inactive receptor is monomeric state 2. Ligand binding induces Dimerization (active) 3. Cross-phosphorylation of the kinase domain 4. Phosphorylation forms docking sites & signaling complexes 5. Tyrosine Phosphatase (TP)","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~7–~9; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-054', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-gprot","skill":"recall","concept":"rtk-tyrosine-phosphatase","tags":["rtk"],"source":"both","stem":"Which enzyme ends receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK) signaling by removing the phosphates?","options":[{"t":"Tyrosine phosphatase","correct":true,"why":"Step 5 on his slide: tyrosine phosphatase removes the phosphates and resets the receptor."},{"t":"GTPase, sped up by RGS proteins","correct":false,"why":"GTPase cuts GTP to GDP on a G protein; that ends GPCR signaling, not RTK signaling."},{"t":"Phosphodiesterase (PDE)","correct":false,"why":"PDE breaks down cAMP in G-protein pathways."},{"t":"Adenylate cyclase (AC)","correct":false,"why":"AC makes cAMP; it does not remove phosphates from an RTK."},{"t":"Phospholipase C (PLC)","correct":false,"why":"PLC is the Gq effector that makes IP3."}],"teach":"RTK activation steps: (1) the inactive receptor is a monomer, (2) ligand binding induces dimerization, the active form, (3) the kinase domains cross-phosphorylate each other, (4) the phosphorylated sites form docking sites and signaling complexes, (5) a tyrosine phosphatase removes the phosphates and ends the signal. An RTK is a 1-transmembrane receptor: binding pocket outside, enzyme (kinase) activity inside. It has no G protein.","quote":"1. Inactive receptor is monomeric state 2. Ligand binding induces Dimerization (active) 3. Cross-phosphorylation of the kinase domain 4. Phosphorylation forms docking sites & signaling complexes 5. Tyrosine Phosphatase (TP)","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~7–~9; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-055', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-gprot","skill":"recall","concept":"rtk-reset-separate","tags":["rtk"],"source":"both","stem":"After tyrosine phosphatase removes the phosphates from an activated receptor tyrosine kinase (RTK), what happens to the two receptors?","options":[{"t":"They separate, and the process can start again","correct":true,"why":"His words: \"you stop the process, they separate from each other, and now you can start the whole process again.\""},{"t":"They stay paired for good and keep signaling at a lower level","correct":false,"why":"Removing the phosphates stops the signal, and the pair comes apart."},{"t":"They exchange GDP for GTP and release an α subunit","correct":false,"why":"GDP/GTP exchange and α subunits belong to G proteins; an RTK has neither."},{"t":"The tyrosine phosphatase adds new phosphates to restart them","correct":false,"why":"The phosphatase removes phosphates; it is the reset step."},{"t":"They are carried into the nucleus to change gene transcription","correct":false,"why":"That describes a nuclear receptor, not the RTK reset."}],"teach":"Reset of an RTK: tyrosine phosphatase cuts the phosphates off, the signal stops, the two receptors separate back into monomers, and the whole process can start again, the same way GTPase resets a G protein.","quote":"a tyrosine phosphatase with an enzyme that comes in and cuts that extra phosphate out, and as it does that, you stop the process, they separate from each other, and now you can start the whole process again","cite":"transcript 9/23; Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~9"},
{id:'gtap-L02-056', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-gprot","skill":"apply","concept":"rtk-cancer-overstimulation","tags":["rtk"],"source":"both","stem":"Many cancers show higher stimulation of receptor tyrosine kinase pathways, and 20–25% of cancers contain a RAS mutation. What happens to the cells?","options":[{"t":"They grow and divide very fast","correct":true,"why":"His words: \"a lot of cancers has a higher stimulation of those receptors. They are growing and dividing really, really fast.\""},{"t":"They stop growing and dividing","correct":false,"why":"He described the opposite: the cells grow and divide faster."},{"t":"They make more cyclic AMP through adenylate cyclase","correct":false,"why":"cAMP is the Gs pathway; the RTK pathway runs through RAS, RAF, MEK and ERK."},{"t":"They lose their receptors by internalization","correct":false,"why":"Internalization is receptor down-regulation, not what he described for cancer."},{"t":"They switch the pathway off with tyrosine phosphatase","correct":false,"why":"Tyrosine phosphatase is the normal reset; it does not explain fast growth."}],"teach":"RTKs carry growth-factor and insulin signals for cell growth and division. In many cancers the pathway is over-stimulated, and 20–25% of cancers contain a RAS mutation, so the cells grow and divide very fast. That is why a drug that stops RAS (drug X) stops the cells from growing.","quote":"a lot of cancers has a higher stimulation of those receptors. They are growing and dividing really, really fast","cite":"transcript 9/23; Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~10"},
{id:'gtap-L02-057', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-gprot","skill":"tell","concept":"ras-drug-indirect","tags":["rtk"],"source":"both","stem":"Drug X binds RAS and stops it from functioning. Which statement is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"The receptors still bind and dimerize, but RAF, MEK and ERK stay off","correct":true,"why":"Drug X acts downstream: the receptor works, and the message stops at RAS (indirect antagonist)."},{"t":"Drug X competes with the growth factor for the binding pocket on the receptor","correct":false,"why":"That is a competitive antagonist; drug X never binds the receptor."},{"t":"Drug X keeps the two receptors from pairing up after the growth factor binds","correct":false,"why":"Dimerization happens at the receptor, upstream of RAS, so it still occurs."},{"t":"Drug X raises RAF, MEK and ERK signaling, so the cells grow and divide faster","correct":false,"why":"Blocking RAS removes RAF, MEK and ERK: \"my cells don't grow, my cells don't divide.\""},{"t":"Drug X binds a second site on the receptor itself and lowers its affinity","correct":false,"why":"That describes an allosteric antagonist; drug X binds RAS, not the receptor."}],"teach":"Drug X is an indirect antagonist: it binds RAS, a component downstream of the receptor tyrosine kinase. The growth factor still binds and the receptors still dimerize and phosphorylate, but without RAS there is no RAF, MEK or ERK, so the cells do not grow or divide. On the growth factor curve it shifts right and lowers the Emax.","quote":"drug X that binds to that RAS protein and stop it from functioning ... I don't have the RAF, I don't have the Mac [MEK], I don't have the ERC [ERK], my cells don't grow, my cells don't divide anymore","cite":"transcript 9/23; Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~10–~11, ~41"},
{id:'gtap-L02-008', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-nuc","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"nuclear-receptor-ligands","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which ligands act through nuclear hormone receptors? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Androgens","correct":true,"why":"Androgens are listed among the steroid hormones acting on nuclear hormone receptors."},{"t":"Estrogens","correct":true,"why":"Estrogens are listed among the steroid hormones acting on nuclear hormone receptors."},{"t":"Glucocorticoids","correct":true,"why":"Glucocorticoids are listed; they affect DNA and messenger RNA production."},{"t":"Thyroid hormone","correct":true,"why":"Thyroid hormone is listed with the nuclear hormone receptor ligands."},{"t":"Insulin","correct":false,"why":"Insulin acts on a receptor tyrosine kinase, a one-transmembrane receptor."},{"t":"Epidermal growth factor","correct":false,"why":"Epidermal growth factor acts on a receptor tyrosine kinase."},{"t":"Norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Norepinephrine acts on G protein–coupled adrenergic receptors."}],"teach":"Nuclear hormone receptors are transcription factor regulators: the ligand (androgens, estrogens, glucocorticoids, thyroid hormone, vitamin D) crosses into the cell, binds the receptor and regulates gene expression, affecting DNA and messenger RNA production. Insulin and growth factors use receptor tyrosine kinases; norepinephrine uses G protein–coupled receptors.","quote":"Steroid hormones ... Androgens, estrogens, glucocorticoids, thyroid hormone, and Vit D ... Regulates gene expression","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Intracellular Receptors for Lipid-Soluble Agents (p. 7–8 of 18)","t":"Lipid-soluble ligands that cross the plasma membrane and act on intracellular receptors include the steroids (corticosteroids, mineralocorticoids, sex steroids and vitamin D) and thyroid hormone. Insulin and epidermal growth factor act instead on receptor tyrosine kinases, and norepinephrine acts on G protein–coupled adrenoceptors."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~5, ~12; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-009', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-nuc","skill":"apply","concept":"aldosterone-mr-mechanism","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Aldosterone binds the mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) in a collecting-duct principal cell. What is the result?","options":[{"t":"More pumps and channels; Na+ reabsorbed, K+ excreted","correct":true,"why":"MR activation codes for mRNA for more Na+/K+ ATPase pumps and channels; sodium is reabsorbed and potassium is excreted."},{"t":"Fewer pumps and channels; Na+ excreted, K+ retained","correct":false,"why":"That is the effect of blocking the MR (spironolactone), not of aldosterone."},{"t":"More pumps and channels; K+ reabsorbed and Na+ excreted","correct":false,"why":"Aldosterone is sodium saving and potassium wasting, not the reverse."},{"t":"Immediate opening of existing Na+ channels only","correct":false,"why":"The MR is a nuclear receptor; it works through messenger RNA to make new pumps and channels."}],"teach":"The MR is a nuclear receptor: aldosterone binding leads to messenger RNA coding for more pumps and channels in the principal cell. Sodium is reabsorbed (water follows) and potassium is excreted, so aldosterone is a sodium-saving, potassium-wasting hormone. Spironolactone is an MR antagonist that spares potassium and wastes sodium, a weak diuretic.","quote":"Aldosterone is going to come and bind to its receptor, the MR receptor, the immunocord cord receptor, and by binding to that MR receptor, it's gonna code for messenger RNA for more pumps and more channels.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Intracellular Receptors for Lipid-Soluble Agents (p. 7–8 of 18)","t":"Mineralocorticoids act on intracellular receptors that regulate gene transcription by binding to response elements on DNA. Their effects appear only after a lag period, the time required for synthesis of new proteins, so they cannot alter a physiologic state within minutes."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~13–~14; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-010', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-bond","skill":"recall","concept":"affinity-depends-on-bonds","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"Affinity of a drug for the receptor is dependent on the type of chemical bonds it makes with that receptor. True or false?","options":[{"t":"True","correct":true,"why":"Covalent, ionic, hydrogen and van der Waals bonds dictate how likely a drug is to bind and stay bound; the class answer was true."},{"t":"False","correct":false,"why":"Affinity is how likely a drug is to bind and stay bound, and that depends on the bonds it forms."}],"teach":"Affinity is how likely a drug is to bind and stay bound to a receptor. It depends on the molecular forces (bonds) between drug and receptor: covalent bonds are very strong, while van der Waals bonds are weak.","quote":"Remember, affinity is how likely a drug is to bind and stay bound to a receptor, and the binding of a drug to the receptor is going to be depending on what type of bonds they form covalent bonds, hydrogen bonds, ion bonds.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~15, ~31; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-011', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"irreversible","lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-bond","skill":"tell","multi":true,"concept":"covalent-irreversible-terms","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Drug binding through a covalent bond is described by which terms? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Irreversible","correct":true,"why":"Covalent bonds are irreversible at body temperature."},{"t":"Insurmountable","correct":true,"why":"Irreversible, insurmountable and non-competitive all mean the same property."},{"t":"Non-competitive","correct":true,"why":"Irreversible, insurmountable and non-competitive all mean the same property."},{"t":"Reversible","correct":false,"why":"Reversible describes ionic, hydrogen and van der Waals binding."},{"t":"Surmountable","correct":false,"why":"Surmountable describes weaker, reversible bonds that can be outcompeted."},{"t":"Competitive","correct":false,"why":"Competitive describes reversible binding."},{"t":"Short duration of action","correct":false,"why":"Covalent binding gives a long duration of action."}],"teach":"A covalent bond forms when two atoms share a pair of electrons; it is irreversible at body temperature and gives a long duration of action. Irreversible, insurmountable and non-competitive are three names for the same property. Ionic, hydrogen and van der Waals bonds are reversible, surmountable and competitive.","quote":"They can be classified as irreversible, insurmountable, and non-competitive, which means is They all mean the same thing.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists (p. 6 of 18)","t":"A noncompetitive antagonist, which often binds irreversibly by forming a covalent bond, cannot be surmounted by agonists irrespective of their concentration. Its duration of action depends on receptor turnover rather than on its own elimination."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~15; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-012', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-bond","skill":"drug","concept":"phenoxybenzamine-covalent","tags":["poll","pollev"],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following drugs is an irreversible antagonist?","options":[{"t":"Phenylephrine","correct":false,"why":"Phenylephrine is a full agonist on the drug list; it is reversible, it binds and pops off."},{"t":"Prazosin","correct":false,"why":"Prazosin is a reversible, competitive, surmountable antagonist that forms weaker bonds."},{"t":"Diazepam","correct":false,"why":"Diazepam is an allosteric agonist, and allosteric agonists and antagonists are reversible."},{"t":"Phenoxybenzamine","correct":true,"why":"Phenoxybenzamine is an irreversible, non-competitive, insurmountable antagonist that forms covalent bonds; 100% of the class picked it."},{"t":"None of the above","correct":false,"why":"One drug on the list, phenoxybenzamine, is irreversible."}],"teach":"Drugs listed as forming covalent (irreversible) bonds include aspirin, omeprazole and phenoxybenzamine. Once phenoxybenzamine is bound, the receptor has to be internalized and recycled and the body must make new ones.","quote":"And phenoxbenzamine is an irreversible covalent bond forming drug.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists (p. 6 of 18)","t":"Phenoxybenzamine is an irreversible α-adrenoceptor antagonist; its blockade is maintained even against very large amounts of catecholamine and cannot be reversed. Benzodiazepines such as diazepam act as allosteric modulators, and drugs acting at an allosteric site are often reversible."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~15, ~18; transcript 9/23; PollEV_s.pdf page 6; transcript 9/30"},
{id:'gtap-L02-013', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-bond","skill":"recall","concept":"weak-bonds-need-fit","tags":["in-class"],"source":"both","stem":"Why must a drug that relies on hydrogen and van der Waals bonds fit the receptor pocket closely?","options":[{"t":"Closer atoms allow more bonds to form","correct":true,"why":"The better the fit, the closer the atoms get and the more bonds can form, so the drug stays bound long enough to act."},{"t":"Weak bonds become covalent when close","correct":false,"why":"Hydrogen and van der Waals bonds stay reversible; they do not turn into covalent bonds."},{"t":"Close fit makes the binding irreversible","correct":false,"why":"Drugs forming these bonds are reversible and eventually come off."},{"t":"Fit determines how well it is absorbed","correct":false,"why":"Absorption is pharmacokinetics; fit in the pocket concerns receptor binding."}],"teach":"Hydrogen bonds (electrostatic attraction between H and atoms such as N, O and S) and van der Waals bonds (weak interactions when two atoms are brought close) are each weak, so the drug needs the right distance, size and charge in the pocket for enough bonds to form and keep it bound; a covalent binder only needs to bond enough to one component.","quote":"The better the fit of the drug into the receptor pocket, the closer atoms get and more bonds can form","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~16; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-014', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-bond","skill":"apply","concept":"stereoisomerism-potency","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Levalbuterol is more potent than racemic albuterol. Which drug property accounts for the difference?","options":[{"t":"Stereoisomer (chirality) composition","correct":true,"why":"Levalbuterol is the more potent isomer of the racemic mixture, which contains both isomers."},{"t":"Molecular size","correct":false,"why":"Isomers have the same exact components; they differ in orientation, not size."},{"t":"Covalent vs ionic bond type","correct":false,"why":"The example concerns isomer orientation, not a change in bond type."},{"t":"Number of receptors the cell expresses","correct":false,"why":"Receptor number is a property of the cell, not of the drug."}],"teach":"Drug size, drug shape and stereoisomerism (chirality) affect receptor binding. Isomers are mirror images with the same components; how they are oriented can make a drug more or less potent. Levalbuterol is the more potent isomer of racemic albuterol.","quote":"So, levo albuterol is the more potent isomer of the racemic mixture of albuterol, which has both confirmations of RNS, you know, isomers in it.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~17; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-015', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"sites","lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-site","skill":"tell","concept":"orthosteric-allosteric-poll","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following is correct?","options":[{"t":"Allosteric agonists compete for the same binding pocket on the receptor","correct":false,"why":"Allosteric agonists bind someplace else on the receptor; they do not compete for the orthosteric pocket."},{"t":"Allosteric agonists have the highest affinity for the orthosteric binding pocket","correct":false,"why":"Allosteric agonists do not bind at the orthosteric pocket at all."},{"t":"Phenoxybenzamine has higher affinity than prazosin","correct":true,"why":"Phenoxybenzamine forms covalent bonds and does not come off; prazosin forms weaker bonds and comes off."},{"t":"In orthosteric interactions, two drugs can be bound at the same time","correct":false,"why":"Orthosteric binding is one or the other; only one drug occupies the pocket at a time."}],"teach":"Orthosteric drugs compete for the same binding pocket, so only one can be bound at a time. Allosteric drugs bind a different site and change the orthosteric pocket. The irreversible (covalent) antagonist phenoxybenzamine has the highest affinity; reversible prazosin binds and comes off.","quote":"So which drug has the greatest affinity? The one that forms covalent bond all day long, which are, are irreversible drugs, right?","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists (p. 6 of 18)","t":"Allosteric modulators bind a site separate from the orthosteric site, so they do not compete for the orthosteric pocket. Phenoxybenzamine is an irreversible antagonist whose block agonists cannot surmount, whereas a competitive antagonist can be displaced by high agonist concentrations."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~18–~20, ~37–~38; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-016', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"sites","lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-site","skill":"apply","concept":"orthosteric-competition","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Prazosin is bound to an α1 receptor when norepinephrine arrives at that same receptor. What happens?","options":[{"t":"Norepinephrine cannot activate that receptor","correct":true,"why":"Both bind the same pocket, so while prazosin occupies it norepinephrine cannot bind and activate."},{"t":"Both drugs bind and the effect is additive","correct":false,"why":"Orthosteric binding is one or the other, never both at once."},{"t":"Norepinephrine binds an allosteric site instead","correct":false,"why":"Norepinephrine is an orthosteric agonist; it competes for the same pocket."},{"t":"Prazosin permanently inactivates the receptor","correct":false,"why":"Prazosin is reversible, competitive and surmountable; permanent binding describes phenoxybenzamine."}],"teach":"Norepinephrine (agonist) and prazosin (reversible α1-selective antagonist) bind the same orthosteric pocket and compete. Only one can occupy the pocket at a time. Because prazosin is reversible, it eventually comes off and can be outcompeted.","quote":"It's going to bind to the same binding pocket as norepinephrine is binding to, and there is going to be a competition. Between the two.","note":"The transcript line reads 'if the prazosin is bound to that receptor, norepinephrine can activate the receptor'; from the competition context and 'it can be either one or the other, it can't be both', this is a transcription error for 'can't'.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists (p. 5–6 of 18)","t":"A competitive antagonist and the agonist compete for binding to the receptors, so the response depends on both concentrations. Increasing concentrations of a competitive antagonist progressively inhibit the agonist response, and sufficiently high agonist concentrations can surmount a given concentration of antagonist."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~18; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-017', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-site","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"allosteric-effects","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"A drug binds a receptor at a site different from the orthosteric pocket. What can it do to an orthosteric drug at that receptor? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Increase its affinity","correct":true,"why":"An allosteric drug can change the pocket so the orthosteric drug binds better."},{"t":"Decrease its efficacy","correct":true,"why":"An allosteric antagonist can decrease the affinity and/or efficacy of another drug."},{"t":"Have no effect on it","correct":true,"why":"Depending on how the pocket changes, some allosteric drugs affect neither affinity nor efficacy."},{"t":"Displace it by competing for its pocket","correct":false,"why":"Competing for the pocket is orthosteric behavior; allosteric drugs bind elsewhere."},{"t":"Covalently lock the receptor inactive","correct":false,"why":"All allosteric agonists and antagonists are described as reversible; they bind and come off."}],"teach":"An allosteric (allotopic) drug binds at a different site and changes the orthosteric pocket. It can increase or decrease the affinity and/or efficacy of another drug, or have no effect, depending on what it does to the pocket. Allosteric agonists increase and allosteric antagonists decrease; both are reversible.","quote":"So some drugs may affect affinity, another drug may affect only the efficacy, another may affect the affinity in the efficacy, and others, they don't even know that it's there.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists (p. 6 of 18)","t":"A drug can act noncompetitively by binding to a site separate from the agonist binding site and modifying receptor activity without blocking agonist binding; such actions are often reversible. Negative allosteric modulators reduce receptor activity, and others increase it."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~20, ~39, ~42–~44; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-018', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"sites","lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-site","skill":"apply","concept":"diazepam-gaba-pam","tags":["exam-cue"],"source":"both","stem":"GABA (γ-aminobutyric acid) is bound to its GABA-A receptor, a chloride (Cl−) ion channel, when diazepam is added. What is the result?","options":[{"t":"GABA binds better; more Cl− enters","correct":true,"why":"Diazepam is a positive allosteric modulator: it increases the affinity and/or efficacy of GABA, so more chloride enters."},{"t":"GABA is displaced; less Cl− enters","correct":false,"why":"Diazepam does not compete for the GABA site; it binds someplace else."},{"t":"Cl− leaves the neuron","correct":false,"why":"Opening the GABA channel lets chloride move from outside to inside the cell."},{"t":"Na+ enters; the neuron is more excitable","correct":false,"why":"GABA is the major inhibitory pathway; the channel conducts chloride and shuts things down."}],"teach":"GABA binds its orthosteric sites and opens the GABA-A channel; chloride enters, the membrane becomes more negative, and the person becomes less anxious and more sleepy. Diazepam, an allosteric agonist (positive allosteric modulator, PAM), binds elsewhere and increases GABA's affinity and/or efficacy, so more chloride enters faster or longer.","quote":"Benzos increase the affinity And/or efficacy of GABA For its receptor","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists (p. 6 of 18)","t":"Benzodiazepines bind to an allosteric site on ion channels that are physiologically activated by GABA. They are positive allosteric modulators because they potentiate, rather than inhibit, the ability of the orthosteric agonist GABA to increase channel conductance."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~21–~24; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-019', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-signal","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"second-messenger-examples","tags":["exam-cue"],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following are second messengers? (cAMP = cyclic AMP; PKA = protein kinase A; IP3 = inositol phosphate 3; AC = adenylate cyclase; PLC = phospholipase C) Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"cAMP","correct":true,"why":"Cyclic AMP is the classic second messenger made by AC."},{"t":"PKA","correct":true,"why":"Protein kinase A is listed as a second messenger; anything below cAMP in the cascade counts."},{"t":"IP3","correct":true,"why":"IP3 is the second messenger made by PLC in the Gαq pathway."},{"t":"Ca++","correct":true,"why":"Calcium is listed as a second messenger (released by IP3 in the Gαq pathway)."},{"t":"AC","correct":false,"why":"Adenylate cyclase is the effector (signal amplifier) that makes cAMP."},{"t":"β1 receptor","correct":false,"why":"The β1 receptor is the receptor, not a second messenger."},{"t":"PLC","correct":false,"why":"Phospholipase C is the effector that makes IP3."}],"teach":"Second messengers are small molecules created, degraded or moved by an enzyme, ion channel or transport protein (the effector). They diffuse near their site of synthesis and convey information to many targets. Examples: cAMP, PKA, protein kinase C (PKC), IP3 and Ca++; the receptor, adenylate cyclase and a calcium channel are not second messengers.","quote":"If there is camp at PKA, then select all, right? But you shouldn't be picking the receptor or the AC or the calcium channel.","note":"Lecture and textbook differ. The slide lists PKA and PKC as second messengers. The textbook counts only the small non-protein molecules (cAMP, calcium, IP3, DAG) as second messengers and calls PKA the enzyme that cAMP switches on. Use the slide list on the exam.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Signaling Mechanisms & Drug Action; G Proteins & Second Messengers (p. 7, 10 of 18)","t":"Second messengers are nonprotein chemical mediators inside the cell; named examples are cAMP, calcium ion and the phosphoinositides. Adenylyl cyclase (AC) is the effector enzyme that converts ATP to cAMP, and phospholipase C (PLC) is the effector that produces IP3."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~29; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-020', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"gpcr","lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-signal","skill":"tell","concept":"transducer-identify","tags":["exam-cue"],"source":"both","stem":"In the pathway epinephrine → β1 receptor → Gαs → adenylate cyclase (AC) → cyclic AMP (cAMP), which component is the transducer?","options":[{"t":"Epinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Epinephrine is the signal: anything that binds and activates the receptor."},{"t":"β1 receptor","correct":false,"why":"The β1 receptor is the receptor that the signal binds."},{"t":"Gαs","correct":true,"why":"The G protein senses and translates the change in receptor conformation; that is the transducer."},{"t":"AC","correct":false,"why":"AC is the effector (signal amplifier)."},{"t":"cAMP","correct":false,"why":"cAMP is the second messenger."}],"teach":"Signal transduction runs signal (hormone, growth factor, agonist drug, neurotransmitter) → receptor → transducer (for example G proteins) → effector, the signal amplifier (adenylate cyclase, phospholipase C) → second messenger → physiological response. The transducer translates, integrates and coordinates the signal.","quote":"Our G protein, for example, can understand that change in the receptor. And by understanding and translating that change, it's going to be called the transducer.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Signaling Mechanisms & Drug Action; G Proteins & Second Messengers (p. 7, 10 of 18)","t":"The G protein is a GTP-binding signal transducer protein that the receptor stimulates and that in turn modulates production of a second messenger. The receptor detects the ligand, and the effector, adenylyl cyclase for cAMP, is the enzyme whose activity the G protein changes."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~26–~28; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-021', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-signal","skill":"apply","concept":"crosstalk-net-response","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Acetylcholine (ACh) activates muscarinic type 2 (M2) receptors coupled to Gαi, while norepinephrine (NE) activates β1 receptors coupled to Gαs, in the same heart cells at the same time. What determines the heart rate response?","options":[{"t":"The net of opposing effects on cyclic AMP","correct":true,"why":"Crosstalk between pathways in the same cell yields a net response: Gαs raises cAMP and heart rate, Gαi suppresses them."},{"t":"Only the receptor that was activated first","correct":false,"why":"The cell integrates all incoming information at once rather than following one receptor."},{"t":"Only the β1 (Gαs) signal","correct":false,"why":"The M2 (Gαi) signal also acts on adenylate cyclase and opposes it."},{"t":"Only the M2 (Gαi) signal","correct":false,"why":"The β1 (Gαs) signal also acts on adenylate cyclase and opposes it."},{"t":"The sum of two increases in cyclic AMP","correct":false,"why":"M2 is Gαi, which suppresses cAMP; the two pathways oppose each other."}],"teach":"Crosstalk is interaction between signal transduction pathways within the same cell that yields a net response. In the heart, β1 (sympathetic, Gαs) increases cAMP and heart rate, while M2 (parasympathetic, Gαi) suppresses cAMP and decreases heart rate; both converge on adenylate cyclase.","quote":"Interaction between signal transduction pathways within the same cell yields a net response.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Table 2–1; Interplay Among Signaling Mechanisms (p. 11, 14 of 18)","t":"β-Adrenergic amines act through Gs to raise cAMP and heart rate, while muscarinic receptors couple to Gi, which lowers cAMP and slows the heart. Pathways active in the same cell at the same time may oppose one another, and the response reflects their interplay."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~30; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-022', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-class","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"pd-general-rules","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which are general rules of pharmacodynamics? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Drug must reach the site of action","correct":true,"why":"General rule 1: drugs have to get to the site of action to produce effects."},{"t":"Drug must bind to and interact with a receptor","correct":true,"why":"General rule 2, true for about 99% of drugs (osmotic diuretics are the stated exception)."},{"t":"Drugs change an ongoing physiological system","correct":true,"why":"General rule 3: drugs do not create anything; they modify an ongoing system."},{"t":"Drugs create new physiological functions","correct":false,"why":"Rule 3 states drugs do not create anything."},{"t":"Drug must form a covalent bond with its receptor","correct":false,"why":"Most receptor interactions are reversible (ionic, hydrogen, van der Waals); covalent binding is not a rule."}],"teach":"Pharmacodynamics is the study of biochemical and physiological effects of drugs and their mechanisms of action. General rules: (1) the drug must reach the site of action, (2) the drug must bind to and interact with a receptor, (3) drugs do not create anything but change the ongoing physiological system. Rule 2 holds about 99% of the time; osmotic diuretics do not bind to anything.","quote":"1) Drug must reach the site of action 2) Drug must bind to and interact with a receptor 3) Drug do not create anything, but change the ongoing phys. system","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Introduction; Variation in Drug Responsiveness (p. 3–4, 16 of 18)","t":"Most drugs act by associating with a receptor, the cell component that interacts with the drug and initiates the events leading to the observed effect. Receptors normally mediate the actions of endogenous signals, so drugs acting on them modify regulation already in place."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~31; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-023', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"efficacy","lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-class","skill":"tell","concept":"efficacy-definition","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"Which best describes efficacy?","options":[{"t":"Ability of a drug to bind and stay bound to a receptor","correct":false,"why":"Binding and staying bound is affinity."},{"t":"Ability of a cell to increase its number of receptors","correct":false,"why":"Receptor number is the cell's business, not a drug property."},{"t":"Ability of a drug to change the receptor behavior","correct":true,"why":"Efficacy is the drug changing the receptor state from inactive to active or active to inactive."},{"t":"Ability of a drug to be absorbed into the body","correct":false,"why":"Absorption is pharmacokinetics, not pharmacodynamics."}],"teach":"Affinity describes the association between drug and receptor (molecular forces, bonds). Efficacy (intrinsic efficacy) describes the mechanism by which the drug changes the receptor. A drug with efficacy changes the receptor state; one with zero efficacy (a neutral antagonist) does not.","quote":"Efficacy is the ability of that drug to change the receptor behavior. It's activity, its state from active to inactive or inactive to active, right?","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Introduction; Receptor-Effector Coupling & Spare Receptors (p. 4–5 of 18)","t":"Agonists activate the receptor by binding it, whereas antagonists bind but do not activate; the conformational change an agonist produces in the receptor protein is the basis of activation. Affinity is a separate property that determines the concentration required to form drug–receptor complexes."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~31; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-024', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"partial","lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-class","skill":"tell","concept":"full-vs-partial-agonist","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"A full agonist and a partial agonist both have greater affinity for active receptors. What separates them?","options":[{"t":"Partial agonist has lower positive efficacy","correct":true,"why":"The full agonist activates receptors at 100%; the partial agonist has lower positive efficacy (1–99%)."},{"t":"Partial agonist cannot bind inactive receptors","correct":false,"why":"Both can bind to inactive receptors and activate them."},{"t":"Partial agonist has negative efficacy","correct":false,"why":"Negative efficacy defines an inverse agonist."},{"t":"Partial agonist has zero efficacy","correct":false,"why":"Zero efficacy defines a neutral antagonist."},{"t":"Partial agonist binds an allosteric site","correct":false,"why":"Full versus partial is about efficacy, not binding site."}],"teach":"Full and partial agonists both have greater affinity for active receptors and both can bind inactive receptors and activate them. The full agonist activates receptors at 100% (positive efficacy); the partial agonist has lower positive efficacy than the full agonist (1–99%). A partial agonist only activates to its own efficacy.","quote":"Has greater affinity for active receptors, but lower (+) Efficacy than the FA (1-99%).","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Partial Agonists (p. 6 of 18)","t":"Partial agonists produce a lower response at full receptor occupancy than full agonists do. This failure is not due to decreased affinity: even at concentrations that saturate all receptors, a partial agonist cannot cause a maximal response."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~32–~34; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-025', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"inverse","lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-class","skill":"apply","concept":"inverse-agonist-vs-antagonist","tags":[],"source":"transcript","stem":"A cell line has 50% of its receptors naturally active, producing cyclic AMP (cAMP). What happens to cAMP production after an inverse agonist compared with a competitive (neutral) antagonist?","options":[{"t":"Inverse agonist: to zero; antagonist: stays at 50%","correct":true,"why":"The inverse agonist shuts off active receptors; the neutral antagonist does not change receptor state."},{"t":"Both drop cAMP to zero","correct":false,"why":"A neutral antagonist has zero efficacy and leaves active receptors active."},{"t":"Inverse agonist: stays at 50%; antagonist: to zero","correct":false,"why":"This reverses the two; negative efficacy belongs to the inverse agonist."},{"t":"Both leave cAMP at 50%","correct":false,"why":"An inverse agonist has negative efficacy and shuts off active receptors."},{"t":"Inverse agonist: rises to 100%; antagonist: to zero","correct":false,"why":"Raising the signal is positive efficacy (agonist), not inverse agonism."}],"teach":"An inverse agonist has highest affinity for inactive receptors and negative efficacy, so it shuts off receptors that are already active. A neutral antagonist has equal affinity for active and inactive states and zero efficacy; it only prevents agonists from binding and keeps receptors wherever they are.","quote":"So if I give an inverse agonist, I'm going to bring the production of camp to zero. But if I give you a competitive antagonist, say it's at 50%, because it's not changing the activity of the receptors, it's just binding and staying bound, so they have affinity but zero efficacy.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists (p. 5 of 18)","t":"A neutral antagonist binds receptors without activating them and leaves the basal level of receptor activity unchanged. An inverse agonist also reduces receptor activity below the basal level observed in the absence of any agonist."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~35–~38; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-026', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-class","skill":"recall","concept":"neutral-antagonist-properties","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which describes a neutral (reversible) antagonist?","options":[{"t":"Equal affinity for active and inactive states; zero efficacy","correct":true,"why":"Neutral antagonists do not care about receptor state; it is all about affinity (ε = zero)."},{"t":"Highest affinity for inactive states; negative efficacy","correct":false,"why":"That describes an inverse agonist."},{"t":"Greater affinity for active states; positive efficacy","correct":false,"why":"That describes a full or partial agonist."},{"t":"Greater affinity for active states; efficacy of 1–99% of the maximum","correct":false,"why":"That describes a partial agonist."}],"teach":"Neutral antagonists have no efficacy (ε = zero) and equal affinity for receptors in the active and inactive state. They work by occupying receptors and preventing agonists from binding, keeping each receptor in whatever state it is in. Irreversible antagonists have the highest affinity.","quote":"Neutral ... No Efficacy (ε = zero); equal affinity for receptors in the active and inactive state ... All about Affinity","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists (p. 5 of 18)","t":"Antagonists bind to receptors but do not activate them; they act by reducing the effects of agonists. An antagonist has no functional effect in the absence of an agonist, which distinguishes it from an inverse agonist that reduces receptor activity below basal level."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~37–~38; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-027', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"inverse","lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-class","skill":"apply","concept":"inverse-agonist-loratadine","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Loratadine is taken during pollen season after histamine has already activated H1 receptors, and it shuts those receptors off. Which property allows this?","options":[{"t":"Negative efficacy","correct":true,"why":"An inverse agonist with negative efficacy inhibits a receptor that is already active."},{"t":"Zero efficacy","correct":false,"why":"Zero efficacy (neutral antagonist) keeps receptors in their current state; it would not shut off active ones."},{"t":"Partial positive efficacy","correct":false,"why":"Positive efficacy increases the signal."},{"t":"Irreversible covalent binding","correct":false,"why":"Loratadine is an inverse agonist; covalent binding is not the property named."},{"t":"Allosteric binding","correct":false,"why":"Inverse agonism is defined by negative efficacy, not by an allosteric site."}],"teach":"Inverse agonists have the highest affinity for inactive receptors and negative efficacy: if they bind an active receptor, they inhibit it. H1-selective antihistamines such as loratadine were the first class classified as inverse agonists; they were long classified as antagonists until cell lines with naturally active receptors showed they bring the signal down.","quote":"Thus, if it binds to an active receptor, an inverse agonist can inhibit the receptor ... E.g., Anti-Histamine (Loratadine)","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists (p. 5 of 18)","t":"An inverse agonist not only interferes with agonist activation but also reduces receptor activity below the basal level observed in the absence of any agonist. A traditional antagonist, by contrast, has no functional effect of its own when no agonist is present."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~35–~36; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-028', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-class","skill":"apply","concept":"indirect-antagonist-caffeine","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"In central nervous system neurons, norepinephrine drives Gαs signaling and the PDE enzyme breaks down cyclic AMP (cAMP). Caffeine blocks the PDE enzyme. How is caffeine classified, and what happens to cAMP?","options":[{"t":"Indirect antagonist; cAMP rises","correct":true,"why":"Caffeine acts on a signaling component, not the receptor; blocking PDE removes the brakes and cAMP goes up."},{"t":"Indirect antagonist; cAMP falls","correct":false,"why":"Blocking the enzyme that breaks down cAMP raises cAMP."},{"t":"Receptor antagonist; cAMP falls","correct":false,"why":"Caffeine does not bind the receptor; it acts downstream on PDE."},{"t":"Allosteric antagonist; cAMP falls","correct":false,"why":"Allosteric drugs bind the receptor at another site; caffeine binds PDE."},{"t":"Inverse agonist; cAMP falls","correct":false,"why":"Inverse agonists bind the receptor with negative efficacy; caffeine binds PDE."}],"teach":"Indirect antagonists bind to components upstream or downstream from the receptor and can be reversible or irreversible. Caffeine blocks PDE, raising cAMP and enhancing the physiological response even though it is an antagonist of PDE. A drug that blocks RAS in the tyrosine kinase pathway is also an indirect antagonist but suppresses the signal.","quote":"So, this is an indirect antagonist. Why is indirect? Because it's not affecting the function of the receptor, it's affecting the, affecting the component of the signaling cascade pathway.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Well-Established Second Messengers — cAMP; Receptor Classes & Drug Development (p. 13–14 of 18)","t":"cAMP is degraded by phosphodiesterases (PDEs), and inhibition of cAMP degradation is how caffeine produces its effects. Drugs acting on phosphodiesterase target an element of the signaling pathway distal to the receptor rather than the receptor itself."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~40–~41; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-029', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-kd","skill":"recall","concept":"kd-definition","tags":[],"source":"slide","stem":"What does the dissociation constant (Kd) define?","options":[{"t":"Concentration binding 50% of available binding sites","correct":true,"why":"Kd is the drug concentration that binds 50% of the available binding sites."},{"t":"Concentration that produces 50% of the maximum response","correct":false,"why":"That is the effective concentration 50 (EC50), a potency measure."},{"t":"Rate at which the drug associates with the receptor","correct":false,"why":"The rate of association is K1; Kd is K2/K1."},{"t":"Maximum number of receptors a drug can bind","correct":false,"why":"100% of the receptors bound is Bmax."},{"t":"Concentration that binds 100% of receptors","correct":false,"why":"100% binding corresponds to Bmax, not Kd."}],"teach":"Kd (coefficient of dissociation) is the drug concentration that binds 50% of available binding sites. From the law of mass action, Kd = [D][R]/[DR] = K2/K1, where K2 is the rate of dissociation and K1 the rate of association. A smaller Kd means greater affinity.","quote":"Define the drug concentration that binds 50% of available binding sites","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Concentration-Effect Curves & Receptor Binding of Agonists; Figure 2–1 (p. 4 of 18)","t":"Kd is the concentration of free drug at which half-maximal binding is observed, and Bmax is the total concentration of receptor sites. EC50 is a different quantity, the concentration that produces 50% of the maximal effect."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~45–~47, ~50"},
{id:'gtap-L02-030', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-kd","skill":"calc","concept":"kd-calculation","tags":[],"source":"slide","stem":"10 mol of drug (D) are added to 10 receptors (R). At equilibrium 1 drug–receptor complex (DR) has formed, leaving [D] = 9 and [R] = 9. Using Kd = [D][R]/[DR], what is Kd, and how does this drug compare with one that leaves [D] = 1, [R] = 1 and [DR] = 9?","options":[{"t":"81; lower affinity than the second drug","correct":true,"why":"(9 × 9)/1 = 81; the second drug gives (1 × 1)/9 = 0.11, and the smaller Kd has the greater affinity."},{"t":"81; higher affinity than the second drug","correct":false,"why":"Kd and affinity are inversely related; the larger Kd (81) means lower affinity."},{"t":"0.11; higher affinity than the second drug","correct":false,"why":"0.11 is the second drug's Kd, from (1 × 1)/9."},{"t":"9; lower affinity than the second drug","correct":false,"why":"Kd multiplies free [D] and free [R] (9 × 9) and divides by [DR] (1)."},{"t":"0.11; lower affinity than the second drug","correct":false,"why":"0.11 is the second drug's Kd, and a smaller Kd means greater affinity."}],"teach":"Kd = [D][R]/[DR]. With 10 drug and 10 receptors: if only 1 complex forms, Kd = (9)(9)/(1) = 81; if 9 complexes form, Kd = (1)(1)/(9) = 0.11. A lower Kd means higher affinity (inverse proportional).","quote":"[9][9] / [1] = 81 ... [1][1] / [9] = 0.11 ... ↓ Kd = ↑ Affinity","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Concentration-Effect Curves & Receptor Binding of Agonists; Receptor-Effector Coupling & Spare Receptors (p. 4–5 of 18)","t":"The relation between free drug and receptor-bound drug follows the law of mass action, and Kd characterizes affinity reciprocally: a low Kd means high binding affinity and a high Kd means low affinity."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~48–~49"},
{id:'gtap-L02-031', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"binding-kd","lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-kd","skill":"figure","concept":"kd-binding-curve","tags":[],"source":"slide","stem":"The binding curves of Drug A and Drug B at the same receptor are plotted as % receptors bound (0% to Bmax, 100%) against log[concentration]. Which is correct?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":-1,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":1,"emax":100}],"y":"% receptors bound","x":"log[concentration]","marks":[{"x":-1,"label":"Kd A"},{"x":1,"label":"Kd B"}],"caption":"Binding curves of two drugs at one receptor; Bmax = 100% of the receptors bound."},"options":[{"t":"Drug A has the smaller Kd and greater affinity","correct":true,"why":"Kd is read on the x-axis at 50% bound; the lower concentration is the smaller Kd, which means greater affinity."},{"t":"Drug B has the smaller Kd and the greater affinity","correct":false,"why":"Drug B needs more drug to bind 50%, so its Kd is larger."},{"t":"Drug A has the larger Kd and greater affinity","correct":false,"why":"A lower concentration at 50% bound is a smaller Kd, not larger."},{"t":"Drug B has greater efficacy than Drug A does","correct":false,"why":"A binding curve measures receptor occupancy (affinity), not efficacy."},{"t":"The two drugs have equal Kd values","correct":false,"why":"Different concentrations at 50% bound mean different Kd values."}],"teach":"On a binding curve, Bmax is 100% of the receptors bound and Kd is the concentration at 50% bound, read on the x-axis. The smaller the Kd, the greater the affinity of the drug for the receptor: it requires the least drug to bind the receptors.","quote":"The smaller the Kd is, the greater the affinity of a drug for the receptor will be. Kd will be reflected in the x-axis.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Concentration-Effect Curves & Receptor Binding of Agonists; Figure 2–1 (p. 4 of 18)","t":"On a binding curve, Bmax is the total concentration of receptor sites and Kd is the concentration at which binding is half-maximal. Kd characterizes affinity reciprocally, so the drug that reaches half-maximal binding at the lower concentration has the lower Kd and higher affinity."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~50"},
{id:'gtap-L02-032', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-kd","skill":"apply","concept":"kd-compare-receptors","tags":["poll"],"source":"slide","img":"gtap-pollev-kd-table","imgCap":"Dissociation constants (Kd) of two drugs at three receptors.","stem":"For which receptor does Drug A have the greatest affinity?","options":[{"t":"M1 (gut)","correct":true,"why":"250 nM is the smallest of Drug A's three Kd values, and the smallest Kd means the greatest affinity."},{"t":"β1 (heart)","correct":false,"miss":"read","why":"30 μM is larger than 250 nM, so Drug A's affinity at β1 is lower than at M1."},{"t":"β2 (vasculature)","correct":false,"miss":"affinity","why":"1 mM is Drug A's largest Kd, so β2 is where its affinity is lowest."},{"t":"Equal at all three","correct":false,"miss":"read","why":"The three Kd values differ, so affinity differs."}],"teach":"Compare affinity by comparing Kd values after putting them in the same units: nM is smaller than μM, which is smaller than mM. The smallest Kd is the highest affinity. Drug A: β1 30 μM, β2 1 mM, M1 250 nM. Drug B: β1 100 nM, β2 20 μM, M1 250 nM.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Clinical Selectivity: Beneficial Versus Toxic Effects of Drugs; Case Study Answer (p. 17–18 of 18)","t":"Selectivity is measured by comparing the binding affinities of a drug for different receptors. The receptor with the lowest Kd is the one the drug binds with the greatest affinity."}],"cite":"PollEV_s.pdf page 1 (Kd table); Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~51"},
{id:'gtap-L02-033', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"potency","lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-drc","skill":"tell","concept":"potency-determinants","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"G-proteins are defined and regulated by the beta subunit","correct":false,"why":"G proteins are defined by the α subunit."},{"t":"Phospholipase C (PLC) is an example of a second messenger","correct":false,"why":"Phospholipase C is the effector system that amplifies the signal."},{"t":"Efficacy is defined by the ability of a drug to bind and stay bound to a receptor","correct":false,"why":"Binding and staying bound is affinity."},{"t":"The higher the Kd, the greater the affinity of a drug for the receptor","correct":false,"why":"The smaller the dissociation constant (Kd), the greater the affinity."},{"t":"The potency of a drug is dependent on the affinity, efficacy and # of receptors","correct":true,"why":"Potency depends on the drug's affinity, its efficacy and the tissue (site of action)."}],"teach":"Potency depends on affinity (Kd), efficacy and the tissue, meaning its number of receptors: more receptors in the tissue and the drug behaves more potent ('when we increase the number of receptors, we increase the potency of a drug'). Norepinephrine at β1 in the heart produces tachycardia.","note":"Three versions of this list. The PollEV key reads: the potency of a drug is dependent on the affinity, efficacy and # of receptors. The slide says affinity, efficacy, tissue (site of action); in class he said affinity, efficacy, the receptor. The poll wording is what he reuses on the exam.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Relation Between Drug Dose & Clinical Response — Potency; Maximal efficacy (p. 15 of 18)","t":"Potency is the concentration (EC50) or dose (ED50) that produces 50% of maximal effect. It depends partly on receptor affinity for the drug (Kd) and partly on how efficiently the drug–receptor interaction is coupled to response in the responding tissue."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~52–~54; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-034', prof:'Gottlieb', "dupOf":"gtap-L02-042","fg":"potency","lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-drc","skill":"figure","concept":"ec50-potency-shift","tags":[],"source":"slide","stem":"Drug A and Drug B are plotted as log[concentration]–response curves. EC50 is the effective concentration that produces 50% of the maximum response. Which drug is more potent?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":-1,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":1,"emax":100}],"x":"log[concentration]","marks":[{"x":-1,"label":"EC50 A"},{"x":1,"label":"EC50 B"}],"caption":"Two drugs with the same Emax; EC50 is read on the x-axis at 50% of the maximum response."},"options":[{"t":"Drug A","correct":true,"why":"Drug A reaches 50% of the maximum at a lower concentration, a lower EC50; lower EC50 means higher potency."},{"t":"Drug B","correct":false,"why":"A right-shifted curve needs more drug for 50% of the maximum, a higher EC50 and lower potency."},{"t":"Equal potency","correct":false,"why":"Equal maximum does not mean equal EC50; the shift changes EC50."},{"t":"Cannot be compared","correct":false,"why":"EC50 values on the same axis compare potency directly."}],"teach":"EC50 is the effective concentration that produces 50% of the maximum response (Emax). Potency = EC50 (or ED50), and higher potency means a lower EC50 or ED50.","quote":"Effective Concentration = EC50 : Effective concentration that produces 50% of the max response. ↑ Potency = ↓ EC50 or ED50","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Relation Between Drug Dose & Clinical Response — Potency; Figure 2–15 (p. 15 of 18)","t":"Potency is judged from the relative positions of dose–response curves along the dose axis. Because potency is the concentration (EC50) that produces 50% of maximal effect, the drug whose curve lies farther left has the lower EC50 and the greater potency."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~57–~58"},
{id:'gtap-L02-035', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-drc","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"drc-purpose","tags":[],"source":"slide","stem":"Pharmacodynamics defines which of the following? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Define concentration and dose responses of a drug","correct":true,"why":"Listed: the curve defines concentration and dose responses of a drug."},{"t":"Predict and compare drugs","correct":true,"why":"Listed: it is a model of prediction or comparison."},{"t":"Determine the potency","correct":true,"why":"Listed: it determines potency (ED50)."},{"t":"Measure how well a drug is absorbed","correct":false,"why":"Absorption is pharmacokinetics; the curve measures response to concentration."},{"t":"Determine how many receptors a cell synthesizes daily","correct":false,"why":"Receptor synthesis is a property of the cell, not something the curve defines."}],"teach":"Pharmacodynamics defines concentration and dose responses of a drug, predicts and compares drugs, and determines potency (Potency = ED50). Dose–response curves serve as models of prediction or comparison.","quote":"Defines: Concentration and dose responses of a drug; Predict and compare drugs; Determine the Potency; Potency = ED50","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Relation Between Drug Dose & Clinical Response; Quantal Dose-Effect Curves (p. 14–16 of 18)","t":"Graded dose–response curves give the relative potency and maximal efficacy of drugs, which the prescriber needs to choose among drugs and determine doses. Comparing the ED50 values of two drugs states how many times more potent one is than the other."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~56"},
{id:'gtap-L02-036', prof:'Gottlieb', "dupOf":"gtap-L02-038","lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-kd","skill":"figure","concept":"kd-binding-curve","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"Binding curves A–E are five drugs binding the α1 receptor, plotted as % receptors bound against log[concentration]. Which curve is most likely phenoxybenzamine?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":1,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":-2,"emax":100},{"label":"C","ec":0,"emax":100},{"label":"D","ec":-1,"emax":100},{"label":"E","ec":2,"emax":100}],"y":"% receptors bound","x":"log[concentration]","caption":"Five binding curves at one receptor; every curve reaches Bmax (100% of the receptors bound). Kd is the concentration at 50% bound."},"options":[{"t":"A","correct":false,"why":"A needs more drug than B, C and D to bind 50% of the receptors, so its Kd is larger and its affinity lower."},{"t":"B","correct":true,"why":"B binds 50% of the receptors at the lowest concentration: the smallest Kd and the greatest affinity, which belongs to the covalent, irreversible drug phenoxybenzamine."},{"t":"C","correct":false,"why":"C sits in the middle; two curves reach 50% bound at lower concentrations, so C does not have the greatest affinity."},{"t":"D","correct":false,"why":"D has the second-smallest Kd; phenoxybenzamine, bound by covalent bonds that never come off, has the highest affinity of all."},{"t":"E","correct":false,"why":"E is the furthest right: the largest Kd and the lowest affinity, the opposite of an irreversible drug."}],"teach":"Kd is read on the x-axis at 50% of the receptors bound, and the smaller the Kd, the greater the affinity. Phenoxybenzamine forms covalent bonds and does not come off, so it has the greatest affinity of any drug on the list and needs the least drug to bind half the receptors: the curve furthest left.","quote":"So which drug has the greatest affinity? The one that forms covalent bond all day long, which are, are irreversible drugs, right?","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Concentration-Effect Curves & Receptor Binding of Agonists; Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists (p. 4, 6 of 18)","t":"Kd is the concentration at which half-maximal binding is observed; if the Kd is low, binding affinity is high. Phenoxybenzamine is an irreversible antagonist whose block is maintained even against very large amounts of catecholamine."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~37, ~49–~50; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-037', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-kd","skill":"figure","concept":"kd-binding-curve","tags":["poll"],"source":"slide","stem":"Binding curves A–E are five drugs binding the β1 receptor, plotted as % receptors bound against log[concentration]. Which of these drugs has the lowest affinity for the receptor?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":-1,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":0,"emax":100},{"label":"C","ec":2,"emax":100},{"label":"D","ec":-2,"emax":100},{"label":"E","ec":1,"emax":100}],"y":"% receptors bound","x":"log[concentration]","caption":"Five binding curves at one receptor; every curve reaches Bmax. Kd is the concentration at 50% bound."},"options":[{"t":"A","correct":false,"why":"A reaches 50% bound at the second-lowest concentration; its Kd is small and its affinity high."},{"t":"B","correct":false,"why":"B is in the middle of the group; two drugs need more concentration than B to bind half the receptors."},{"t":"C","correct":true,"why":"C needs the highest concentration to bind 50% of the receptors: the largest Kd, and Kd and affinity are inversely related."},{"t":"D","correct":false,"why":"D is the furthest left, the smallest Kd and therefore the greatest affinity, the opposite of what is asked."},{"t":"E","correct":false,"why":"E needs less drug than C to reach 50% bound, so its Kd is smaller and its affinity higher than C's."}],"teach":"Affinity is inversely proportional to Kd: the smaller the Kd, the greater the affinity, and Kd is reflected on the x-axis at 50% of the receptors bound. The curve furthest right needs the most drug to occupy half the receptors, so it has the largest Kd and the lowest affinity.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Concentration-Effect Curves & Receptor Binding of Agonists; Figure 2–1 (p. 4 of 18)","t":"Kd characterizes the receptor’s affinity for binding the drug in a reciprocal fashion: if the Kd is low, binding affinity is high, and vice versa."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~49–~50"},
{id:'gtap-L02-038', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-kd","skill":"figure","concept":"phenoxybenzamine-vs-prazosin-affinity","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"Curve C is the binding curve of prazosin at the α1 receptor (% receptors bound against log[concentration]). Which curve best represents phenoxybenzamine binding the same receptor?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":1,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":-1.5,"emax":100},{"label":"C","ec":0,"emax":100,"dashed":true},{"label":"D","ec":2,"emax":100}],"y":"% receptors bound","x":"log[concentration]","marks":[{"x":0,"label":"Kd C"}],"caption":"Dashed curve C is prazosin, the point of reference; the solid curves are three other drugs at the same receptor."},"options":[{"t":"A","correct":false,"why":"A needs more drug than prazosin to bind half the receptors: a larger Kd and a lower affinity than prazosin."},{"t":"B","correct":true,"why":"Phenoxybenzamine forms covalent bonds and does not come off, so it has higher affinity than prazosin: a smaller Kd, a curve to the left of C."},{"t":"C","correct":false,"why":"C is prazosin itself; a drug with higher affinity has a different (smaller) Kd."},{"t":"D","correct":false,"why":"D has the largest Kd of all, the lowest affinity; the irreversible drug has the highest."},{"t":"None of the above","correct":false,"why":"One curve (B) does show a smaller Kd than prazosin's, which is what higher affinity looks like."}],"teach":"Phenoxybenzamine is irreversible: its covalent bonds bind and do not come off, so it has higher affinity than prazosin, which forms weaker bonds, binds and comes off. On a binding curve, higher affinity is a smaller Kd, and Kd sits on the x-axis at 50% bound, so phenoxybenzamine's curve lies to the left of prazosin's.","quote":"it's an irreversible, which means they form what covalent bonds, which means they bind and they don't come off asprazocene is reversible form all the weaker bonds they're gonna bind, they're gonna come off. So which of these can have the highest affinity? Pbenzamine, right?","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Concentration-Effect Curves & Receptor Binding of Agonists; Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists (p. 4–6 of 18)","t":"If the Kd is low, binding affinity is high. A competitive antagonist such as prazosin can be surmounted by agonist, whereas the irreversible antagonist phenoxybenzamine forms a covalent bond and its blockade cannot be reversed."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~18, ~37, ~49–~50; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-039', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-class","skill":"figure","concept":"full-vs-partial-agonist","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"Dose–response curves A–D are four different drugs acting on the same receptor. Which of these drugs is a partial agonist?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":-1,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":0.5,"emax":100},{"label":"C","ec":0,"emax":45},{"label":"D","ec":1.5,"emax":100}],"caption":"Four drugs on one receptor; the y-axis is % of the maximal response of the system."},"options":[{"t":"A","correct":false,"why":"A reaches 100% of the maximal response, so it activates receptors at 100%: a full agonist, and the most potent one here."},{"t":"B","correct":false,"why":"B also reaches 100%; being further right only makes it less potent, not partial."},{"t":"C","correct":true,"why":"C plateaus at about 45% no matter how much dose is added: positive efficacy lower than the full agonist (1–99%), which defines a partial agonist."},{"t":"D","correct":false,"why":"D is the least potent curve, but it still reaches 100%, so its efficacy is that of a full agonist."},{"t":"None of the above","correct":false,"why":"One curve (C) never reaches the full response, and that is the partial agonist."}],"teach":"A full agonist activates receptors at 100% of the response; a partial agonist has positive efficacy but lower than the full agonist (1–99%), and it only activates to its own efficacy, like a lifter who can only bench 100 pounds no matter what is loaded. Left or right position is potency, not efficacy.","quote":"the partial is producing a different signal that only produces, say, 25% of the response or 75 or 39 or 68. It doesn't matter. But they have different efficacies","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Partial Agonists (p. 6 of 18)","t":"Partial agonists produce a lower response at full receptor occupancy than full agonists do. Even at concentrations that saturate all receptors, a partial agonist cannot cause a maximal response."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~32–~34; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-040', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-class","skill":"figure","concept":"inverse-agonist-vs-antagonist","tags":["poll"],"source":"transcript","stem":"A cell line has 50% of its receptors naturally active and producing cyclic AMP (cAMP); the dashed line is that baseline. Curves A–C show the cAMP signal as increasing doses of three different drugs are added. Which drug is most likely drug B?","graph":{"base":50,"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":0,"emax":50},{"label":"B","ec":0,"emax":2},{"label":"C","ec":0,"emax":100}],"y":"cAMP signal (% of max)","caption":"Receptors that are 50% active with no drug present; each curve is one drug added at increasing doses."},"options":[{"t":"Loratadine","correct":true,"why":"An inverse agonist has negative efficacy: it binds active receptors and shuts them off, bringing cAMP production from 50% down to zero."},{"t":"Prazosin","correct":false,"why":"A neutral antagonist has zero efficacy; it keeps receptors wherever they are, so the signal stays flat at 50% (curve A)."},{"t":"Norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"A full agonist activates inactive receptors and raises the signal toward 100% (curve C)."},{"t":"Diazepam","correct":false,"why":"Diazepam is an allosteric agonist that helps GABA increase its signal; it does not shut active receptors off."},{"t":"Phenoxybenzamine","correct":false,"why":"An antagonist has no efficacy and does not change the state of the receptors; the signal it leaves is the flat line, not a drop to zero."}],"teach":"In a dish where 50% of the receptors are naturally active, an inverse agonist (negative efficacy) shuts off active receptors and brings cAMP to zero; a neutral antagonist has affinity but zero efficacy, so cAMP stays at 50%; an agonist has positive efficacy and raises the signal. Loratadine is the inverse agonist on the drug list.","quote":"So if I give an inverse agonist, I'm going to bring the production of camp to zero. Because it's going to shut it off. But if I give you a competitive antagonist, say it's at 50%, because it's not changing the activity of the receptors","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists (p. 5 of 18)","t":"An inverse agonist reduces receptor activity below the basal level observed in the absence of any agonist. A neutral antagonist binds receptors without activating them and leaves the basal level of receptor activity unchanged."}],"cite":"Day 2 (9/23) petri-dish example (Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~35–~38); transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-041', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-class","skill":"figure","concept":"neutral-antagonist-properties","tags":["poll"],"source":"transcript","stem":"A cell line has 50% of its receptors naturally active and producing cyclic AMP (cAMP); the dashed line is that baseline. Curves A–C show the cAMP signal as increasing doses of three different drugs are added. Which curve best represents prazosin?","graph":{"base":50,"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":0,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":0,"emax":50},{"label":"C","ec":0,"emax":2}],"y":"cAMP signal (% of max)","caption":"Receptors that are 50% active with no drug present; each curve is one drug added at increasing doses."},"options":[{"t":"A","correct":false,"why":"A raises the signal: positive efficacy, an agonist such as norepinephrine, not a neutral antagonist."},{"t":"B","correct":true,"why":"Prazosin is a neutral antagonist with affinity but zero efficacy: it binds and stays bound without changing receptor state, so cAMP stays at 50%."},{"t":"C","correct":false,"why":"C brings the signal to zero: negative efficacy, which is an inverse agonist such as loratadine."},{"t":"None of the above","correct":false,"why":"One curve (B) is the flat line a neutral antagonist leaves at baseline."}],"teach":"Antagonists are neutral: equal affinity for active and inactive receptors and zero efficacy. They work by occupying receptors and preventing agonists from binding, keeping each receptor in whatever state it already has. With 50% of receptors active, cAMP stays at 50%; only an inverse agonist brings it to zero.","quote":"if I give you a competitive antagonist, say it's at 50%, because it's not changing the activity of the receptors, it's just binding and staying bound, so they have affinity but zero efficacy.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists (p. 5 of 18)","t":"A neutral antagonist binds receptors without activating them and leaves the basal level of receptor activity unchanged, which distinguishes it from an inverse agonist that reduces activity below basal."}],"cite":"Day 2 (9/23) petri-dish example (Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~37–~38); transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-042', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-drc","skill":"figure","concept":"ec50-potency-shift","tags":["poll"],"source":"slide","stem":"Dose–response curves A–C are three drugs acting on the same receptor and reaching the same maximum response. Which of these drugs is most potent?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":1,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":-1,"emax":100},{"label":"C","ec":0,"emax":100}],"x":"log[concentration]","caption":"Three drugs with the same Emax. EC50 is the concentration that produces 50% of the maximum response."},"options":[{"t":"A","correct":false,"why":"A needs the highest concentration to reach 50% of the maximum: the largest EC50 and the lowest potency."},{"t":"B","correct":true,"why":"B reaches 50% of the maximum response at the lowest concentration: the smallest EC50, and higher potency means a lower EC50."},{"t":"C","correct":false,"why":"C's EC50 is between A's and B's; one drug (B) still needs less concentration for the same response."},{"t":"They are equally potent","correct":false,"why":"Equal Emax does not mean equal potency; the curves sit at different EC50 values on the x-axis."}],"teach":"Potency = EC50 (or ED50): the effective concentration that produces 50% of the maximum response. Higher potency means a lower EC50, so among curves with the same Emax the one furthest left is the most potent. Potency is read on the x-axis; efficacy (Emax) on the y-axis.","quote":"Effective Concentration = EC50 : Effective concentration that produces 50% of the max response. ↑ Potency = ↓ EC50 or ED50","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Relation Between Drug Dose & Clinical Response — Potency; Figure 2–15 (p. 15 of 18)","t":"Potency refers to the concentration (EC50) or dose (ED50) of a drug required to produce 50% of that drug’s maximal effect, and is judged from the relative positions of dose–response curves along the dose axis."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~57–~59"},
{id:'gtap-L02-043', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-bond","skill":"figure","concept":"stereoisomerism-potency","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"Dose–response curve B is racemic albuterol at the β2 receptor. Which curve best represents levalbuterol at the same receptor?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":1,"emax":60},{"label":"B","ec":0,"emax":60,"dashed":true},{"label":"C","ec":-1,"emax":60},{"label":"D","ec":0,"emax":100}],"x":"log[concentration]","marks":[{"x":0,"label":"EC50 B"}],"caption":"Dashed curve B is racemic albuterol, the point of reference; the solid curves are three candidates."},"options":[{"t":"A","correct":false,"why":"A needs a higher concentration than B for the same response: a larger EC50, less potent, the opposite of the more potent isomer."},{"t":"B","correct":false,"why":"B is the racemic mixture itself; the more potent isomer has a different (lower) EC50."},{"t":"C","correct":true,"why":"Levalbuterol is the more potent isomer of the racemic mixture, and higher potency means a lower EC50: the same response at a lower concentration, a curve to the left of B."},{"t":"D","correct":false,"why":"D reaches a higher maximal response: a change in efficacy, not the potency difference that separates the isomers."},{"t":"None of the above","correct":false,"why":"One curve (C) shows the lower EC50 of a more potent drug."}],"teach":"Isomers are mirror images with the same components; how they are oriented can make a drug more or less potent. Levalbuterol is the more potent isomer of racemic albuterol, and higher potency means a lower EC50, so its curve reaches the same response at a lower concentration: to the left of the racemic mixture.","quote":"So, levo albuterol is the more potent isomer of the racemic mixture of albuterol, which has both confirmations of RNS, you know, isomers in it.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Relation Between Drug Dose & Clinical Response — Potency (p. 15 of 18)","t":"Potency refers to the concentration (EC50) required to produce 50% of a drug’s maximal effect; drugs are more potent because of the relative positions of their dose–response curves along the dose axis."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~17, ~58; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-044', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-gprot","skill":"recall","concept":"g-alpha-effector-messenger","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"Which statement is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"G proteins are defined and regulated by the α subunit","correct":false,"why":"True, but not the only true statement."},{"t":"Gαs activates adenylate cyclase and increases cyclic AMP","correct":false,"why":"True, but not the only true statement."},{"t":"Gαq activates phospholipase C and increases IP3 and Ca++","correct":false,"why":"True, but not the only true statement."},{"t":"Gαi acts on adenylate cyclase and suppresses cyclic AMP","correct":false,"why":"True, but not the only true statement."},{"t":"All of the above","correct":true,"why":"Every statement is correct; when two or more are definitely right, all of the above is the answer."}],"teach":"G proteins are defined by the α subunit, and there are three types to know: Gαs (stimulation) increases cAMP through adenylate cyclase, Gαi (inhibition) also acts on adenylate cyclase but suppresses cAMP, and Gαq increases IP3 and Ca++ through phospholipase C.","quote":"G proteins are defined and regulated by the beta subunit, no, by who? The alpha, right?","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"G Proteins & Second Messengers; Table 2–1 (p. 10–11 of 18)","t":"Gs stimulates adenylyl cyclase, which converts ATP to cAMP, while Gi proteins decrease adenylyl cyclase and lower cAMP. Gq stimulates phospholipase C, which raises IP3, diacylglycerol and cytoplasmic calcium."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~3; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-045', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-site","skill":"tell","concept":"orthosteric-allosteric-poll","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"Which statement is INCORRECT?","options":[{"t":"Covalent bonds are irreversible at body temperature","correct":false,"why":"Correct: once a covalent bond forms, the drug is bound unless something else breaks the bond."},{"t":"Prazosin and norepinephrine compete for the same binding pocket","correct":false,"why":"Correct: both are orthosteric at the α1 receptor, and only one can occupy the pocket at a time."},{"t":"Allosteric drugs compete for the orthosteric binding pocket","correct":true,"why":"Incorrect: allosteric drugs do not bind or compete for the orthosteric pocket; they bind someplace else on the receptor and change the pocket."},{"t":"Van der Waals bonds are weak interactions between atoms brought close together","correct":false,"why":"Correct: van der Waals bonds are weak, so the fit in the pocket has to be close for enough of them to form."},{"t":"All of the above","correct":false,"why":"Only one statement is wrong; the other three are correct."}],"teach":"Orthosteric drugs share one binding pocket, so it is one or the other, never both. Allosteric (allotopic) drugs bind a different site and change the orthosteric pocket, affecting the affinity and/or efficacy of the drug in it. Covalent bonds are irreversible at body temperature; van der Waals bonds are weak.","quote":"These drugs do not bind or compete for the orthostatic binding pocket. They bind someplace else on the receptor.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists (p. 6 of 18)","t":"Allosteric modulators bind a site separate from the orthosteric site, so they do not compete for the orthosteric pocket. A noncompetitive antagonist often binds irreversibly by forming a covalent bond."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~15–~16, ~18–~20; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-046', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-class","skill":"tell","concept":"efficacy-definition","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"Efficacy is the ability of a drug to bind and stay bound to a receptor. True or false?","options":[{"t":"True","correct":false,"why":"The ability to bind and stay bound is affinity, the poll distractor he warned about."},{"t":"False","correct":true,"why":"Efficacy is the ability of a drug to change the receptor behavior, its state from inactive to active or active to inactive; binding and staying bound is affinity."}],"teach":"Two basic concepts: affinity describes the association of the drug with the receptor (how likely it is to bind and stay bound, set by the bonds it forms); efficacy describes the mechanism by which the drug changes the receptor, its state from active to inactive or inactive to active.","quote":"The ability to bind and stay bound, that's affinity. Efficacy is the ability of that drug to change the receptor behavior.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Introduction; Receptor-Effector Coupling & Spare Receptors (p. 4–5 of 18)","t":"Agonists activate the receptor by binding it, whereas antagonists bind but do not activate. Affinity is a separate property that determines the concentration required to form drug–receptor complexes."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slide ~31; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-047', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-site","skill":"tell","concept":"orthosteric-competition","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"In orthosteric interactions, two drugs can be bound to the same receptor pocket at the same time. True or false?","options":[{"t":"True","correct":false,"why":"Orthosteric drugs compete for the same pocket; only one body can occupy the same space at the same time."},{"t":"False","correct":true,"why":"Orthosteric binding is one or the other, never both, like the single seat in a Formula One car; binding both at once is the allosteric case."}],"teach":"Orthosteric drugs (norepinephrine, prazosin, phenoxybenzamine at α1) all use the same binding pocket, so it is one or the other and never both. Allosteric drugs bind someplace else, so an allosteric and an orthosteric drug can be bound at the same time, one helping or hindering the other.","quote":"According to the laws of physics, only one body can occupy the same space and time, right? ... So for orthostatic, it can either be one or the other, it can't be both.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists (p. 5–6 of 18)","t":"A competitive antagonist and the agonist compete for binding to the receptors. Allosteric modulators instead bind a site separate from the orthosteric site."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~18–~20; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-048', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-class","skill":"tell","concept":"neutral-antagonist-properties","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"Which statement is INCORRECT?","options":[{"t":"The smaller the Kd, the greater the affinity of the drug for the receptor","correct":false,"why":"Correct: Kd and affinity are inversely proportional."},{"t":"Potency depends on affinity, efficacy and the tissue (site of action)","correct":false,"why":"Correct: those are the three determinants of potency."},{"t":"EC50 is the concentration that produces 50% of the maximum response","correct":false,"why":"Correct: that is the definition of the effective concentration 50."},{"t":"A neutral antagonist has negative efficacy","correct":true,"why":"Incorrect: a neutral antagonist has no efficacy (ε = zero) and equal affinity for active and inactive receptors; negative efficacy belongs to an inverse agonist."},{"t":"All of the above","correct":false,"why":"Only one statement is wrong; the other three are correct."}],"teach":"Neutral antagonists have affinity but zero efficacy: they occupy receptors and keep them in whatever state they are in. Negative efficacy (shutting off active receptors) is the inverse agonist. Kd is inversely related to affinity; EC50 is the concentration giving 50% of the maximum response; potency depends on affinity, efficacy and tissue.","quote":"they have affinity but zero efficacy. They're neutral You have a switch stuck. You can't change it what it is.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists; Relation Between Drug Dose & Clinical Response (p. 5, 15 of 18)","t":"A neutral antagonist binds receptors without activating them and leaves basal receptor activity unchanged, whereas an inverse agonist reduces activity below basal. Potency refers to the concentration (EC50) that produces 50% of a drug’s maximal effect."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~35–~38, ~49, ~52–~54, ~57; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-049', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-gprot","skill":"recall","concept":"gtp-exchange-dissociation","tags":["poll"],"source":"transcript","stem":"Put the G protein pathway (agonist at a Gαs-coupled receptor) into its sequence of steps. Which order is correct?","options":[{"t":"Agonist binds → receptor activates → GDP off, GTP on → α separates → AC activated → cAMP made","correct":true,"why":"Binding is always the first step; the activated receptor triggers the GDP/GTP exchange, α separates and activates the effector (AC), which makes the second messenger."},{"t":"GDP off, GTP on → agonist binds → receptor activates → α separates → AC activated → cAMP made","correct":false,"why":"The nucleotide exchange cannot come first; the agonist binding and activating the receptor is what triggers the α subunit to swap GDP for GTP."},{"t":"Agonist binds → receptor activates → α separates → GDP off, GTP on → AC activated → cAMP made","correct":false,"why":"GDP has to come off and GTP come in before the α subunit separates; the exchange is the trigger for separation."},{"t":"Agonist binds → receptor activates → GDP off, GTP on → cAMP made → AC activated → α separates","correct":false,"why":"cAMP is made by AC, so AC must be activated first, and α must separate before it can activate AC."}],"teach":"Sequence: the agonist binds the receptor, the receptor becomes activated, that triggers the α subunit (s, i or q) to let GDP come off and GTP come in, α dissociates and activates an effector such as AC or PLC, and the effector makes the second messenger (cAMP). Reversal removes the extra phosphate (GTP to GDP) and the drug pops off.","quote":"first step is you have to bind to the receptor ... receptor is going to become activated ... that's the trigger for the alpha S I or Q to change its behavior, which allows the GDP to come off and the GDP [GTP] comes in. So alpha S going to dissociate ... It's going to activate in a factor system ... such as the AC or the PLC.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"G Proteins & Second Messengers; Figure 2–10 and Figure 2–11 (p. 10–11 of 18)","t":"Agonist binding reduces the nucleotide affinity of the G protein, so GDP dissociates and GTP replaces it, switching the G protein to its active form, which then regulates the effector enzyme that produces the second messenger."}],"cite":"Day 1 material reviewed in class (no Day 2 slide); transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L02-050', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L02","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L02","sub":"gtap-class","skill":"figure","concept":"efficacy-definition","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"Dose–response curves A–D are four different drugs acting on the same receptor. Which of these drugs has the highest efficacy?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":-1,"emax":60},{"label":"B","ec":0,"emax":100},{"label":"C","ec":1,"emax":30},{"label":"D","ec":0.5,"emax":80}],"caption":"Four drugs on one receptor; the y-axis is % of the maximal response of the system."},"options":[{"t":"A","correct":false,"why":"A is the most potent (furthest left) but plateaus at 60%: a partial agonist with lower efficacy than B."},{"t":"B","correct":true,"why":"B produces the greatest response, 100% of the maximum: the full agonist, and efficacy is the size of the signal the drug produces."},{"t":"C","correct":false,"why":"C produces the smallest response (about 30%), the lowest efficacy of the four."},{"t":"D","correct":false,"why":"D reaches 80%, positive efficacy but still lower than the full agonist's 100%."}],"teach":"Efficacy is the property of the drug that lets it produce the signal seen as the physiological response: a full agonist produces 100% of the response, a partial agonist a smaller one (25, 39, 68%...). The plateau of each curve shows its efficacy; how far left it sits shows potency, a different property.","quote":"my partial agony [full agonist] is creating a signal that is producing 100% of the response as the partial is producing a different signal that only produces, say, 25% of the response or 75 or 39 or 68.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Relation Between Drug Dose & Clinical Response — Maximal efficacy (p. 15 of 18)","t":"The maximal efficacy of a drug is the greatest response it can produce; a partial agonist produces a lower maximal response than a full agonist even at full receptor occupancy."}],"cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_2_2026s copy.pdf slides ~32–~34, ~58; transcript 9/23"},
{id:'gtap-L04-001', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"two-state","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-recap","skill":"tell","concept":"full-vs-partial-agonist-summary","tags":[],"source":"transcript","stem":"In the two-state model, which property do a full agonist and a partial agonist share?","options":[{"t":"Highest affinity for active-state receptors","correct":true,"why":"Both bind active receptors best and keep them active; both also have positive efficacy and are reversible."},{"t":"The ability to reach 100% of the system’s response","correct":false,"why":"Only the full agonist reaches 100%; the partial agonist plateaus below that."},{"t":"Negative efficacy","correct":false,"why":"Both have positive efficacy; negative efficacy belongs to the inverse agonist."},{"t":"Equal affinity for active and inactive receptors","correct":false,"why":"Equal affinity for both states describes the neutral competitive antagonist."}],"teach":"Full and partial agonists are alike in the two-state model: both have the highest affinity for receptors in the active state, both can bind an inactive receptor and turn it on (positive efficacy), and both are reversible. They differ only in how much efficacy they carry: the full agonist reaches 100%, the partial agonist plateaus between 1% and 99%.","quote":"So when we think about full and partial agonists, the first thing that should come to mind is they love receptors that are active, and if they bind to, they’re gonna have the highest affinity for those receptors. However, if they bind to receptors that are inactive, they can shut, they can turn them on, right?","cite":"transcript 9/28; Pharmacodynamics-Day_3_2026s.pdf slides ~32–~41","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Receptor-Effector Coupling & Spare Receptors","t":"Full agonists shift the conformational equilibrium of receptors more strongly than partial agonists do."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-002', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"two-state","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-recap","skill":"apply","concept":"baseline-reflects-receptor-activity","tags":[],"source":"transcript","stem":"A full agonist is given to a cell in which 50% of the receptors are active on their own. Where does its dose–response curve start and where does it end?","options":[{"t":"Starts at 50%, ends at 100%","correct":true,"why":"The baseline reflects the receptors already active; a full agonist has 100% efficacy, so it goes to 100% from wherever it starts."},{"t":"Starts at 0% and ends at 100%","correct":false,"why":"A baseline of 0% belongs to a system in which nearly all receptors are inactive (R > R*)."},{"t":"Starts at 50%, ends at 50%","correct":false,"why":"A flat line at the baseline is what a neutral antagonist gives, not a full agonist."},{"t":"Starts at 50%, ends at 0%","correct":false,"why":"Going down to 0% is the inverse agonist’s curve."}],"teach":"The baseline is a reflection of the receptor activity in the system before any drug is given. With most receptors inactive (R > R*, one sign on the exam means about 99% inactive) the baseline is 0%. With half the receptors active it is 50%. A full agonist goes to 100% independently of where it starts; if all receptors are already active the curve is a flat line at 100%.","quote":"It, the baseline is just a reflection of the receptor activity that is happening. But when I go from 50, I go all the way to 100.","cite":"transcript 9/28; Pharmacodynamics-Day_3_2026s.pdf slides ~37–~40"},
{id:'gtap-L04-003', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"partial","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-recap","skill":"figure","multi":true,"concept":"partial-agonist-range","tags":["poll"],"source":"transcript","stem":"Five drugs at the same receptor are plotted as dose–response curves, A–E, each given on its own. Which of these drugs are partial agonists? Select all that apply.","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":-1.2,"emax":80},{"label":"B","ec":0.2,"emax":75},{"label":"C","ec":-0.4,"emax":25},{"label":"D","ec":0.6,"emax":5},{"label":"E","ec":-0.5,"emax":100}],"base":0,"x":"log dose","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"Drug A (80%)","correct":true,"why":"Any effect between 1% and 99% is a partial agonist; 80% is close to the top but not 100%."},{"t":"Drug B (75%)","correct":true,"why":"75% lies between 1% and 99%."},{"t":"Drug C (25%)","correct":true,"why":"25% lies between 1% and 99%."},{"t":"Drug D (5%)","correct":true,"why":"5% is a weak but positive effect, so the drug is still a partial agonist."},{"t":"Drug E (100%)","correct":false,"why":"A drug that reaches 100% is a full agonist, not a partial agonist."}],"teach":"A partial agonist is any drug that produces an effect between 1% and 99% of the system’s maximum: equal to or greater than 1 and equal to or less than 99. At 100% it is a full agonist; at 0% it is not a partial agonist. A curve that gets close to 100% but does not reach it is still partial.","quote":"a partial agonist is going to be any drug that produce an effect between 1 and 99, right? Has to be equal or greater than 1, and equal or less than 99.","cite":"transcript 9/28; Pharmacodynamics-Day_3_2026s.pdf slides ~50–~52","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Partial Agonists","t":"Partial agonists produce a lower response at full receptor occupancy than full agonists do."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-004', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"drc-basic","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-recap","skill":"figure","concept":"fa-plus-fa","tags":[],"source":"transcript","stem":"Levophed is synthetic norepinephrine. The dotted line is norepinephrine alone. A pretreatment dose of Levophed that by itself produces 40% of the maximal effect is given, then the norepinephrine dose–response curve is rebuilt. Which solid curve shows the result?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"NE","ec":0,"emax":100,"dashed":true},{"label":"A","ec":-1,"emax":100,"base":40},{"label":"B","ec":1,"emax":100},{"label":"C","ec":-1,"emax":60,"base":40},{"label":"D","ec":1,"emax":100,"base":40}],"base":0,"x":"log dose norepinephrine","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"A: starts at 40%, shifts left, still reaches 100%","correct":true,"why":"Levophed has already activated enough receptors for 40%, norepinephrine needs fewer receptors of its own (shift left), and both drugs reach the same Emax."},{"t":"B: starts at 0%, shifts right, still reaches 100%","correct":false,"why":"A second full agonist helps; a right shift belongs to drugs that compete against the agonist."},{"t":"C: starts at 40%, shifts left, plateaus at 60%","correct":false,"why":"Emax does not change: the heart cannot beat faster than its maximum whether one or two full agonists are present."},{"t":"D: starts at 40%, shifts right, reaches 100%","correct":false,"why":"The raised starting point is right, but a right shift means norepinephrine was made less potent; a second full agonist shares the work and shifts the curve left."}],"teach":"A full agonist in the presence of another full agonist: the second drug activates some receptors on the first drug’s behalf, so the first drug appears more potent (shift left, apparent affinity increased); a pretreatment dose that already produces an effect raises the baseline; and Emax is unchanged since both are reversible and both want the same maximal effect.","quote":"now norepinephrine still goes to 100%, but it’s further to the left and the baseline has going up, right? Because levofed has efficacy, positive efficacy.","cite":"transcript 9/28; Pharmacodynamics-Day_3_2026s.pdf slides ~42–~49"},
{id:'gtap-L04-005', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"potency","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-recap","skill":"figure","concept":"fa-plus-fa","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"If DRC B is the DRC of norepinephrine binding to the β1 receptor alone, which DRC best represents the DRC of norepinephrine in the presence of epinephrine?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":-1,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":0,"emax":100},{"label":"C","ec":1,"emax":100},{"label":"D","ec":1,"emax":50}],"base":0,"x":"log dose norepinephrine","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"A","correct":true,"why":"Norepinephrine and epinephrine are both full agonists at β1; each makes the other appear more potent, so the curve moves left."},{"t":"B","correct":false,"why":"B is the point of reference (norepinephrine alone); a second full agonist changes the curve."},{"t":"C","correct":false,"why":"A curve to the right means the agonist appears less potent, which is what a competing drug does, not a helping one."},{"t":"D","correct":false,"why":"A lower or right-shifted curve would mean epinephrine made norepinephrine’s life harder; two full agonists help each other."},{"t":"None of the above","correct":false,"why":"One curve (A) does show the left shift produced by a second full agonist."}],"teach":"The first question to ask is whether the second drug helps the agonist or makes its life more difficult. A second full agonist helps, so the only possible answers are curves to the left; curves to the right or below are eliminated. Norepinephrine makes epinephrine more potent and epinephrine makes norepinephrine more potent.","quote":"we have two full agonists, norepinephrine and Epi, that binds to the same receptor and depending on who is bound to it, they’re going to do the same effect. And thus shifting to the left.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slide ~3; transcript 9/28"},
{id:'gtap-L04-006', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"partial","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-recap","skill":"apply","concept":"fa-plus-pa-aripiprazole","tags":[],"source":"transcript","stem":"A manic patient has a high level of dopamine (full agonist, Emax 100%) at the dopamine D2 receptor. Therapy with aripiprazole, a D2 partial agonist with an Emax of 60%, is started. What happens to the response as aripiprazole occupies more receptors than dopamine?","options":[{"t":"It falls toward 60%","correct":true,"why":"Both drugs are reversible; whichever has the higher concentration occupies more receptors, and receptors occupied by aripiprazole can only produce its 60%."},{"t":"It rises to 100%","correct":false,"why":"Adding a partial agonist to a system already at 100% cannot add efficacy; it competes and brings the response down."},{"t":"It falls to 0%","correct":false,"why":"Only an inverse agonist brings the response to 0%; a partial agonist brings it to its own efficacy."},{"t":"It stays unchanged at 100%","correct":false,"why":"Once aripiprazole outcompetes dopamine by mass action, the occupied receptors signal at 60%, so the response changes."}],"teach":"Dopamine and aripiprazole compete for the same D2 binding pocket and both are reversible, so by the law of mass action the drug with the higher concentration occupies more receptors. Receptors occupied by aripiprazole signal at its efficacy of 60%, so in a manic (high dopamine) patient the response is brought down to about 60%; in a depressed patient (low baseline) the same drug brings the response up.","quote":"as long as I’m occupying enough receptors, more than dopamine is, I can outcompete dopamine and bring that baseline to about 60%. So that my patient is not manic anymore.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_3_2026s.pdf slides ~54–~58; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Partial Agonists","t":"A partial agonist cannot cause a maximal response even when it saturates all receptors, so it competitively inhibits the responses produced by a full agonist at the same receptor."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-007', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"inverse","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-recap","skill":"tell","concept":"partial-vs-inverse-endpoint","tags":[],"source":"transcript","stem":"A system starts at 100% activity. A partial agonist with 75% efficacy is given to one preparation and an inverse agonist to another, each in high enough dose to occupy most receptors. Where does each response end?","options":[{"t":"Partial agonist 75%; inverse agonist 0%","correct":true,"why":"A partial agonist brings the response to whatever its own efficacy is; an inverse agonist brings it to 0%."},{"t":"Partial agonist 100%; inverse agonist 0%","correct":false,"why":"A partial agonist never reaches 100%, not alone and not when competing with a full agonist."},{"t":"Partial agonist 75%; inverse agonist 25%","correct":false,"why":"The inverse agonist has negative efficacy and ends at 0% regardless of the starting point."},{"t":"Partial agonist 0%; inverse agonist 0%","correct":false,"why":"A partial agonist has positive efficacy; once it occupies the receptors the response is its 75%, not zero."}],"teach":"A partial agonist has a dual nature: from a low baseline it raises the response (agonist behavior) and from a high baseline it competes the response down (antagonist behavior), but in every case the endpoint is its own efficacy (75% goes to 75%, 30% to 30%). An inverse agonist ends at 0% whether the baseline was 25%, 50% or 100%.","quote":"The key difference over here when we compare to inverse agonist is that a partial agonist is only going to bring the dose response curve to whatever its efficacy is. If it’s 75%, everything finishes at 75%.","cite":"transcript 9/28; Pharmacodynamics-Day_3_2026s.pdf slides ~59–~63; Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slide ~12"},
{id:'gtap-L04-008', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-recap","skill":"apply","concept":"partial-agonist-dual-nature","tags":["varenicline"],"source":"transcript","stem":"A patient taking varenicline for smoking cessation smokes one cigarette. Compared with smoking before therapy, what does that cigarette do?","options":[{"t":"Produces less effect than before","correct":true,"why":"Varenicline is present at a concentration high enough to outcompete the nicotine from one cigarette, so the receptors signal at the partial agonist’s level."},{"t":"Produces a larger effect than before","correct":false,"why":"A partial agonist occupying receptors competes with nicotine; it does not add to a full agonist’s effect."},{"t":"Produces the same effect as before","correct":false,"why":"Receptors occupied by varenicline cannot give nicotine’s full response."},{"t":"Brings the response to 0%","correct":false,"why":"A partial agonist has positive efficacy; bringing everything to 0% is the inverse agonist."}],"teach":"Varenicline serves two purposes. In withdrawal (no nicotine, receptors inactive) it activates some nicotinic receptors and removes the cravings without opening the ion channel 100%. If the patient smokes, varenicline outcompetes the nicotine of a cigarette, so the cigarette does little; only nonstop smoking could outcompete varenicline. Which behavior appears depends on the physiological state.","quote":"If they start to smoke and they’re taking Chantix, now Chantix is gonna outcompete nicotine, so that one cigarette, it’s not gonna do much for them.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_3_2026s.pdf slides ~64–~65; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Variation in Drug Responsiveness","t":"A partial agonist may exhibit dramatically different responses depending on the amount of endogenous full agonist present, acting as an agonist when it is low and as an antagonist when it is high."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-009', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-recap","skill":"recall","concept":"partial-agonist-table","tags":["poll","albuterol"],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following drugs is a partial agonist?","options":[{"t":"Albuterol","correct":true,"why":"Albuterol is the β2 partial agonist on the drug list; it opens the airways enough for an asthma patient to breathe without opening them 100%."},{"t":"Epinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Epinephrine is a full agonist."},{"t":"Histamine","correct":false,"why":"Histamine is the body’s full agonist at histamine receptors."},{"t":"Acetylcholine","correct":false,"why":"Acetylcholine is a full agonist."},{"t":"Metoprolol","correct":false,"why":"Metoprolol is a competitive antagonist at β1."}],"teach":"From the drug list, albuterol is the partial agonist; the others in this poll are agonists (epinephrine, histamine, acetylcholine) or an antagonist (metoprolol). A partial agonist is used when the maximal response is not needed: albuterol opens the airways just enough for an asthma patient to breathe better.","quote":"If your patient has asthma, albuterol, which is a partial agonist, is going to open up your airways just enough for you to breathe better in and out. You don’t have to open your lungs 100%.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slide ~3; transcript 9/28"},
{id:'gtap-L04-010', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"inverse","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-ia","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"inverse-agonist-two-state","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which statements describe an inverse agonist in the two-state model? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Highest affinity for receptors in the inactive state","correct":true,"why":"It binds and stabilizes the receptor in the inactive state."},{"t":"Negative efficacy","correct":true,"why":"If it binds an active receptor it shuts it off, shifting DR* to DR."},{"t":"Reversible","correct":true,"why":"All inverse agonists on the market are reversible: they bind and come off."},{"t":"Highest affinity for receptors in the active state","correct":false,"why":"Preference for active receptors belongs to full and partial agonists."},{"t":"Ends the curve between 1% and 99%","correct":false,"why":"An inverse agonist brings the curve all the way to 0%; the 1–99% plateau is the partial agonist."}],"teach":"An inverse agonist has the highest affinity for receptors in the inactive state and keeps them inactive; if it binds a receptor that is active, its negative efficacy shuts that receptor off. It lowers the dose–response curve all the way to 0%. Like every other orthosteric agonist class in this course, it is reversible.","quote":"Binds and stabilize the receptor in an inactive state ↑ Affinity. Evoke a change in the receptor - Efficacy.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~4–~12; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"Some antagonists show inverse agonist activity: they reduce receptor activity below the basal level observed in the absence of any agonist."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-011', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"inverse","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-ia","skill":"apply","concept":"inverse-agonist-needs-raised-baseline","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"An inverse agonist is given alone, in increasing doses, to a tissue in which the receptors are mostly in the inactive state (R > R*). What would its DRC look like?","options":[{"t":"A flat line at 0%","correct":true,"why":"The receptors are already inactive, which is what the inverse agonist wants; there is nothing to bring down."},{"t":"A flat line at 100%","correct":false,"why":"A flat line at 100% is the starting point of a system in which every receptor is active (R <<< R*) before the drug is added; the inverse agonist would bring it down, not leave it there."},{"t":"A curve rising to 100%","correct":false,"why":"Rising to 100% is the full agonist; the inverse agonist has negative efficacy."},{"t":"A sigmoid curve falling from 100% to 0%","correct":false,"why":"That is the inverse agonist’s curve only when the baseline has been raised, for example in a cell line whose receptors are naturally active (R <<< R*)."}],"teach":"To see what an inverse agonist does, the baseline must be raised: the drug must be put into a system whose receptors are active by themselves (for example a cancer cell line in which 100% of the receptors are active). Then the curve goes from the baseline down to 0%. At a baseline of 0% every dose gives a straight line at 0%.","quote":"if I give you my inverse agonist, where all the receptors are inactive, what would be my baseline? 0. What would it do to that baseline? Stays at zero because it's already doing what I want.","note":"His Jeopardy form of this idea (J16): \"What effect an inverse agonist would have on a full agonist DRC? Assume that the tissue has 50% of receptor present in the active state (R = R*)\", keyed \"decrease the baseline and shift the DRC to the right\". Expect the receptor state to be given as R > R*, R = R* or R <<< R*, as on Day 4 slides 7–11.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~7–~11; transcript 9/28"},
{id:'gtap-L04-012', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"inverse","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-ia","skill":"figure","multi":true,"concept":"fa-plus-inverse-agonist","tags":["histamine","loratadine"],"source":"both","stem":"Histamine dose–response curves are built in a system with 50% of receptors active, first alone (dotted line) and then after increasing pretreatment doses of loratadine. Which changes appear in histamine’s curve? Select all that apply.","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"H","ec":-0.5,"emax":100,"dashed":true},{"label":"+L","ec":0.5,"emax":100,"base":25},{"label":"+10L","ec":1.5,"emax":100,"base":3}],"base":50,"x":"log dose histamine","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"Baseline decreases","correct":true,"why":"Each loratadine dose shuts off more of the receptors that were active on their own, lowering the starting point toward 0%."},{"t":"Shift to the right","correct":true,"why":"Histamine must reach a higher concentration to outcompete loratadine, so it appears less potent."},{"t":"No change to Emax","correct":true,"why":"Loratadine is reversible; with enough histamine the inverse agonist is outcompeted and the maximal response is reached."},{"t":"Shift to the left","correct":false,"why":"A left shift means the second drug helped the agonist; loratadine competes against histamine."},{"t":"Emax decreases","correct":false,"why":"Only an irreversible antagonist or an allosteric antagonist affecting efficacy lowers the Emax."}],"teach":"Take-home message for a full agonist in the presence of an inverse agonist: apparent affinity of the agonist decreases (shift to the right, appears less potent), basal activity of the receptors is reduced (baseline goes down, eventually to 0%), and there is no effect on Emax because the inverse agonist is reversible and can always be outcompeted.","quote":"So, take home message over here, it’s gonna bring the baseline down to 0, and it’s gonna shift to the right with no change to the Emax, right?","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~13–~22; transcript 9/28","note":"Slide ~22 says \"No Effect on Emax against a high efficacy agonist\"; in class the rule was stated without the qualifier: histamine, a full agonist, always gets back to Emax because loratadine is reversible. For the exam, use the class rule: reversible drugs never change the Emax of a full agonist."},
{id:'gtap-L04-013', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-ia","skill":"recall","concept":"fa-plus-inverse-emax-why","tags":["histamine","loratadine"],"source":"both","stem":"In the presence of loratadine, histamine still reaches its maximal response if enough histamine is released. Which property of loratadine makes this possible?","options":[{"t":"It is reversible","correct":true,"why":"Loratadine binds and comes off, so whichever drug has the higher concentration wins the receptors; enough histamine outcompetes it."},{"t":"It has positive efficacy","correct":false,"why":"Loratadine has negative efficacy; efficacy is not what lets histamine reach Emax."},{"t":"It binds an allosteric site","correct":false,"why":"Loratadine competes for the same orthosteric pocket as histamine."},{"t":"It forms covalent bonds","correct":false,"why":"Covalent binding would make it irreversible and would lower the Emax instead."}],"teach":"Any drug that is reversible, competitive and surmountable can be outcompeted: the agonist only has to be present at a higher concentration than the inverse agonist. That is why the inverse agonist changes potency (shift right) and baseline but never the ability of the full agonist to reach Emax. A higher loratadine dose simply requires more histamine to outcompete it.","quote":"They are reversible. As long as I can outcompete them because they’re going to bind and they’re going to come off, whoever has the highest concentration is going to occupy the most receptors to it.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~14, ~21; transcript 9/28"},
{id:'gtap-L04-014', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"classes","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-ia","skill":"tell","concept":"inverse-vs-competitive-need-baseline","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"A full agonist’s curve is built with the baseline at 0%, then rebuilt after pretreatment with drug B. B shifts the curve to the right with no change to the baseline or the Emax. Which classification of B cannot be ruled out from this figure alone?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A alone","ec":-0.5,"emax":100,"dashed":true},{"label":"A + B","ec":0.7,"emax":100}],"base":0,"x":"log dose agonist","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"Inverse agonist or competitive antagonist","correct":true,"why":"At a baseline of 0% an inverse agonist has nothing to lower, so it looks exactly like a neutral competitive antagonist; a raised baseline is needed to tell them apart."},{"t":"Full agonist or allosteric agonist","correct":false,"why":"Both would help the agonist and shift the curve to the left."},{"t":"Partial agonist or full agonist","correct":false,"why":"A partial agonist has positive efficacy and would change the baseline; a full agonist shifts left."},{"t":"Irreversible antagonist or allosteric antagonist affecting efficacy","correct":false,"why":"An allosteric antagonist affecting efficacy always lowers the Emax, so this pair is out; the irreversible antagonist is only excluded once enough shifts are drawn without a drop (spare receptors can delay it)."}],"teach":"The system must have receptors in the active state (R = R*) to distinguish a full agonist plus inverse agonist from a full agonist plus competitive antagonist. With the baseline already at 0% both give a right shift with no change in baseline or Emax. Antihistamines were classed as antagonists until cell lines with naturally active receptors showed that they shut activity down.","quote":"if I give you that figure in the right, you can’t differentiate between a competitive and an inverse because you’re at 0 and there’s no change. I have to give you something different for you to see a change to occur.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~15, ~22; transcript 9/28"},
{id:'gtap-L04-015', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-ia","skill":"recall","concept":"constitutive-activity-inverse","tags":["loratadine"],"source":"both","stem":"Which receptors are more sensitive to inverse agonists?","options":[{"t":"Receptors with constitutive activity","correct":true,"why":"Receptors that are active on their own give the inverse agonist something to shut down; antihistamines such as loratadine and cetirizine act this way."},{"t":"Receptors held entirely in the inactive state","correct":false,"why":"With every receptor inactive an inverse agonist produces a flat line at 0%."},{"t":"Receptors bound by an irreversible antagonist","correct":false,"why":"Covalently blocked receptors are not available to any other drug."},{"t":"Receptors with no orthosteric site","correct":false,"why":"Inverse agonists in this course are orthosteric and compete for the binding pocket."}],"teach":"Receptors that have constitutive activity (active by themselves, without any drug, hormone or neurotransmitter) are more sensitive to inverse agonists. The antihistamines cetirizine and loratadine are the classic inverse agonists; histamine, opioid, cannabinoid, dopamine, bradykinin and adenosine receptors are listed as showing this. Loratadine is the one inverse agonist to know on the drug list.","quote":"Receptors that have constitutive activity are more sensitive to inverse agonists.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slide ~23; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"Inverse agonist activity means the drug reduces receptor activity below the basal level seen with no agonist present at all."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-016', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"classes","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-ia","skill":"figure","concept":"five-curve-e-loratadine","tags":["poll","loratadine"],"source":"both","stem":"The DRCs below represent the individual DRCs of five different drugs. DRC E most likely represents the DRC of:","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":-1,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":0.6,"emax":100},{"label":"C","ec":0,"emax":75},{"label":"D","ec":0,"emax":50},{"label":"E","ec":0,"emax":0}],"base":50,"x":"log dose","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"Loratadine","correct":true,"why":"A curve that brings the baseline to 0% is an inverse agonist, and loratadine is the inverse agonist on the drug list."},{"t":"Norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Norepinephrine is a full agonist; its curve rises to 100%."},{"t":"Albuterol","correct":false,"why":"Albuterol is a partial agonist; its curve rises to a plateau below 100%."},{"t":"Epinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Epinephrine is a full agonist; its curve rises to 100%."}],"teach":"Two steps: first classify curve E (it lowers the baseline to zero, so it is an inverse agonist), then find the drug on the list that belongs to that class (loratadine, an antihistamine). Knowing the classifications without knowing the drugs, or the drugs without the classifications, leaves the question unanswerable.","quote":"Because it’s bringing the baseline to zero. The next step is which of these drugs would fall into the category of being an inverse agonist.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slide ~24; transcript 9/28"},
{id:'gtap-L04-017', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-allo","skill":"recall","concept":"allosteric-two-things","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"What are the two things to know about an allosteric agonist?","options":[{"t":"Two drugs bound at once; affects affinity or efficacy or both","correct":true,"why":"It binds a different site, so the agonist can be bound at the same time, and it changes the pocket in a way that alters affinity, efficacy or both."},{"t":"Competes for the orthosteric pocket; has positive efficacy of its own","correct":false,"why":"An allosteric drug binds someplace else and does not compete for the pocket."},{"t":"Forms covalent bonds; lowers the receptor pool","correct":false,"why":"Covalent, pool-lowering binding is the irreversible antagonist; all allosterics in this course are reversible."},{"t":"Binds only inactive receptors; has negative efficacy","correct":false,"why":"That describes the inverse agonist."}],"teach":"Allosteric drugs are the exception to one-or-the-other binding: two drugs can be bound to the receptor at the same time. The allosteric agonist binds someplace else in the receptor and changes the orthosteric pocket so the agonist binds more easily, increasing its affinity, its efficacy or both. For this course all allosterics are reversible.","quote":"Allosteric: 2 Things you should know - Two drugs bound at the same time - Affinity or Efficacy or Both.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~25–~28; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"Allosteric modulators bind a site on the receptor separate from the orthosteric site bound by the agonist, so they modify receptor activity without blocking agonist binding."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-018', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"potency","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-allo","skill":"figure","concept":"allosteric-agonist-affinity-only","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"If DRC B is the DRC of an agonist alone, which curve would best represent agonist B in the presence of an allosteric agonist that affects only the affinity?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":-1.2,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":0,"emax":100},{"label":"C","ec":-0.6,"emax":60},{"label":"D","ec":1.2,"emax":100},{"label":"E","ec":1,"emax":45}],"base":0,"x":"log dose","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"A","correct":true,"why":"An allosteric agonist helps, so the curve moves left; affinity-only means the maximal response is untouched."},{"t":"B","correct":false,"why":"B is the point of reference; an allosteric agonist changes the agonist’s curve."},{"t":"C","correct":false,"why":"A changed maximum means efficacy was affected, which the stem rules out."},{"t":"D","correct":false,"why":"A right shift with the same maximum is an allosteric antagonist affecting affinity."},{"t":"E","correct":false,"why":"A changed maximum means efficacy was affected, which the stem rules out."}],"teach":"An allosteric agonist that affects only affinity shifts the agonist’s curve to the left (more potent) with no change to the efficacy. Curves whose maximum changed (C, E) involve efficacy; a curve to the right (D) means the agonist was made less potent, which is an allosteric antagonist affecting affinity.","quote":"Help, which means you’re gonna make the drug more potent, right? And it because it only affects affinity, you’re not affecting the ability to produce its effect, is moving to the left.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~26, ~42; transcript 9/28"},
{id:'gtap-L04-019', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"efficacy","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-allo","skill":"figure","concept":"allosteric-agonist-efficacy-only","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"The dotted line is drug A alone, which plateaus at 60% (a partial agonist). An allosteric agonist that affects only the efficacy of drug A is added. Which solid curve shows the result?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":0,"emax":60,"dashed":true},{"label":"W","ec":0,"emax":100},{"label":"X","ec":-1.2,"emax":60},{"label":"Y","ec":1.2,"emax":35},{"label":"Z","ec":-1.2,"emax":100}],"base":0,"x":"log dose drug A","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"W: rises to a higher maximum with no shift left or right","correct":true,"why":"Efficacy is read on the y-axis: the partial agonist now behaves like a full agonist, while its EC50 position is unchanged."},{"t":"X: shifts left with the same 60% maximum","correct":false,"why":"A pure left shift is an affinity effect, not an efficacy effect."},{"t":"Y: shifts right with a lower maximum","correct":false,"why":"A right shift plus lower maximum is an allosteric antagonist affecting both affinity and efficacy."},{"t":"Z: shifts left and also rises to a higher maximum than before","correct":false,"why":"Both changes together mean affinity and efficacy were affected; the stem says efficacy only."}],"teach":"An allosteric agonist affecting efficacy only does not move the curve left or right; it moves it up, so a partial agonist can be converted into a full agonist. Affecting affinity only shifts the curve left. Affecting both shifts left and raises the maximum. A full agonist already at 100% cannot show an efficacy effect, so only the affinity shift is visible.","quote":"I have that drug in the presence of an allosteric agonist that affects efficacy only now it’s not moving left or right, it’s moving what up ... It went from being a partial agonist to a full agonist, right?","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~27–~29; transcript 9/28"},
{id:'gtap-L04-020', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-allo","skill":"figure","multi":true,"concept":"allosteric-characteristics","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"A full agonist’s curve (dotted line) is rebuilt after each of several increasing doses of an allosteric agonist (solid lines, 1×, 10× and 100×). Which features are seen? Select all that apply.","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"alone","ec":0.6,"emax":100,"dashed":true},{"label":"1×","ec":-0.3,"emax":100},{"label":"10×","ec":-1.1,"emax":100},{"label":"100×","ec":-1.7,"emax":100}],"base":0,"x":"log dose agonist","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"Shifts to the left","correct":true,"why":"The allosteric agonist increases the agonist’s affinity, so it appears more potent."},{"t":"Shifts of unequal size","correct":true,"why":"Binding at the allosteric site is non-competitive and does not depend on the agonist, so each dose occupies an unpredictable fraction; the shifts are asymmetrical."},{"t":"Shifts stop growing at high doses","correct":true,"why":"Once all allosteric sites are occupied the drug has done all it can; the effect is saturable."},{"t":"Emax rises above 100%","correct":false,"why":"A full agonist is already at 100%, so no efficacy effect can be seen."},{"t":"Shifts of equal size","correct":false,"why":"Equal (symmetrical) shifts belong to orthosteric competition, for example a competitive antagonist."}],"teach":"Four characteristics of a full agonist with multiple doses of an allosteric agonist: affinity affected (yes, shift left), efficacy affected (no, already at 100%), symmetrical shifts (no, the shifts differ in size), saturability (yes, after a certain number of shifts there is a limit to how much the allosteric drug can affect the system). Asymmetrical, saturable shifts are how an allosteric drug is recognized.","quote":"I can keep giving more and more of my allosteric, and he can’t go more to the left. He has maxed out all that he can do to the system.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~30–~34; transcript 9/28"},
{id:'gtap-L04-021', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-antag","skill":"tell","concept":"chemical-vs-physiological-antagonism","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors lowers heart rate while epinephrine at β1 receptors raises it. What kind of antagonism is this?","options":[{"t":"Physiological","correct":true,"why":"Two agonists acting independently at different receptors in the same organ produce opposing effects."},{"t":"Chemical","correct":false,"why":"Chemical antagonism is a direct chemical interaction between the agonist and the antagonist, as with chelating agents such as dimercaprol."},{"t":"Competitive (orthosteric)","correct":false,"why":"The two drugs bind different receptors; they do not compete for the same pocket."},{"t":"Allosteric","correct":false,"why":"Neither drug binds another site on the other’s receptor."}],"teach":"Chemical antagonism: a direct chemical interaction between the agonist and the antagonist, for example chelating agents such as dimercaprol used to treat gold, mercury or arsenic poisoning. Physiological antagonism: two agonists acting independently of each other on different receptors and producing opposing effects, for example acetylcholine and epinephrine in the heart. The pharmacological antagonists (competitive, irreversible, allosteric) are the focus.","quote":"Physiological: Two agonist acting independent of each other producing opposing effects. Ach and Epi in the heart.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slide ~35; transcript 9/28"},
{id:'gtap-L04-022', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-antag","skill":"recall","concept":"antagonist-synonyms","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which term is another name for a competitive antagonist?","options":[{"t":"Surmountable","correct":true,"why":"Competitive, reversible and surmountable are different names for the same drug: it binds and comes off, and the agonist can outcompete it."},{"t":"Insurmountable","correct":false,"why":"Insurmountable names the irreversible antagonist, which cannot be overcome by more agonist."},{"t":"Non-equilibrium","correct":false,"why":"Non-equilibrium names the irreversible antagonist; there is no balance between binding and unbinding."},{"t":"Non-competitive","correct":false,"why":"Non-competitive covers the irreversible antagonist (binds and does not come off) and the allosteric antagonist (binds someplace else)."}],"teach":"Pharmacological antagonisms: competitive (orthosteric) = surmountable = reversible, the most common in clinical practice, agonist and antagonist competing for the same receptor site; nonequilibrium-competitive (orthosteric) = irreversible = insurmountable, cannot be overcome by increasing the agonist concentration; allosteric antagonist = non-competitive, reduces the agonist’s affinity and/or efficacy from another site.","quote":"One is competitive antagonist, also known as reversible antagonists, also known as surmountable antagonists, different names for the same drug. They bind and they come off.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slide ~36; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"Sufficiently high concentrations of agonist can surmount the effect of a given concentration of a competitive antagonist, whereas agonists cannot surmount a noncompetitive antagonist irrespective of their concentration."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-023', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-antag","skill":"recall","concept":"pam-nam","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"In the literature, what is a negative allosteric modulator (NAM)?","options":[{"t":"An allosteric antagonist","correct":true,"why":"A NAM binds another site and reduces the agonist’s affinity and/or efficacy."},{"t":"An allosteric agonist","correct":false,"why":"The allosteric agonist is the positive allosteric modulator (PAM)."},{"t":"An inverse agonist","correct":false,"why":"An inverse agonist is orthosteric with negative efficacy, not an allosteric ligand."},{"t":"An irreversible antagonist","correct":false,"why":"The irreversible antagonist binds the orthosteric pocket covalently."}],"teach":"Allosteric ligands: the allosteric agonist is a positive allosteric modulator (PAM); the allosteric antagonist is a negative allosteric modulator (NAM). Whatever the positive one does (raises affinity, efficacy or both), the negative one does the opposite.","quote":"we can also call allosteric agonists PAMs, positive allosteric modulators, you see that in the literature, or NAMs, negative allosteric modulators, but the key thing is if you know what the positive does, the negative is the opposite.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slide ~37; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"Negative allosteric modulators bind an allosteric site to reduce receptor activity; positive allosteric modulators bind an allosteric site to increase it."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-024', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"efficacy","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-allo","skill":"figure","concept":"allosteric-antagonist-effects","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"If DRC D is the DRC of an agonist alone, which curve would best represent agonist D in the presence of an allosteric antagonist that affects the affinity and efficacy of D?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":-1.6,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":-0.8,"emax":100},{"label":"C","ec":-1,"emax":55},{"label":"D","ec":0,"emax":100},{"label":"E","ec":1.1,"emax":55}],"base":0,"x":"log dose","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"E","correct":true,"why":"Lower affinity shifts the curve right and lower efficacy brings the maximum down."},{"t":"C","correct":false,"why":"C moves left (affinity improved) but drops the maximum (efficacy reduced); no drug does that."},{"t":"A","correct":false,"why":"A left shift means the agonist was helped; an antagonist makes its life harder."},{"t":"B","correct":false,"why":"A left shift with the same maximum would be an allosteric agonist affecting affinity."},{"t":"D","correct":false,"why":"D is the point of reference; the allosteric antagonist changes the curve."}],"teach":"An allosteric antagonist is the opposite of the allosteric agonist. Affinity only: shift to the right, Emax still reached at higher doses. Efficacy only: no shift, the full agonist behaves like a partial agonist. Both: shift right and a lower maximum. The shifts are asymmetrical and the drug is non-competitive (it binds someplace else). A curve that moves left while losing its maximum has no matching drug.","quote":"it’s affecting the affinity, so it’s gonna shift to the right and it’s affecting the efficacy, so it’s gonna bring it down. So E would be the best answer.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~38–~42; transcript 9/28"},
{id:'gtap-L04-025', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-allo","skill":"tell","concept":"noncompetitive-two-reasons","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Irreversible antagonists and allosteric antagonists are both called non-competitive. What makes each one non-competitive?","options":[{"t":"Irreversible: binds and does not come off; allosteric: binds a different site","correct":true,"why":"No competition is possible with a drug that never leaves the pocket, or with a drug that never enters it."},{"t":"Both bind the orthosteric pocket covalently","correct":false,"why":"Only the irreversible antagonist forms covalent bonds; allosterics are reversible in this course."},{"t":"Both have negative efficacy","correct":false,"why":"Negative efficacy is the inverse agonist; these antagonists have no pharmacological efficacy of their own."},{"t":"Irreversible: binds a different site; allosteric: binds and does not come off","correct":false,"why":"The reasons are reversed."}],"teach":"Two different reasons give the same label. The irreversible antagonist forms covalent bonds in the orthosteric pocket and never comes off, so there is no equilibrium or competition. The allosteric antagonist binds reversibly at another site in the receptor, so it is not competing for the pocket at all; the agonist cannot overcome it and the shifts it causes are asymmetrical.","quote":"One because one binds and doesn’t come off, the other one because it binds someplace else. There’s no competition with it along the way.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~36, ~41; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"Once a receptor is bound by a noncompetitive antagonist, agonists cannot surmount the inhibition; some noncompetitive antagonists bind irreversibly, others bind reversibly at a separate allosteric site."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-026', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"two-state","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-comp","skill":"apply","concept":"competitive-antagonist-neutral-alone","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"A competitive antagonist alone is given in increasing doses to a system in which 50% of the receptors are active. What does its own dose–response curve show?","options":[{"t":"A flat line at 50%","correct":true,"why":"The antagonist has equal affinity for active and inactive receptors and no efficacy, so it leaves the balance where it found it."},{"t":"A curve falling from 50% to 0%","correct":false,"why":"Bringing active receptors down to zero requires negative efficacy, which is the inverse agonist."},{"t":"A curve rising from 50% to 100%","correct":false,"why":"Rising to 100% requires positive efficacy, which the antagonist lacks."},{"t":"A flat line at 0%","correct":false,"why":"The antagonist does not change the baseline; the system stays at the 50% it started with."}],"teach":"A competitive antagonist is neutral in the two-state model: it does not care whether the receptor is active or inactive, has equal affinity for both states (R = affinity = R*), and does not evoke a change in the receptor. It has affinity but no pharmacological efficacy. Alone, it keeps the baseline wherever it is: 0 stays 0, 50 stays 50, 100 stays 100.","quote":"they don’t care what the receptor is, active or inactive, they hang out with both types. They don’t discriminate as such, they don’t change the balance of the receptor activity.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~43–~44; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"Receptor antagonists bind to receptors but do not activate them; their primary action is to reduce the effects of agonists that normally activate the receptor."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-027', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"competitive","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-comp","skill":"figure","multi":true,"concept":"competitive-antagonist-shift","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Drug A is a full agonist and drug B is a competitive antagonist at the same receptor. A’s curve (dotted line) is rebuilt after increasing doses of B (solid lines). Which statements describe the result? Select all that apply.","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":-1.5,"emax":100,"dashed":true},{"label":"+B","ec":-0.5,"emax":100},{"label":"+10B","ec":0.5,"emax":100},{"label":"+100B","ec":1.5,"emax":100}],"base":0,"x":"log dose drug A","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"Shift to the right","correct":true,"why":"A must reach a higher concentration to outcompete B for the receptors, so its ED50 rises and it appears less potent."},{"t":"No change to the baseline","correct":true,"why":"B is neutral: it has no efficacy, so it neither raises nor lowers the starting point."},{"t":"No change to Emax","correct":true,"why":"B is reversible, so with enough A the antagonist is outcompeted and the maximal response is reached."},{"t":"Symmetrical shifts","correct":true,"why":"Each tenfold increase in B needs a tenfold increase in A; the shifts are the same size."},{"t":"Emax decreases","correct":false,"why":"A lower Emax is the mark of an irreversible antagonist or an allosteric antagonist affecting efficacy."},{"t":"Baseline decreases","correct":false,"why":"A falling baseline is the inverse agonist; a competitive antagonist has no efficacy."}],"teach":"Full agonist plus competitive antagonist: shift to the right (A can outcompete B, but needs a higher dose), no change to the baseline (B has no efficacy), no change to Emax (B is reversible), and symmetrical shifts (one or the other occupies the pocket, and a tenfold increase in antagonist needs a tenfold increase in agonist). The shifts can continue toward infinity.","quote":"Very symmetrical shifts with no change to the baseline, no change to the Emax, just shifting to the right.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~45–~50; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"A competitive antagonist increases the agonist concentration required for a given response, shifting the agonist curve to the right, while the Emax for the agonist stays the same at any fixed antagonist concentration."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-028', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"competitive","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-comp","skill":"calc","concept":"competitive-tenfold-rule","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"A full agonist’s curve has been shifted to the right by one dose of a competitive antagonist. The antagonist dose is then increased 10 times. By how much must the agonist dose increase to outcompete the antagonist again?","options":[{"t":"10 times","correct":true,"why":"By the law of mass action, whoever has the greater concentration occupies more receptors; matching a tenfold antagonist increase takes a tenfold agonist increase, giving symmetrical shifts."},{"t":"2 times","correct":false,"why":"A twofold increase would leave the antagonist with the higher concentration."},{"t":"100 times","correct":false,"why":"A hundredfold increase would be more than the competition requires; the shifts are proportional."},{"t":"It cannot outcompete the antagonist","correct":false,"why":"A competitive antagonist is reversible, so the agonist can always outcompete it with enough drug."}],"teach":"Competition for one orthosteric pocket follows the law of mass action: the drug with the greater concentration occupies the most receptors. If the antagonist dose is increased tenfold, the agonist dose must be increased tenfold to outcompete it, so successive shifts are symmetrical. A competitive antagonist raises the agonist’s ED50, so the agonist appears less potent.","quote":"if I increase the dose by tenfold, I have to increase the dose of the other by tenfold to outcompete them.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~47–~49; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"The agonist concentration needed for a given effect in the presence of a competitive antagonist is greater than without it, and the ratio of the two concentrations depends on the antagonist concentration."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-029', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"irreversible","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-irrev","skill":"recall","concept":"irreversible-antagonist-mechanism","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Why does an irreversible antagonist lower the maximal response (Emax) of a full agonist?","options":[{"t":"It removes receptors from the available pool permanently","correct":true,"why":"Covalent bonds lock the antagonist on, so the receptors it occupies are gone; once too few remain, the agonist cannot reach Emax no matter the dose."},{"t":"It has negative efficacy","correct":false,"why":"The irreversible antagonist is neutral, with no pharmacological efficacy; it does not change receptor state."},{"t":"It changes the shape of the orthosteric pocket from another site","correct":false,"why":"Changing the pocket from another site is the allosteric antagonist."},{"t":"It has the lowest affinity of the drug classes","correct":false,"why":"It has the greatest affinity (smallest Kd) of any class."}],"teach":"An irreversible antagonist forms covalent bonds that cannot be reversed under normal metabolic function, so it has the greatest affinity (smallest Kd) and once bound it does not come off. That lowers the number of receptors available. With fewer receptors the agonist cannot outcompete it, so it cannot reach the maximal effect, and at high enough antagonist doses the response can be abolished completely.","quote":"There’s a lowering of my receptor pool availability because some of them are going to be permanently bound to the irreversible antagonist. And because I don’t have enough receptors available and I can’t outcompete it, I can put all the drug I want, I cannot outcompete those guys.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~51–~53; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"After an irreversible antagonist occupies some proportion of receptors, the number of unoccupied receptors may be too low for the agonist, even at high concentrations, to produce the previous maximal response."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-030', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"irreversible","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-irrev","skill":"figure","concept":"irreversible-antagonist-curve","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"A full agonist’s curve (dotted line) is rebuilt after increasing doses of an irreversible antagonist (solid lines). Which set of changes is seen?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"alone","ec":-1.5,"emax":100,"dashed":true},{"label":"1×","ec":-0.5,"emax":100},{"label":"10×","ec":0.4,"emax":65},{"label":"100×","ec":1,"emax":25}],"base":0,"x":"log dose agonist","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"Shift right, Emax falls, baseline unchanged","correct":true,"why":"Fewer receptors remain, so a higher agonist dose is needed and the maximum drops, eventually to zero; the neutral antagonist does not move the baseline."},{"t":"Shift right, Emax unchanged, baseline unchanged","correct":false,"why":"An unchanged Emax is the competitive antagonist; an irreversible one is not outcompeted."},{"t":"Shift right, Emax unchanged, baseline falls","correct":false,"why":"A falling baseline belongs to the inverse agonist."},{"t":"Shift left, Emax unchanged, baseline rises","correct":false,"why":"That is a second full agonist helping the first."}],"teach":"Take-home message for the irreversible antagonist: no change to the baseline (it is neutral, no efficacy), shift to the right (competition for the same pocket, symmetrical), and a decrease in Emax that can go all the way to 0 and abolish the curve completely. The agonist appears both less potent (higher ED50) and less efficacious.","quote":"IrA = ↑ED50 FA = Appears less Potent. ↓ Emax FA = Appears less efficacious.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~53–~54; transcript 9/28","note":"Katzung states an irreversible antagonist reduces the maximal effect \"although it may not change its EC50\"; the slides and class say the curve shifts to the right with a higher ED50 as well as a lower Emax. The exam is written from the lecture: shift right and reduced Emax.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"An irreversible antagonist reduces the maximal effect the agonist can achieve, and high agonist concentrations cannot overcome it."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-031', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"classes","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-irrev","skill":"tell","multi":true,"concept":"emax-lowering-classes","tags":[],"source":"transcript","stem":"Which drug classes can lower the Emax of a full agonist? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Irreversible antagonist","correct":true,"why":"It permanently removes receptors, so the agonist cannot outcompete it to reach the maximum."},{"t":"Allosteric antagonist that affects efficacy","correct":true,"why":"It changes the pocket so the agonist signals less, lowering the maximum without competition."},{"t":"Competitive antagonist","correct":false,"why":"Reversible, so the agonist always outcompetes it; Emax is unchanged."},{"t":"Inverse agonist","correct":false,"why":"Reversible, so the agonist always outcompetes it; it lowers the baseline, not the Emax."},{"t":"Allosteric agonist","correct":false,"why":"It helps the agonist; it raises efficacy or affinity rather than lowering the maximum."}],"teach":"Concrete exam rule: anything that lowers the Emax has to be either an irreversible antagonist or an allosteric antagonist that affects efficacy. Every other class is reversible and competitive, so the Emax is unchanged. The difference between the two: the allosteric antagonist saturates and cannot bring the response all the way down, while the irreversible antagonist can abolish the response completely.","quote":"If you see something that it lowers the Emax, you know that has to be either an irreversible antagonist or an allosteric antagonist that affects efficacy, right? Those are the only two that can affect the Emax.","cite":"transcript 9/28; Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~39–~41, ~53–~54"},
{id:'gtap-L04-032', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"competitive","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-comp","skill":"figure","concept":"dotted-line-drug-x-competitive","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"The dotted line represents the DRC of the agonist alone and the solid lines represent the DRC of the agonist in the presence of increasing doses of drug \"X.\" Drug X is most likely:","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"alone","ec":-1.5,"emax":100,"dashed":true},{"label":"X","ec":-0.5,"emax":100},{"label":"10X","ec":0.5,"emax":100},{"label":"100X","ec":1.5,"emax":100}],"base":0,"x":"log dose agonist","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"A competitive antagonist","correct":true,"why":"Right shift with the baseline and the Emax unchanged is the competitive antagonist’s signature."},{"t":"A full agonist","correct":false,"why":"A full agonist would help and move the curve to the left."},{"t":"A partial agonist","correct":false,"why":"A partial agonist has efficacy and would change the baseline."},{"t":"An irreversible antagonist","correct":false,"why":"An irreversible antagonist lowers the Emax; here the maximum is unchanged."},{"t":"An allosteric agonist","correct":false,"why":"An allosteric agonist would help the agonist and shift it to the left."}],"teach":"Process of elimination: drugs that help the agonist (full agonist, allosteric agonist) move it left, so they are out. A partial agonist has efficacy and would affect the baseline. An irreversible antagonist would lower the Emax. What remains, a right shift with the baseline and Emax unchanged, is the competitive antagonist. With too few shifts an irreversible antagonist may look competitive at first.","quote":"Who can you eliminate right away? A full agonist because they’ll move you to the left or an allosteric agonist, right?","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slide ~55; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"In the presence of a competitive antagonist, higher agonist concentrations are required to produce a given effect, so the agonist curve is shifted to the right and high agonist concentrations overcome the inhibition."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-033', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"competitive","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-comp","skill":"apply","concept":"drug-x-metoprolol","tags":["poll","metoprolol"],"source":"both","stem":"The dotted line represents the DRC of norepinephrine alone and the solid lines represent the DRC of norepinephrine in the presence of increasing doses of drug \"X.\" Drug X is most likely:","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"NE","ec":-1.5,"emax":100,"dashed":true},{"label":"X","ec":-0.5,"emax":100},{"label":"10X","ec":0.5,"emax":100},{"label":"100X","ec":1.5,"emax":100}],"base":0,"x":"log dose norepinephrine","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"Metoprolol","correct":true,"why":"The figure shows a competitive, reversible, surmountable antagonist, and metoprolol is the β1 competitive antagonist on the drug list."},{"t":"Epinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Epinephrine is a full agonist and would shift norepinephrine to the left."},{"t":"Albuterol","correct":false,"why":"Albuterol is a partial agonist and would change the baseline."},{"t":"Phenoxybenzamine","correct":false,"why":"Phenoxybenzamine is the only irreversible antagonist on the list; it would lower the Emax."},{"t":"Diazepam","correct":false,"why":"Diazepam is the allosteric agonist on the list and would help the agonist."}],"teach":"Same classes of drugs, just different drug names: first read the figure (competitive antagonist), then match it to the drug list. Epinephrine is a full agonist, albuterol the partial agonist, phenoxybenzamine the only irreversible antagonist on the list, diazepam the allosteric agonist, and metoprolol the competitive reversible surmountable antagonist.","quote":"So same classes of drugs. Just different drug names.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slide ~55; transcript 9/28"},
{id:'gtap-L04-034', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"spare","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-irrev","skill":"apply","concept":"spare-receptors-irreversible","tags":[],"source":"transcript","stem":"An agonist needs to occupy only 10% of a tissue’s receptors to produce its maximal response. An irreversible antagonist that blocks 75% of the receptors is given. Can the agonist still produce its maximal response?","options":[{"t":"Yes, at a higher agonist dose","correct":true,"why":"25% of the receptors remain, more than the 10% needed, so the maximum is reached once enough agonist is given to occupy them."},{"t":"No, the Emax falls to 25%","correct":false,"why":"Emax only falls once the receptors available drop below the fraction the agonist needs."},{"t":"No, the response is abolished","correct":false,"why":"Abolishing the response takes blockade of essentially all the receptors."},{"t":"Yes, at the same agonist dose","correct":false,"why":"With fewer receptors available the agonist appears to have less affinity and a higher dose is required."}],"teach":"Agonists do not have to occupy 100% of the receptors to produce a response; the rest are spare receptors. If the agonist needs 10% and the irreversible antagonist leaves 25% or 15% available, the maximum is still reached with more agonist (the curve shifts right); once fewer than 10% remain the Emax falls, all the way to zero if all receptors are blocked.","quote":"Our agonists don’t have to occupy 100% of the receptors to produce a response. They only have to occupy a certain percentage of those receptors to do so.","cite":"transcript 9/28; Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~54, ~56","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Receptor-Effector Coupling & Spare Receptors","t":"Receptors are spare when a maximal response can be produced at an agonist concentration that does not occupy all available receptors. With spare receptors, a lower dose of irreversible antagonist can leave enough receptors for the maximal response, though a higher agonist concentration is required."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-035', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"classes","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-comp","skill":"tell","multi":true,"concept":"helps-or-hinders-direction","tags":[],"source":"transcript","stem":"A full agonist’s dose–response curve is rebuilt in the presence of a second drug. Which second drugs shift the agonist’s curve to the left? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Another full agonist","correct":true,"why":"It activates receptors on the agonist’s behalf, so less agonist is needed."},{"t":"An allosteric agonist that affects affinity","correct":true,"why":"It makes the pocket more accessible, so the agonist binds more easily and appears more potent."},{"t":"A competitive antagonist","correct":false,"why":"It competes for the pocket, so more agonist is needed: shift right."},{"t":"An inverse agonist","correct":false,"why":"It competes and pulls receptors inactive, so more agonist is needed: shift right."},{"t":"An irreversible antagonist","correct":false,"why":"It removes receptors, so more agonist is needed and the maximum falls."}],"teach":"Before looking at any figure, ask whether the second drug helps the agonist or makes its life more difficult. Helpers (another full agonist, an allosteric agonist) make the agonist appear more potent: shift to the left. Drugs that compete or block (competitive antagonist, inverse agonist, irreversible antagonist, allosteric antagonist affecting affinity) make it appear less potent: shift to the right. That one question eliminates most of the options.","quote":"would you assume that this other drug is gonna help or is gonna make the life more difficult for your agonist? And that should allow you to eliminate a lot of the answers.","cite":"transcript 9/28; Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~3, ~22, ~42, ~50"},
{id:'gtap-L04-036', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"competitive","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-comp","skill":"figure","concept":"competitive-antagonist-shift","tags":["poll","metoprolol"],"source":"both","stem":"If DRC B is the DRC of norepinephrine binding to the β1 receptor alone, which DRC best represents the DRC of norepinephrine in the presence of metoprolol?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":-1,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":0,"emax":100},{"label":"C","ec":1,"emax":55},{"label":"D","ec":1,"emax":100}],"base":0,"x":"log dose norepinephrine","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"A","correct":false,"why":"A moved left: norepinephrine was helped. Metoprolol competes for the β1 receptor, so it makes norepinephrine’s life harder, not easier."},{"t":"B","correct":false,"why":"B is the point of reference (norepinephrine alone); an antagonist that competes for the same receptor changes the curve."},{"t":"C","correct":false,"why":"C lost its maximum. Metoprolol is reversible, so enough norepinephrine outcompetes it and Emax is unchanged; only an irreversible or efficacy-lowering allosteric antagonist drops the Emax."},{"t":"D","correct":true,"why":"Metoprolol is a competitive, reversible β1 antagonist: shift to the right (norepinephrine appears less potent), no change to the baseline and no change to the Emax."},{"t":"None of the above","correct":false,"why":"D shows exactly the competitive antagonist’s signature: right shift, same baseline, same maximum."}],"teach":"First ask whether metoprolol helps norepinephrine or makes its life more difficult: it competes for the β1 receptor, so the curves to the left are out. Then apply the competitive antagonist rule: shift to the right, no change to the baseline (no efficacy), no change to Emax (reversible, so norepinephrine can outcompete it). A lower maximum would need an irreversible antagonist.","quote":"One is competitive antagonist, also known as reversible antagonists, also known as surmountable antagonists, different names for the same drug. They bind and they come off.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~3, ~45–~50, ~55; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"A competitive antagonist increases the agonist concentration required for a given response, shifting the agonist curve to the right, while the Emax for the agonist stays the same at any fixed antagonist concentration."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-037', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"irreversible","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-irrev","skill":"figure","concept":"irreversible-antagonist-curve","tags":["poll","phenoxybenzamine"],"source":"both","stem":"If DRC B is the DRC of norepinephrine alone, which DRC best represents the DRC of norepinephrine in the presence of phenoxybenzamine?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":-1,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":0,"emax":100},{"label":"C","ec":1,"emax":55},{"label":"D","ec":1,"emax":100}],"base":0,"x":"log dose norepinephrine","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"A","correct":false,"why":"A moved left, which means norepinephrine was helped; an antagonist makes its life more difficult."},{"t":"B","correct":false,"why":"B is the point of reference (norepinephrine alone); an antagonist that removes receptors changes the curve."},{"t":"C","correct":true,"why":"Phenoxybenzamine is the only irreversible antagonist on the drug list: covalent bonds remove receptors from the pool, so the curve shifts right and the Emax falls."},{"t":"D","correct":false,"why":"D keeps its maximum, which is the competitive (reversible) antagonist; norepinephrine cannot outcompete a drug that never comes off."},{"t":"None of the above","correct":false,"why":"C shows the irreversible antagonist’s two changes: shift to the right and a lower Emax."}],"teach":"Phenoxybenzamine forms covalent bonds that cannot be reversed under normal metabolic function, so the receptors it occupies are gone. Norepinephrine needs a higher dose for the receptors that remain (shift right) and, once too few remain, cannot reach the maximal response (Emax falls). Anything that lowers the Emax has to be an irreversible antagonist or an allosteric antagonist affecting efficacy.","quote":"If you see something that it lowers the Emacs, you know that has to be either an irreversible antagonist or an allosteric antagonist that affects efficacy, right? Those are the only two that can affect the Emacs.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~3, ~51–~55; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"After an irreversible antagonist occupies some proportion of receptors, the number of unoccupied receptors may be too low for the agonist, even at high concentrations, to produce the previous maximal response."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-038', prof:'Gottlieb', "dupOf":"gtap-L04-018","fg":"potency","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-allo","skill":"figure","concept":"allosteric-agonist-affinity-only","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"If DRC C is the DRC of norepinephrine binding to the β1 receptor alone, which DRC best represents the DRC of norepinephrine in the presence of an allosteric agonist that affects only the affinity?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":1,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":-1,"emax":60},{"label":"C","ec":0,"emax":100},{"label":"D","ec":-1,"emax":100}],"base":0,"x":"log dose norepinephrine","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"A","correct":false,"why":"A moved to the right, so norepinephrine was made less potent; that is an allosteric antagonist affecting affinity, not an agonist."},{"t":"B","correct":false,"why":"B moved left but lost its maximum, so efficacy was affected; the stem says affinity only, and no drug raises affinity while lowering efficacy."},{"t":"C","correct":false,"why":"C is the point of reference (norepinephrine alone); an allosteric agonist changes the curve."},{"t":"D","correct":true,"why":"An allosteric agonist helps: it makes the pocket easier to bind, so norepinephrine appears more potent (shift left) and, affinity only, the Emax is untouched."},{"t":"None of the above","correct":false,"why":"D shows the affinity-only allosteric agonist: shift to the left with the same maximum."}],"teach":"Same poll, different point of reference. An allosteric agonist binds someplace else on the receptor and helps the agonist, so only curves to the left are possible. Affinity only means the ability to produce the effect is untouched: the maximum does not move. A curve whose maximum changed (B) involves efficacy; a curve to the right (A) means the agonist was made less potent.","quote":"Help, which means you’re gonna make the drug more potent, right? And it because it only affects affinity, you’re not affecting the ability to produce its effect, is moving to the left.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~26, ~42; transcript 9/28"},
{id:'gtap-L04-039', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"classes","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-comp","skill":"figure","concept":"competitive-antagonist-neutral-alone","tags":["poll","metoprolol"],"source":"both","stem":"The DRCs below represent the individual DRCs of five different drugs given on their own to a system in which half the receptors are active. DRC C most likely represents the DRC of:","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":-1,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":0,"emax":0},{"label":"C","ec":0,"emax":50},{"label":"D","ec":0.4,"emax":100},{"label":"E","ec":0.6,"emax":80}],"base":50,"x":"log dose","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"Loratadine","correct":false,"why":"Loratadine is the inverse agonist; alone it brings a raised baseline down to 0%, which is curve B, not C."},{"t":"Metoprolol","correct":true,"why":"A flat line at the baseline is a drug with affinity but no efficacy: the neutral competitive antagonist. Metoprolol is the competitive antagonist on the list."},{"t":"Epinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Epinephrine is a full agonist; alone it rises from the baseline to 100%."},{"t":"Albuterol","correct":false,"why":"Albuterol is a partial agonist; alone it goes up or down from the 50% baseline to its own efficacy. (A partial agonist with exactly 50% efficacy would also sit flat, but with no more information a flat line is read as the competitive antagonist.)"}],"teach":"Two steps: classify curve C, then match the class to the drug list. A competitive antagonist has equal affinity for active and inactive receptors and does not evoke a change, so alone it leaves the balance where it found it: 50 stays 50. Full and partial agonists go up from the baseline; an inverse agonist goes down to 0%. Metoprolol is the competitive antagonist.","quote":"they don’t care what the receptor is, active or inactive, they hang out with both types. They don’t discriminate as such, they don’t change the balance of the receptor activity.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~24, ~43–~44; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"Receptor antagonists bind to receptors but do not activate them; their primary action is to reduce the effects of agonists that normally activate the receptor."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-040', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"partial","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-recap","skill":"figure","concept":"partial-agonist-table","tags":["poll","albuterol"],"source":"both","stem":"The DRCs below represent the individual DRCs of five different drugs. DRC B most likely represents the DRC of:","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":0,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":0.3,"emax":60},{"label":"C","ec":-1,"emax":100},{"label":"D","ec":0,"emax":0},{"label":"E","ec":1,"emax":100}],"base":0,"x":"log dose","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"Norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Norepinephrine is a full agonist and would reach 100%, like A, C or E."},{"t":"Loratadine","correct":false,"why":"Loratadine is the inverse agonist; with the baseline already at 0% it gives a flat line at zero, like D."},{"t":"Albuterol","correct":true,"why":"A curve that rises but plateaus between 1% and 99% is a partial agonist, and albuterol is the partial agonist on the drug list."},{"t":"Metoprolol","correct":false,"why":"Metoprolol has no efficacy; alone it gives a flat line at the baseline, like D."},{"t":"Epinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Epinephrine is a full agonist and would reach 100%."}],"teach":"Classify first, then match. Curve B produces a positive effect but plateaus at 60%, which makes it a partial agonist (any maximum from 1% to 99%). On the drug list the partial agonist is albuterol, which opens the airways just enough for an asthma patient without opening them 100%. Full agonists reach 100%; drugs with no positive efficacy stay flat at a 0% baseline.","quote":"If your patient has asthma, albuterol, which is a partial agonist, is going to open up your airways just enough for you to breathe better in and out. You don’t have to open your lungs 100%.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~3, ~24; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Partial Agonists","t":"Partial agonists produce a lower response at full receptor occupancy than full agonists do."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-041', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"irreversible","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-irrev","skill":"figure","concept":"irreversible-antagonist-curve","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"The dotted line represents the DRC of the agonist alone and the solid lines represent the DRC of the agonist in the presence of increasing doses of drug \"X.\" Drug X is most likely:","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"alone","ec":-1.5,"emax":100,"dashed":true},{"label":"X","ec":-0.5,"emax":100},{"label":"10X","ec":0.4,"emax":65},{"label":"100X","ec":1,"emax":25}],"base":0,"x":"log dose agonist","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"A full agonist","correct":false,"why":"A full agonist would help and move the curve to the left."},{"t":"A partial agonist","correct":false,"why":"A partial agonist has efficacy and would raise the baseline; here the baseline never moves."},{"t":"A competitive (reversible) antagonist","correct":false,"why":"A competitive antagonist shifts right but is reversible, so the Emax would be unchanged; here the maximum keeps falling."},{"t":"An irreversible antagonist","correct":true,"why":"Shift to the right, no change to the baseline and an Emax that falls with each dose: fewer receptors remain, so the agonist cannot outcompete it."},{"t":"An allosteric agonist","correct":false,"why":"An allosteric agonist would help the agonist and shift it to the left."}],"teach":"Process of elimination. Helpers (full agonist, allosteric agonist) move the curve left, so they are out. A partial agonist would change the baseline. That leaves the two antagonists that shift right; only one lowers the Emax. Anything that lowers the Emax has to be an irreversible antagonist or an allosteric antagonist affecting efficacy, and only the irreversible one can abolish the response completely.","quote":"What could this be, uh, irreversible? It’s not changing, right? The Emacs is staying the same, so the best answer here is what? ... If you see something that it lowers the Emacs, you know that has to be either an irreversible antagonist or an allosteric antagonist that affects efficacy.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~53–~55; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"An irreversible antagonist reduces the maximal effect the agonist can achieve, and high agonist concentrations cannot overcome it."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-042', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"irreversible","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-irrev","skill":"figure","concept":"drug-x-phenoxybenzamine","tags":["poll","phenoxybenzamine"],"source":"both","stem":"The dotted line represents the DRC of norepinephrine alone and the solid lines represent the DRC of norepinephrine in the presence of increasing doses of drug \"X.\" Drug X is most likely:","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"NE","ec":-1.5,"emax":100,"dashed":true},{"label":"X","ec":-0.5,"emax":100},{"label":"10X","ec":0.4,"emax":65},{"label":"100X","ec":1,"emax":25}],"base":0,"x":"log dose norepinephrine","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"Epinephrine (adrenaline)","correct":false,"why":"Epinephrine is a full agonist and would shift norepinephrine to the left."},{"t":"Albuterol","correct":false,"why":"Albuterol is the partial agonist; it has efficacy and would change the baseline."},{"t":"Phenoxybenzamine","correct":true,"why":"The figure shows an irreversible antagonist (shift right, falling Emax), and phenoxybenzamine is the only irreversible antagonist on the drug list."},{"t":"Metoprolol","correct":false,"why":"Metoprolol is a competitive, reversible antagonist: it would shift the curve right without lowering the Emax."},{"t":"Diazepam","correct":false,"why":"Diazepam is the allosteric agonist on the list and would help norepinephrine, shifting it left."}],"teach":"Same figure, now with drug names. First read the class: right shift with a falling Emax is an irreversible antagonist. Then match the list: epinephrine is a full agonist, albuterol the partial agonist, metoprolol the competitive reversible surmountable antagonist, diazepam the allosteric agonist, and phenoxybenzamine the only irreversible antagonist on the list.","quote":"Fenoxbenzammine is the only irreversible that is on your list, and diazepam is your allosteric agonist.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~53–~55; transcript 9/28"},
{id:'gtap-L04-043', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"drc-basic","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-recap","skill":"figure","concept":"fa-plus-fa","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"The dotted line represents the DRC of norepinephrine alone and the solid lines represent the DRC of norepinephrine in the presence of increasing doses of drug \"X.\" Drug X is most likely:","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"NE","ec":0,"emax":100,"dashed":true},{"label":"X","ec":-0.7,"emax":100,"base":20},{"label":"10X","ec":-1.4,"emax":100,"base":45},{"label":"100X","ec":-2.1,"emax":100,"base":70}],"base":0,"x":"log dose norepinephrine","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"Epinephrine","correct":true,"why":"Two full agonists at the same receptor help each other: each dose of epinephrine activates receptors on norepinephrine’s behalf, raising the baseline and shifting the curve left with the same Emax."},{"t":"Albuterol","correct":false,"why":"Albuterol is a partial agonist: it would raise the baseline only to its own efficacy and, competing for the pocket, make norepinephrine’s life harder rather than easier; a baseline climbing toward 100% with a left shift is two full agonists."},{"t":"Phenoxybenzamine","correct":false,"why":"Phenoxybenzamine is the irreversible antagonist; it would shift the curve right and lower the Emax."},{"t":"Metoprolol","correct":false,"why":"Metoprolol competes for the receptor and would shift the curve to the right with no change to the baseline."},{"t":"Loratadine","correct":false,"why":"Loratadine is the inverse agonist; it would shift the curve right and bring the baseline down, not up."}],"teach":"A drug that shifts the agonist to the left is a helper. Because drug X has positive efficacy of its own, each pretreatment dose also raises the starting point, and because both drugs are full agonists at the same receptor every curve still reaches 100%. On the drug list the full agonist is epinephrine: norepinephrine makes epinephrine more potent and epinephrine makes norepinephrine more potent.","quote":"now norepinephrine still goes to 100%, but it’s further to the left and the baseline has going up, right? Because levofed has efficacy, positive efficacy.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~3, ~55; Pharmacodynamics-Day_3_2026s.pdf slides ~42–~49; transcript 9/28"},
{id:'gtap-L04-044', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"inverse","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-ia","skill":"figure","concept":"fa-plus-inverse-agonist","tags":["poll","histamine","loratadine"],"source":"both","stem":"The dotted line represents the DRC of histamine alone in a system in which half the receptors are active, and the solid lines represent the DRC of histamine in the presence of increasing doses of drug \"X.\" Drug X is most likely:","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"H","ec":-0.5,"emax":100,"dashed":true},{"label":"X","ec":0.3,"emax":100,"base":25},{"label":"10X","ec":1,"emax":100,"base":8},{"label":"100X","ec":1.7,"emax":100,"base":1}],"base":50,"x":"log dose histamine","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"Epinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Epinephrine is a full agonist; a helper would raise the baseline and shift the curve left."},{"t":"Albuterol","correct":false,"why":"Albuterol is a partial agonist with positive efficacy; it cannot bring a baseline down toward 0%."},{"t":"Loratadine","correct":true,"why":"Baseline falling toward 0%, shift to the right and no change to the Emax: an inverse agonist that histamine can still outcompete. Loratadine is the inverse agonist on the list."},{"t":"Metoprolol","correct":false,"why":"A competitive antagonist would shift the curve right but has no efficacy, so the baseline would not move."},{"t":"Phenoxybenzamine","correct":false,"why":"The irreversible antagonist leaves the baseline alone and lowers the Emax; here the Emax is unchanged."}],"teach":"Read the three changes: baseline down (negative efficacy shuts off receptors that were active on their own), shift to the right (histamine must outcompete the drug, so it appears less potent) and no change to the Emax (the drug is reversible). Only an inverse agonist lowers the baseline, and the one inverse agonist to know is loratadine, an antihistamine.","quote":"So, take home message over here, it’s gonna bring the baseline down to 0, and it’s gonna shift to the right with no change to the Emax, right?","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~13–~24, ~55; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"Some antagonists show inverse agonist activity: they reduce receptor activity below the basal level observed in the absence of any agonist."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-045', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-allo","skill":"figure","concept":"allosteric-characteristics","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"The dotted line represents the DRC of the agonist alone and the solid lines represent the DRC of the agonist in the presence of increasing doses of drug \"X\" (1×, 10× and 100×). Drug X is most likely:","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"alone","ec":0.6,"emax":100,"dashed":true},{"label":"1×","ec":-0.3,"emax":100},{"label":"10×","ec":-1.1,"emax":100},{"label":"100×","ec":-1.7,"emax":100}],"base":0,"x":"log dose agonist","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"A full agonist","correct":false,"why":"A second full agonist also shifts left, but as an orthosteric drug its shifts would be symmetrical and keep going; here the shifts are unequal and stop growing, which is allosteric."},{"t":"A partial agonist","correct":false,"why":"A partial agonist has efficacy and would change the baseline."},{"t":"A competitive antagonist","correct":false,"why":"A competitive antagonist makes the agonist less potent: shift to the right, in equal (symmetrical) steps."},{"t":"An allosteric agonist","correct":true,"why":"Shift to the left with the same Emax, shifts of unequal size that stop growing at high doses (saturable) and no change to the baseline: the drug binds someplace else and only raises the agonist’s affinity."},{"t":"An inverse agonist","correct":false,"why":"An inverse agonist makes the agonist’s life harder: shift to the right and, with active receptors present, a lower baseline."}],"teach":"The curve moved left, so drug X helps. Then look at the shifts: they are asymmetrical (unequal sizes) and saturable (the 100× dose barely moves the curve beyond the 10× dose), which is how an allosteric drug is recognized on a figure; an orthosteric competitor keeps shifting in equal steps. Emax is unchanged because a full agonist is already at 100%, so only affinity can be seen.","quote":"I can keep giving more and more of my allosteric, and he can’t go more to the left. He has maxed out all that he can do to the system.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~30–~34, ~55; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"Allosteric modulators bind a site on the receptor separate from the orthosteric site bound by the agonist, so they modify receptor activity without blocking agonist binding."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-046', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"two-state","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-recap","skill":"recall","concept":"full-vs-partial-agonist-summary","tags":["poll"],"source":"transcript","stem":"Which of the following is CORRECT about a full agonist?","options":[{"t":"Its Emax is 100% of the system’s response","correct":false,"why":"True, but not the only true statement; a full agonist gets all the way up to the maximal response."},{"t":"It has the highest affinity for receptors in the active state","correct":false,"why":"True, but not the only true statement; full agonists love receptors that are active."},{"t":"If it binds a receptor that is inactive, it can activate it","correct":false,"why":"True, but not the only true statement; that is its positive efficacy."},{"t":"It shifts the DRC of another full agonist to the left","correct":false,"why":"True, but not the only true statement; two full agonists help each other."},{"t":"All of the above","correct":true,"why":"Every statement is correct: Emax of 100%, highest affinity for active receptors, positive efficacy on inactive receptors, and a left shift of another full agonist’s curve."}],"teach":"Four facts that all hold for a full agonist: its Emax is 100% (it gets all the way up to the response); it has the highest affinity for receptors in the active state; if it binds a receptor that is inactive, it can activate it (positive efficacy); and it shifts the dose–response curve of another full agonist to the left because the two help each other. When every statement is right, the answer is all of the above.","quote":"I have all the above. Is that the correct answer? Yes, right. It’s Emacs is 100%. It gets all the way up to the response. It has the highest affinity for receptors on the active state. However, if you bind into receptors that are inactive, it can activate them, right? And a full agonist will shift the dose response curve on another full agonist to the left because they’re gonna be helping each other.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_3_2026s.pdf slides ~33–~49 (lectured 9/28); transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Receptor-Effector Coupling & Spare Receptors","t":"Full agonists shift the conformational equilibrium of receptors more strongly than partial agonists do."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-047', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"competitive","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-comp","skill":"recall","concept":"competitive-antagonist-shift","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"Which statement is INCORRECT about a competitive antagonist?","options":[{"t":"It has equal affinity for receptors in the active and inactive states","correct":false,"why":"This is correct: the competitive antagonist is neutral (R = affinity = R*)."},{"t":"It has affinity but no pharmacological efficacy","correct":false,"why":"This is correct: it binds but does not evoke a change in the receptor."},{"t":"It shifts a full agonist’s DRC to the right in symmetrical steps","correct":false,"why":"This is correct: a tenfold increase in antagonist needs a tenfold increase in agonist."},{"t":"It lowers the Emax of a full agonist","correct":true,"why":"This is the incorrect statement: a competitive antagonist is reversible, so enough agonist outcompetes it and the Emax is unchanged. Only an irreversible antagonist or an allosteric antagonist affecting efficacy lowers the Emax."}],"teach":"The competitive antagonist is neutral in the two-state model (equal affinity for both states, affinity but no efficacy) and reversible. In the presence of a full agonist it shifts the curve to the right in symmetrical steps with no change to the baseline and no change to the Emax, because the agonist can always outcompete it. A lower Emax points to an irreversible antagonist or an allosteric antagonist affecting efficacy.","quote":"Everybody else is competitive. Everybody else is reversible, no change to the Emacs.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~43–~50; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"Sufficiently high concentrations of agonist can surmount the effect of a given concentration of a competitive antagonist, whereas agonists cannot surmount a noncompetitive antagonist irrespective of their concentration."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-048', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-allo","skill":"recall","concept":"noncompetitive-two-reasons","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"An allosteric antagonist binds irreversibly to the receptor. True / False","options":[{"t":"True","correct":false,"why":"The allosteric antagonist binds reversibly, at another site in the receptor; it is called non-competitive because it binds someplace else, not because it never comes off."},{"t":"False","correct":true,"why":"An allosteric antagonist binds reversibly to the receptor at another site. Only the irreversible antagonist forms covalent bonds; all allosterics are reversible."}],"teach":"Two different reasons give the same non-competitive label. The irreversible antagonist forms covalent bonds in the orthosteric pocket and never comes off. The allosteric antagonist binds reversibly at another site in the receptor, so the agonist cannot overcome it by competition, the shifts are asymmetrical, and there is no irreversible binding: every allosteric drug is reversible.","quote":"For a purpose in this class, all allosterics are reversible. There is no that forms irreversible binding.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~36, ~38–~41; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"Once a receptor is bound by a noncompetitive antagonist, agonists cannot surmount the inhibition; some noncompetitive antagonists bind irreversibly, others bind reversibly at a separate allosteric site."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-049', prof:'Gottlieb', "dupOf":"gtap-L04-034","fg":"spare","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-irrev","skill":"apply","concept":"spare-receptors-irreversible","tags":[],"source":"transcript","stem":"An agonist has to occupy 10% of a tissue’s receptors to produce its maximal response. A dose of an irreversible antagonist that blocks 75% of the receptors is given. What effect would that have on the Emax of the agonist?","options":[{"t":"Would lower it","correct":false,"why":"Emax only falls once fewer receptors remain than the agonist needs; 25% are still available and the agonist needs 10%."},{"t":"Would increase it","correct":false,"why":"An irreversible antagonist has no efficacy and removes receptors; it can never raise the maximal response."},{"t":"No effect","correct":true,"why":"25% of the receptors remain, more than the 10% required, so the maximal response is still reached; only a higher agonist dose is needed (shift to the right)."}],"teach":"Agonists do not have to occupy 100% of the receptors to produce a response; the rest are spare receptors. If the agonist needs 10% and the irreversible antagonist leaves 25% available, the maximum is still reached with more agonist (the curve only shifts right). Once fewer than 10% remain the Emax falls, all the way to zero if every receptor is blocked.","quote":"if I give a dose of the irreversible that blocks 75% of the receptors, but I still have 25 available, can I still make the max response? Yes, because I want to require what? 10.","cite":"transcript 9/28; Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~54, ~56","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Receptor-Effector Coupling & Spare Receptors","t":"With spare receptors, a lower dose of irreversible antagonist can leave enough receptors for the maximal response, though a higher agonist concentration is required."}]},
{id:'gtap-L04-050', prof:'Gottlieb', "fg":"efficacy","lecture":"gtap-L04","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L04","sub":"gtap-allo","skill":"figure","concept":"allosteric-antagonist-effects","tags":["poll"],"source":"both","stem":"If DRC D is the DRC of an agonist alone, which curve would best represent agonist D in the presence of an allosteric antagonist that affects only the affinity of D?","graph":{"curves":[{"label":"A","ec":-1,"emax":100},{"label":"B","ec":1.1,"emax":55},{"label":"C","ec":1.2,"emax":100},{"label":"D","ec":0,"emax":100},{"label":"E","ec":0,"emax":55}],"base":0,"x":"log dose","y":"% of maximal response"},"options":[{"t":"A","correct":false,"why":"A moved to the left, which means the agonist was helped; an antagonist makes its life more difficult."},{"t":"B","correct":false,"why":"B moved right and lost its maximum, so both affinity and efficacy were affected; the stem says affinity only."},{"t":"C","correct":true,"why":"Affinity only: the agonist appears less potent (shift to the right) but can still reach the maximal response at higher doses."},{"t":"D","correct":false,"why":"D is the point of reference; an allosteric antagonist changes the curve."},{"t":"E","correct":false,"why":"E did not move but lost its maximum, which is an allosteric antagonist affecting efficacy only."}],"teach":"Whatever the allosteric agonist does, the allosteric antagonist does the opposite. Affinity only: shift to the right, Emax still reached at higher doses. Efficacy only: no shift, the maximum falls (a full agonist behaves like a partial agonist). Both: right and down. Since the stem says affinity only, any curve whose maximum changed is out, and a curve to the left was helped.","quote":"what would happen to the dose response curve of this agonist in the presence of an allosteric antagonist that only affects affinity? What would you predict to do? It’s gonna shift to the right. Without affecting the ability to get the max response.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~38–~42; transcript 9/28","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 2","sec":"Competitive & Irreversible Antagonists","t":"Negative allosteric modulators bind an allosteric site to reduce receptor activity; positive allosteric modulators bind an allosteric site to increase it."}]},
{id:'gtap-L10-001', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-rec","skill":"recall","concept":"adrenergic-g-proteins","tags":["alpha"],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following statements about the adrenergic receptors and their G proteins is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"α2 is coupled to Gi","correct":true,"why":"α2 is coupled to Gi: inhibition of adenylyl cyclase (AC) and less cyclic AMP (cAMP)."},{"t":"α1 is coupled to Gs","correct":false,"why":"α1 is coupled to Gq (phospholipase C, IP3, Ca++), not Gs."},{"t":"β1 is coupled to Gi","correct":false,"why":"All β receptors are Gs; β1 activates AC and raises cAMP."},{"t":"β2 is coupled to Gq","correct":false,"why":"β2 is Gs (activation of AC, more cAMP)."},{"t":"β3 is coupled to Gi","correct":false,"why":"β3 is also Gs: it increases AC and cAMP."}],"teach":"Adrenergic receptors and their G proteins: α1 → Gq → phospholipase C (PLC) → IP3; α2 → Gi → inhibition of adenylyl cyclase (AC) → less cyclic AMP (cAMP); β1, β2 and β3 → Gs → activation of AC → more cAMP. All the β receptors are Gs, so they act in the same direction on the second messenger.","quote":"We have alpha 1s, alpha 2s, beta 1s, beta 2s, and beta 3s. The good news is all betas are GS, so you kind of know what they're going to do ... alpha 2s are GI, and alpha 1s are GQ.","note":"In the same sentence he also said “alpha 2s are GQ” before correcting himself to “alpha 2s are GI”; the slide table gives α2 = Gi. Keyed to the slide.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 3; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-002', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-rec","skill":"tell","concept":"alpha2-like-m2-alpha1-like-m3","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which adrenergic receptor uses the same G protein and second-messenger change as the muscarinic M2 receptor?","options":[{"t":"α2","correct":true,"why":"M2 and α2 are both Gi: inhibition of adenylyl cyclase and less cAMP."},{"t":"α1","correct":false,"why":"α1 is Gq, like M3 (PLC, IP3, ↑Ca++)."},{"t":"β1","correct":false,"why":"β1 is Gs, which raises cAMP, the opposite of M2."},{"t":"β2","correct":false,"why":"β2 is Gs and raises cAMP."},{"t":"β3","correct":false,"why":"β3 is Gs and raises cAMP."}],"teach":"The adrenergic receptors reuse the muscarinic second-messenger patterns. α2, like M2, is Gi (inhibition of adenylyl cyclase, less cyclic AMP). α1, like M3, is Gq (phospholipase C, IP3, more Ca++). They differ in where in the body they are located.","quote":"So if you know what M2 does, you know what alpha-2 is gonna do. If you know what M3 does, alpha-1 is gonna do a similar process. They're just located in different parts of our body","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 3; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-003', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-rec","skill":"recall","concept":"beta3-bladder","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Where do β3 receptors have their greatest effect?","options":[{"t":"Bladder","correct":true,"why":"β3 (Gs, more cAMP) has its primary location, where it has its greatest effect, in the bladder, which it relaxes."},{"t":"Heart","correct":false,"why":"The heart is run by β1."},{"t":"Resistance arteries","correct":false,"why":"Arteries and veins are run by α1 (with β2 causing vasodilation)."},{"t":"Brain","correct":false,"why":"In the brain the key adrenergic receptor is α2 (Gi), which suppresses sympathetic outflow."},{"t":"Salivary glands","correct":false,"why":"No β3 site was given for the glands."}],"teach":"β3 is coupled to Gs like β1 and β2: it increases adenylyl cyclase (AC) and cyclic AMP (cAMP). Its primary site, where it has the greatest effect, is the bladder, which lets drugs act there with fewer side effects.","quote":"The one thing that you need to remember about beta 3 is their primary location where they have the greatest effect is gonna be in the bladder, OK?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 3; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-004', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-rec","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"beta-gs-camp-raising","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Activation of which adrenergic receptors increases cyclic AMP (cAMP)? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"β1","correct":true,"why":"β1 → Gs → activation of adenylyl cyclase (AC) → more cAMP."},{"t":"β2","correct":true,"why":"β2 → Gs → activation of AC → more cAMP."},{"t":"β3","correct":true,"why":"β3 → Gs → ↑AC → ↑cAMP."},{"t":"α1","correct":false,"why":"α1 → Gq → phospholipase C → IP3 and Ca++; not cAMP."},{"t":"α2","correct":false,"why":"α2 → Gi → inhibition of AC → cAMP goes down."}],"teach":"Only the β receptors raise cAMP: all three are Gs and activate adenylyl cyclase (AC). α2 does the opposite (Gi, less cAMP), and α1 works through Gq, phospholipase C, IP3 and calcium.","quote":"They're gonna increase the GS activity, they're gonna increase AC which is gonna increase camp.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 3; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-005', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-map","skill":"tell","concept":"preload-vs-afterload","tags":["in-class","alpha1"],"source":"both","stem":"Which statement about preload and afterload is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"Preload is the blood in the ventricle at end diastole","correct":true,"why":"Preload = end-diastolic volume (diastolic pressure): how much blood is in the relaxed ventricle, ready to be pumped."},{"t":"Afterload is the blood returning to the heart","correct":false,"why":"Blood returning to the heart is preload (venous return)."},{"t":"Preload is the pressure needed to open the aortic valve","correct":false,"why":"The pressure the heart must overcome to open the valve is afterload (systolic pressure)."},{"t":"Afterload is set mainly by the veins","correct":false,"why":"Afterload is set by the resistance arteries; the veins set preload."},{"t":"Preload and afterload both mean heart rate","correct":false,"why":"Heart rate and contractility are the factors within the heart; preload and afterload are outside it."}],"teach":"Preload (diastolic pressure, end-diastolic volume) is the blood in the ventricle at the end of diastole, set by venous return. Afterload (systolic pressure) is the force the ventricle must overcome to open the valve and push blood out, set by the resistance arteries. Heart rate and contractility are the factors within the heart.","quote":"So preload is how much blood is in the ventricle at the top of diastole when your ventricle is the most relaxed, right? Another word for that is called end diastolic volume or diastolic pressure. ... So afterload is the same thing as systolic pressure, right? What is the pressure in your resistant arteries?","note":"He asked the class “So what is preload?” and “What about afterload?”; a student answered “The resistance of the vessels” for afterload.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 4, 6; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-006', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-map","skill":"apply","concept":"afterload-cardiac-output","tags":["alpha1"],"source":"both","stem":"With nothing else changed, an increase in afterload is most likely to do which of the following?","options":[{"t":"Decrease stroke volume","correct":true,"why":"More of the heart's work goes into opening the valve, leaving less to push blood out: stroke volume and cardiac output fall."},{"t":"Increase stroke volume","correct":false,"why":"A higher afterload means the heart spends more of its work opening the aortic valve, so less blood is ejected per beat; stroke volume rises only when afterload falls (a vasodilator)."},{"t":"Decrease preload directly","correct":false,"why":"A fall in stroke volume from higher afterload is compensated by an increase in preload, not a decrease."},{"t":"Leave cardiac output unchanged","correct":false,"why":"Afterload affects how much the heart can eject, so cardiac output changes."}],"teach":"Afterload is the pressure the ventricle must overcome to open the valve. The higher it is, the more work the heart does just to open the valve and the less blood it squeezes out, so stroke volume and cardiac output fall; this must be compensated by an increase in preload. A vasodilator lowers afterload and frees the heart to eject more.","quote":"The higher the afterload is, the work, the more work my heart has to do to open that valve and squeeze it out. So that's going to decrease my cardiac output. ... At the same token, if we give vasodilators ... now I have to work less to open the valve and I have more energy to squeeze the blood out, thus increasing the cardiac output.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 6; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-007', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-map","skill":"tell","concept":"cv-receptor-roles","tags":["beta1"],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following statements about the adrenergic receptors of the cardiovascular system is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"β1 sets heart rate and contractility","correct":true,"why":"Within the heart, β1 (Gs, more cAMP) controls how fast it beats and how strongly it contracts."},{"t":"α1 in the veins sets afterload","correct":false,"why":"α1 in the veins sets preload (venous return); afterload is α1 in the resistance arteries."},{"t":"α1 in the resistance arteries sets preload","correct":false,"why":"α1 in the resistance arteries sets afterload."},{"t":"α2 in the brain raises sympathetic outflow","correct":false,"why":"α2 is Gi and inhibitory; in the brain it suppresses the sympathetic system."},{"t":"β2 causes vasoconstriction","correct":false,"why":"β2 causes vasodilation, a check on α1 vasoconstriction."}],"teach":"The cardiovascular map: α1 in the veins (preload) and in the resistance arteries (afterload), both directly innervated by the sympathetic system; β1 in the heart (heart rate, contractility, rate of relaxation) and kidney (renin); β2 causes vasodilation to counter constriction; α2 in the brain and on nerves suppresses sympathetic outflow. Placing a receptor on the mean arterial pressure equation predicts what a drug acting there will do.","quote":"So in the heart, within the heart, the beta ones run the show. Those are the receptors that are going to affect how fast your heart is beating and how strongly it is contracting in the arteries. It's going to be our alpha 1 adrenergic receptors.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 4–5; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-008', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-map","skill":"apply","concept":"venous-alpha1-preload","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Sympathetic activity to the α1 receptors in the veins increases. Which change is most likely?","options":[{"t":"Preload rises","correct":true,"why":"Squeezing the veins (Gq, ↑Ca++) sends more blood back to the heart: more venous return, more preload."},{"t":"Preload falls","correct":false,"why":"Relaxed veins pool blood in the feet and lower preload; α1 activation does the opposite."},{"t":"Heart rate falls through β1","correct":false,"why":"Venous α1 does not act on β1; the question is about venous tone."},{"t":"Veins dilate through Gi","correct":false,"why":"α1 is Gq: more calcium and constriction, not Gi."}],"teach":"Venous return depends on venous tone. Norepinephrine released by sympathetic nerves activates α1 (Gq → ↑Ca++ → constriction) in the veins, squeezing blood back to the heart and raising preload. When the veins relax, blood stays in the feet and preload falls.","quote":"So, as I squeeze the veins, depending on how much tone of my sympathetics are activating those alpha-1 in the veins, that's gonna determine how much blood returns to the heart and how much blood comes out of the heart.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 5; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-009', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-map","skill":"recall","concept":"beta1-kidney-renin","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Activation of β1 receptors in the kidney mainly increases the production of which substance?","options":[{"t":"Renin","correct":true,"why":"Renal β1 increases renin, which leads to more angiotensin II, a vasoconstrictor."},{"t":"Nitric oxide","correct":false,"why":"Nitric oxide comes from endothelial M3 activation, not renal β1."},{"t":"Acetylcholine","correct":false,"why":"Acetylcholine is released by cholinergic nerves, not made by renal β1."},{"t":"Norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Norepinephrine is released by sympathetic nerves; β1 is the receptor it acts on."}],"teach":"The kidney is the long-term regulator of blood pressure. β1 receptors there increase renin production, which makes more angiotensin II, a vasoconstrictor that raises blood pressure. Blocking renal β1 therefore lowers renin release.","quote":"And finally, we have a long-term component over here that is involved in the long-term regulation of blood pressure, and that's gonna be the kidneys. OK, and in the kidneys, the beta ones also can affect its function. Primarily by increasing the production of what? Ren, which it makes more gen testing 2, which is a vasoconstrictor, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 5; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-010', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-map","skill":"recall","concept":"map-equation","tags":["exam-cue","alpha1"],"source":"both","stem":"Which equation gives mean arterial pressure (MAP)?","options":[{"t":"MAP = CO × TPR","correct":true,"why":"Mean arterial pressure equals cardiac output (CO) times total peripheral resistance (TPR), and CO = heart rate (HR) × stroke volume (SV)."},{"t":"MAP = CO + TPR","correct":false,"why":"The two terms multiply, not add."},{"t":"MAP = CO ÷ TPR","correct":false,"why":"Raising resistance raises pressure; it does not divide it."},{"t":"MAP = HR × TPR","correct":false,"why":"Heart rate alone is incomplete: cardiac output is HR × stroke volume."},{"t":"MAP = SV × HR","correct":false,"why":"HR × SV is cardiac output, only one of the two terms."}],"teach":"MAP = CO (HR × SV) × TPR: mean arterial pressure equals cardiac output (heart rate × stroke volume) times total peripheral resistance. Drugs lower MAP by lowering cardiac output (less contractility and heart rate, less ventricular filling) or TPR (vasodilation of resistance vessels, less sympathetic tone).","quote":"Some drugs are going to be more selective to one component versus the other, but the goal over here is for us to be able to predict what component of the aminote pressure formula is being affected by our drugs.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 7; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-011', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-map","skill":"apply","concept":"carvedilol-map","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Carvedilol blocks β1, β2 and α1 receptors. Which describes its effect on the mean arterial pressure (MAP) equation?","options":[{"t":"It lowers both CO and TPR","correct":true,"why":"β1 block decreases the heart's effects (cardiac output, CO); α1 block in arteries and veins decreases total peripheral resistance (TPR)."},{"t":"It lowers CO only","correct":false,"why":"Its α1 block also relaxes arteries and veins, lowering TPR."},{"t":"It lowers TPR only","correct":false,"why":"Its β1 block also lowers the heart's rate and force, lowering CO."},{"t":"It raises CO and lowers TPR","correct":false,"why":"Blocking β1 lowers, not raises, cardiac output."}],"teach":"MAP = cardiac output (CO) × total peripheral resistance (TPR). Carvedilol blocks β1 (heart: less rate and contractility, lower CO) and α1 (arteries and veins: vasodilation, lower TPR), so it acts on both terms. The skill is to place each drug on the part of the equation it changes.","quote":"As such, if I block the beta ones, I can decrease the effects in the heart, and if I block the alpha ones, I can decrease the effects in the arteries and the veins, thus decreasing my total peripheral resistance. So carvedilol can do it all.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 7; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-012', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-map","skill":"apply","concept":"blood-pressure-rules","tags":["alpha1"],"source":"both","stem":"A drug lowers a patient's blood pressure. Which reflex change is most likely?","options":[{"t":"Heart rate goes up","correct":true,"why":"“Blood pressure rules”: the baroreceptors sense the fall and raise sympathetic drive, so heart rate goes up to restore mean arterial pressure."},{"t":"Heart rate goes down","correct":false,"why":"Heart rate falls when blood pressure rises, not when it falls."},{"t":"Heart rate does not change","correct":false,"why":"The body defends mean arterial pressure, so heart rate responds."},{"t":"The veins dilate further","correct":false,"why":"The compensation is more sympathetic drive, which constricts rather than dilates."}],"teach":"Mean arterial pressure is defended: when blood pressure falls, sympathetic drive and heart rate rise; when it rises, heart rate falls. This is why vasodilators such as prazosin cause reflex tachycardia.","quote":"And there's a rule that we need to follow, and the rule is blood blood pressure rules. What do I mean by that? If my blood pressure goes down, heart rate goes up. If my blood pressure goes up, heart rate goes down.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 5; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-013', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-map","skill":"recall","concept":"beta1-rate-of-relaxation","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Besides heart rate and contractility, which cardiac function does β1 activation increase?","options":[{"t":"Rate of relaxation","correct":true,"why":"β1 (Gs) increases heart rate, contractility and the rate of relaxation, so the ventricle can refill between fast beats."},{"t":"Afterload","correct":false,"why":"Afterload is set by α1 in the resistance arteries."},{"t":"Venous capacitance","correct":false,"why":"Venous tone is α1 in the veins."},{"t":"Acetylcholine release","correct":false,"why":"β1 does not control acetylcholine release."}],"teach":"β1 receptors in the heart are Gs: they increase heart rate, contractility and the rate of relaxation. Relaxation takes energy too; if the heart beats fast but does not relax fast enough, the ventricles cannot fill.","quote":"We're going to increase the heart rate. We're going to increase the contractility, and also we're going to increase the rate of relaxation. ... It's just how fast your heart has to relax to fill the blood so that it can pump again.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 5; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-014', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-ne","skill":"recall","concept":"ne-synthesis","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which sequence shows the synthesis of norepinephrine?","options":[{"t":"Tyrosine → L-Dopa → dopamine → norepinephrine","correct":true,"why":"In the sympathetic nerve terminal tyrosine is made into L-Dopa, L-Dopa into dopamine, and dopamine into norepinephrine, which is stored in vesicles."},{"t":"L-Dopa → tyrosine → dopamine → norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Tyrosine comes first; L-Dopa is made from it."},{"t":"Tyrosine → dopamine → L-Dopa → norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"L-Dopa comes before dopamine."},{"t":"Dopamine → tyrosine → L-Dopa → norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Dopamine is made from L-Dopa, which is made from tyrosine."}],"teach":"In the sympathetic nerve terminal, tyrosine → L-Dopa → dopamine → norepinephrine (NE). NE is stored in vesicles and released on an action potential, like acetylcholine in the cholinergic nerve.","quote":"So very similar to the muscarinic, uh, to the acetylcholines, norepinephrine is gonna be made by a precursor, and it's gonna be stored in a vesicle, and upon an extra potential, we're gonna have the release of norepinephrine to the synapse.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 8; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-015', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-ne","skill":"recall","concept":"presynaptic-alpha2-feedback","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"What does activation of the presynaptic α2 receptor do?","options":[{"t":"Suppresses norepinephrine release","correct":true,"why":"Presynaptic α2 (Gi) is negative feedback: activating it suppresses further release of norepinephrine."},{"t":"Increases norepinephrine release","correct":false,"why":"More release is what blocking the α2 autoreceptor gives (mirtazapine): the Gi brake on release is removed. Activating it does the opposite."},{"t":"Breaks down norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Breakdown is done by the enzymes COMT and MAO."},{"t":"Recycles norepinephrine into vesicles","correct":false,"why":"Recycling is the norepinephrine transporter (NET)."},{"t":"Contracts the effector cell","correct":false,"why":"The effector cell carries postsynaptic α1 and β receptors, not the presynaptic α2."}],"teach":"Postsynaptically, norepinephrine acts on α1 or β receptors. Presynaptically sits α2, coupled to Gi: it is negative feedback that suppresses further norepinephrine release.","quote":"Typically, on the post-synaptic side, we're gonna have either alpha one or beta ones, OK? On the pre-synaptic side, we're typically gonna have alpha-2s. Which are negative feedback. This is what's going to suppress the release of norepinephrine. This is our gatekeeper.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 8; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-016', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-ne","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"ne-removal","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which remove released norepinephrine from the synapse? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT)","correct":true,"why":"One of the two enzymes that break norepinephrine down to inactive metabolites."},{"t":"Monoamine oxidase (MAO)","correct":true,"why":"The other enzyme that breaks norepinephrine down."},{"t":"Norepinephrine transporter (NET)","correct":true,"why":"The NET takes norepinephrine back so it can be recycled into the vesicle."},{"t":"Acetylcholinesterase","correct":false,"why":"Acetylcholinesterase breaks down acetylcholine, not norepinephrine."},{"t":"Presynaptic α2 receptor","correct":false,"why":"α2 lowers release; it does not remove norepinephrine already released."}],"teach":"After release, norepinephrine is removed by two enzymes, catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) and monoamine oxidase (MAO), and, more economically, by the norepinephrine transporter (NET), which recycles it back into the vesicle. Each is a drug target: NET blockers and MAO inhibitors raise norepinephrine.","quote":"We have two enzymes, the COAMT or the MAO, right? And they're gonna cut it down, break down the norepinephrine. More economically, can we move them to the net transporter, the norepinephrine transporter, and recycle it back into the vesicle so that we don't have to make new ones.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 8; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-017', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-ne","skill":"tell","concept":"net-recycles","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following returns norepinephrine to the nerve so it can be stored in the vesicle again?","options":[{"t":"Norepinephrine transporter (NET)","correct":true,"why":"The NET moves norepinephrine back into the nerve to be recycled into the vesicle."},{"t":"Monoamine oxidase (MAO)","correct":false,"why":"MAO breaks norepinephrine down; it does not recycle it."},{"t":"Catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT)","correct":false,"why":"COMT breaks norepinephrine down to inactive metabolites."},{"t":"Presynaptic α2 receptor","correct":false,"why":"α2 suppresses release; it does not take norepinephrine up."}],"teach":"Removal routes for norepinephrine: two enzymes that destroy it (COMT, MAO) and one transporter that recycles it (NET). Cocaine blocks the NET; phenelzine and selegiline block MAO.","quote":"If we have too much of it, the enzymes come and break it. If we don't have enough, we might shunt more through the transporter pathways, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 8; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-018', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-class","skill":"tell","concept":"oxymetazoline-nonselective","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which drug is a non-selective, direct-acting α1 and α2 agonist?","options":[{"t":"Oxymetazoline","correct":true,"why":"Oxymetazoline binds and activates both α1 and α2, so it is non-selective."},{"t":"Phenylephrine","correct":false,"why":"Phenylephrine is a selective α1 agonist."},{"t":"Clonidine","correct":false,"why":"Clonidine is a selective α2 agonist."},{"t":"Cocaine","correct":false,"why":"Cocaine is indirect acting (reuptake inhibitor)."},{"t":"Selegiline","correct":false,"why":"Selegiline is indirect acting (MAO inhibitor)."}],"teach":"A selective drug binds one receptor; a drug that binds two or more is non-selective. Selective direct agonists: phenylephrine (α1), clonidine (α2). Non-selective direct agonists: oxymetazoline (α1 and α2), and epinephrine and norepinephrine (α1, α2, β1, β2).","quote":"And some of those drugs are gonna be selective, they only bind to one receptor or non-selective. They bind to one or more receptors. If you bind to two or more, you're not selective, OK?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 9, 19; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-019', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-class","skill":"recall","concept":"ephedrine-mixed","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Ephedrine is classified as mixed acting. Which describes it?","options":[{"t":"Activates α and β receptors and releases norepinephrine","correct":true,"why":"Ephedrine is an α1, α2, β1, β2 agonist and a releasing agent: direct and indirect at once."},{"t":"Blocks the norepinephrine transporter only","correct":false,"why":"Blocking the norepinephrine transporter (NET) alone is cocaine, an indirect drug that never touches the receptor; ephedrine binds the receptors itself and also pushes norepinephrine out."},{"t":"Irreversibly blocks MAO-A and MAO-B (monoamine oxidase)","correct":false,"why":"Irreversible MAO block is phenelzine, an indirect drug that stops breakdown; ephedrine neither binds the enzyme nor acts only indirectly."},{"t":"Selectively activates α1 receptors","correct":false,"why":"Selective α1 activation is phenylephrine, a purely direct agonist; ephedrine activates α and β receptors and releases norepinephrine as well."}],"teach":"Mixed-acting means both mechanisms: ephedrine binds and activates α1, α2, β1 and β2 and also increases norepinephrine release. The result is massive sympathetic activation (constricted vessels, a working heart, continuous norepinephrine), which caused strokes and heart attacks and ended its use in US dietary supplements.","quote":"You can see that this drug not only can bind and activate all the receptors but also increase the release of norepinephrine. So we have this massive activation of your sympathetic nervous system, which causes people to have a heart attack","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 9; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-020', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-class","skill":"tell","multi":true,"concept":"indirect-acting-group","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which drugs are indirect-acting adrenergic agonists? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Amphetamine","correct":true,"why":"Amphetamine pushes norepinephrine (and dopamine, serotonin) out of the terminal onto the receptors without binding them: indirect, a releasing agent."},{"t":"Cocaine","correct":true,"why":"Cocaine blocks the norepinephrine transporter (NET), so released norepinephrine stays in the synapse longer: indirect, a reuptake inhibitor."},{"t":"Phenelzine","correct":true,"why":"Phenelzine blocks monoamine oxidase (MAO) A and B irreversibly, so more norepinephrine is stored and released: indirect."},{"t":"Selegiline","correct":true,"why":"Selegiline blocks MAO-B irreversibly, so less catecholamine is broken down: indirect, acting on the enzyme and not on the receptor."},{"t":"Phenylephrine","correct":false,"why":"A direct, selective α1 agonist."},{"t":"Clonidine","correct":false,"why":"A direct, selective α2 agonist."},{"t":"Oxymetazoline","correct":false,"why":"A direct, non-selective α1 and α2 agonist."}],"teach":"Indirect-acting drugs do not bind the adrenergic receptor; they raise norepinephrine. Three groups: releasing agents (amphetamine), reuptake inhibitors (cocaine) and monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitors (phenelzine, selegiline). Direct agonists (phenylephrine, clonidine, oxymetazoline) bind the receptor themselves.","quote":"The other ones over here that are indirectly we have the amphetamine-like drugs. ... We have drugs like cocaine that are used recreationally ... And then we have some of the MAO inhibitors that by blocking the enzyme that breaks down catecholamines, we can increase the levels in the brain","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 9; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-021', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-indirect","skill":"apply","concept":"cocaine-nasal-vasoconstriction","tags":["in-class"],"source":"both","stem":"A person who snorts cocaine has repeated nosebleeds and damage to the nasal septum. Which mechanism explains it?","options":[{"t":"Excess α1 vasoconstriction","correct":true,"why":"Blocking the NET raises synaptic norepinephrine, which constricts nasal vessels through α1 so much that the mucosa dies."},{"t":"More acetylcholine on M3 → vasodilation","correct":false,"why":"Cocaine acts on norepinephrine reuptake, not acetylcholine."},{"t":"β2 activation → vasodilation","correct":false,"why":"The damage comes from too much constriction, not dilation."},{"t":"α2 activation → less norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Cocaine raises synaptic norepinephrine; it does not lower it."},{"t":"MAO block → tyramine crisis","correct":false,"why":"Monoamine oxidase (MAO) block is phenelzine and selegiline; cocaine blocks the norepinephrine transporter (NET)."}],"teach":"Cocaine is a reuptake inhibitor: it blocks the norepinephrine transporter (NET), raising synaptic norepinephrine and activating the postsynaptic receptors. In nasal blood vessels the α1 vasoconstriction is severe enough to kill the mucosa and destroy the septum, so users bleed from the nose. In the central nervous system it causes excitation; in the heart, higher heart rate and risk of a heart attack.","quote":"You're releasing norepinephrine, you're vasoconstricting those blood vessels so much that it starts to kill your mucosa of your sinus and destroy your septum and everything else in there. And that's gonna lead to bleeding on their nose. So, in the blood vessels, this drug can cause severe vasoconstriction.","note":"He asked the class “do you know why that is happening?” about the nosebleeds and answered himself.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 9","sec":"The Norepinephrine Transporter","t":"Blocking the norepinephrine transporter, for example by the non-selective psychostimulant cocaine, impairs the main route by which norepinephrine is removed from the synapse, so synaptic norepinephrine levels rise and adrenergic receptors are stimulated more."}],"cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 10; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-022', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":2,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-indirect","skill":"apply","concept":"cocaine-lidocaine-local","tags":[],"source":"transcript","lowYield":true,"stem":"Cocaine or another strong vasoconstrictor is sometimes added to lidocaine when a cut is sutured. What is the rationale?","options":[{"t":"Keeps lidocaine local","correct":true,"why":"Vasoconstriction decreases bleeding and stops lidocaine from going systemic and reaching the heart, where it can cause arrhythmias."},{"t":"Speeds lidocaine's spread to the heart","correct":false,"why":"The goal is the opposite: keep lidocaine out of the heart."},{"t":"Blocks the pain by dilating vessels","correct":false,"why":"The added drug constricts, not dilates, the vessels."},{"t":"Treats an arrhythmia caused by the cut","correct":false,"why":"The vasoconstrictor prevents lidocaine-induced arrhythmias; it does not treat one."}],"teach":"Lidocaine numbs the area. Adding a powerful vasoconstrictor (cocaine, or epinephrine/norepinephrine) constricts local vessels, which decreases bleeding during suturing and keeps lidocaine from being distributed systemically, where it could reach the heart and cause arrhythmias.","quote":"But the reason why we can add that cocaine or powerful vasoconstrictor is that we don't want it to become systemic because the lidocaine can stop your heart. So you want to stay local. So by vasoconstricting those blood vessels, you decrease the bleeding while you're suturing, and also you prevent the systemic distribution of our drug into the heart.","note":"A use, which he generally does not test (10/5: “I'm not testing you on the use”), so marked low yield. The drug list instead says cocaine is “used with lidocaine to control arrythmias (Na+ channel blocker)”; he said the vasoconstrictor keeps lidocaine away from the heart to prevent arrhythmias. Keyed to the transcript (see DL2 conflicts).","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 10; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-023', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-indirect","skill":"tell","concept":"amphetamine-primary-moa","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"What is the primary mechanism of action of dextroamphetamine and methylphenidate?","options":[{"t":"Presynaptic catecholamine release","correct":true,"why":"Primary action: release of norepinephrine, dopamine and serotonin."},{"t":"Fully block the norepinephrine transporter","correct":false,"why":"Full block of the norepinephrine transporter (NET) is cocaine’s mechanism; amphetamines leave the transporter mostly working and only moderately slow reuptake as a secondary effect."},{"t":"Irreversibly block monoamine oxidase B","correct":false,"why":"Irreversible MAO-B block is selegiline’s mechanism; amphetamines block monoamine oxidase (MAO) only moderately and as a secondary effect."},{"t":"Directly activate α1 receptors","correct":false,"why":"Direct α1 activation is phenylephrine’s mechanism; amphetamines do not typically bind and activate the receptors, they push norepinephrine out of the terminal onto them."},{"t":"Directly activate α2 receptors","correct":false,"why":"Direct α2 (Gi) activation is clonidine’s mechanism and lowers norepinephrine release; amphetamines raise it."}],"teach":"The amphetamine-like stimulants (dextroamphetamine, amphetamine, lisdexamfetamine, methylphenidate, dexmethylphenidate) work mainly by stimulating presynaptic release of norepinephrine (NE), dopamine (DA) and serotonin (5HT). Secondarily they moderately decrease reuptake and block monoamine oxidase (MAO).","quote":"So they do not work typically by binding and activating the receptors. They are indirectly enhancing the release, so they stimulate the release of catecholamine, such as norepinephrine, serotonin, and dopamine, OK?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 11; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-024', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-indirect","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"amphetamine-secondary-moa","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Besides stimulating catecholamine release, which weaker actions do the amphetamine-like stimulants have? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Decrease reuptake of norepinephrine","correct":true,"why":"Second, moderate action: decrease reuptake (weak NET inhibition)."},{"t":"Block monoamine oxidase (MAO)","correct":true,"why":"Second, moderate action: blocks MAO, the enzyme that breaks catecholamines down."},{"t":"Activate presynaptic α2","correct":false,"why":"Activating α2 would lower release; that is clonidine."},{"t":"Block postsynaptic α1","correct":false,"why":"Blocking α1 is prazosin."},{"t":"Activate COMT","correct":false,"why":"Catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) breaks norepinephrine down; activating it would lower synaptic norepinephrine, the opposite of a stimulant."}],"teach":"Primary mechanism: release of norepinephrine, dopamine and serotonin. Secondary, weak-to-moderate mechanisms: inhibition of reuptake and inhibition of monoamine oxidase (MAO). The drug touches several steps of norepinephrine handling.","quote":"You also have a secondary effect of being a very weaker inhibitor of the reuptake and the enzyme that breaks down catecholamine. So it has multiple mechanisms of action. The primary is releasing of norepinephrine with a very weak to moderate inhibition of the reuptake as well as the breakdown of catecholamines.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 11; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-025', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-indirect","skill":"apply","multi":true,"concept":"stimulant-effects","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"A patient takes increasing doses of dextroamphetamine. Which effects are expected? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Decreased appetite","correct":true,"why":"Central effect of more norepinephrine and dopamine in the brain: less sleep and less appetite."},{"t":"Tremor","correct":true,"why":"Peripheral effect of higher sympathetic outflow: activated β2 receptors cause tremor."},{"t":"Increased heart rate","correct":true,"why":"Increased sympathetic outflow raises heart rate and blood pressure."},{"t":"Sedation","correct":false,"why":"Stimulants increase energy and decrease sleep."},{"t":"Bradycardia","correct":false,"why":"More sympathetic tone raises, not lowers, heart rate."}],"teach":"Stimulants raise sympathetic outflow. Central: mood, more energy, less sleep and appetite, better focus and alertness, faster breathing. Peripheral: tremor, sweating (diaphoresis), cardiovascular effects (higher blood pressure and heart rate) and hyperthermia. Side effects grow with dose.","quote":"So these drugs are gonna increase the sympathetic outflow as a side effect. So it may increase your blood pressure, increase your, your heart rate, it cause tremors, right, because you're activating those beta 2s","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 12; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-026', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":2,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-indirect","skill":"apply","concept":"stimulant-caffeine-ddi","tags":[],"source":"transcript","stem":"A student taking a prescribed stimulant drinks several caffeinated energy shots while studying overnight. Which drug–drug interaction is most likely?","options":[{"t":"Additive stimulant effects","correct":true,"why":"Caffeine is also a stimulant: two stimulants on board add up and push the dose toward side effects such as arrhythmias."},{"t":"Caffeine cancels the stimulant","correct":false,"why":"Both drugs stimulate; their effects add."},{"t":"No interaction","correct":false,"why":"Two stimulants together have an additive effect."},{"t":"Sedation from both drugs","correct":false,"why":"Both drugs raise catecholamine signaling (the stimulant by releasing norepinephrine and dopamine, caffeine as a stimulant), so the sum is more stimulation, not sedation."}],"teach":"Caffeine is a stimulant, so combining it with an amphetamine-like drug is additive: the therapeutic window shrinks toward the side-effect end. For example, a student drinking energy shots all night before an exam on top of a stimulant ended up in the emergency room with arrhythmias.","quote":"Caffeine is a stimulant. So if you are taking a stimulant and then you add caffeine and Red Bull in 5 minutes, you know, whatever crap that is, uh, you're just compounding. ... She was in the ER because she was having arrhythmias.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 12; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-027', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-mao","skill":"tell","concept":"mao-a-vs-b-location","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which statement about the two monoamine oxidase (MAO) subtypes is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"MAO-B is mainly in the brain","correct":true,"why":"MAO-B is present in the CNS striatum and breaks down dopamine (“B for brain”)."},{"t":"MAO-A is only in the brain","correct":false,"why":"MAO-A is in the brain, liver and gut; its gut location is why non-selective inhibitors cause the tyramine reaction."},{"t":"MAO-B is mainly in the gut","correct":false,"why":"The gut enzyme is MAO-A."},{"t":"MAO-B breaks down acetylcholine","correct":false,"why":"MAO breaks down catecholamines (norepinephrine, dopamine), not acetylcholine."}],"teach":"Both monoamine oxidase (MAO) subtypes break down catecholamines. MAO-A is in the brain, liver and gastrointestinal tract and breaks down norepinephrine and dopamine; MAO-B is in the central nervous system (striatum) and breaks down dopamine. Memory aid: B for brain.","quote":"So the older drugs. They are less selective, so they can inhibit both enzymes, the MAOA that is in the gut, as well as the MAOB that is for the brain. So if you're not sure, think about B for brain","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 13; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-028', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-mao","skill":"tell","concept":"phenelzine-vs-selegiline","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following statements is CORRECT? (MAO = monoamine oxidase; COMT = catechol-O-methyltransferase)","options":[{"t":"Phenelzine blocks MAO-A and MAO-B","correct":true,"why":"Phenelzine is the non-selective MAO inhibitor: it blocks both monoamine oxidase (MAO) subtypes."},{"t":"Selegiline blocks MAO-A and MAO-B","correct":false,"why":"Selegiline is selective for MAO-B (at low doses)."},{"t":"Phenelzine blocks MAO-B only","correct":false,"why":"Phenelzine is non-selective."},{"t":"Selegiline blocks the norepinephrine transporter","correct":false,"why":"Blocking the norepinephrine transporter is cocaine."},{"t":"Phenelzine blocks COMT","correct":false,"why":"Phenelzine blocks monoamine oxidase (both subtypes), not catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT)."}],"teach":"Phenelzine: non-selective inhibitor of monoamine oxidase A and B (MAO-A and MAO-B), irreversible. Selegiline (low doses): selective MAO-B inhibitor, irreversible. Both raise catecholamines by blocking their breakdown.","quote":"There are MOA inhibitors. We have the non-selectives, and I picked phenelzine as our prototypical drug. ... And then we have a better MAO inhibitor, uh, selegiline, which is selective to the MAOB.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 14; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-029', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-mao","skill":"recall","concept":"maoi-irreversible","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Phenelzine and selegiline bind monoamine oxidase (MAO):","options":[{"t":"Irreversibly","correct":true,"why":"Both are irreversible antagonists: once bound, the enzyme is out and the body has to make new ones."},{"t":"Reversibly and competitively","correct":false,"why":"Both are irreversible: once bound, that enzyme stays blocked until the body makes new enzyme."},{"t":"As partial agonists","correct":false,"why":"They block (antagonize) the enzyme; they do not activate it."},{"t":"Phenelzine reversibly, selegiline irreversibly","correct":false,"why":"Both are irreversible."}],"teach":"Phenelzine and selegiline are both irreversible antagonists of monoamine oxidase (MAO). Once the drug binds, that enzyme molecule no longer works; activity returns only when the body makes new enzyme.","quote":"This drug is an irreversible antagonist, which means is, once you bind that enzyme, your body has to make new ones. That one is, is toasted, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 14; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-030', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":2,"ladder":"mao-inhibitor","ladderName":"MAO inhibitor, tyramine and the hypertensive crisis","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-mao","skill":"apply","concept":"cheese-effect-crisis","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"A patient taking phenelzine eats aged cheese and drinks wine at a party. Which is most likely?","options":[{"t":"Hypertensive crisis","correct":true,"why":"Non-selective MAO inhibition leaves dietary tyramine unprocessed: the “cheese effect”, a hypertensive crisis."},{"t":"Severe hypotension","correct":false,"why":"The tyramine reaction raises blood pressure."},{"t":"Bradycardia from vagal tone","correct":false,"why":"The reaction is sympathetic and hypertensive."},{"t":"No reaction","correct":false,"why":"Fermented foods are high in tyramine, which the patient cannot break down."},{"t":"Sedation","correct":false,"why":"The tyramine reaction is sympathetic: blood pressure climbs to a hypertensive crisis; it does not sedate."}],"teach":"A non-selective monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitor blocks MAO in the gut as well as the brain, so dietary tyramine from fermented foods (cheese, bread, wine) is not broken down. The result is a hypertensive crisis, the “cheese effect”. A patient on the drug who eats cheese can pass out from the very high pressure and need emergency care.","quote":"You cannot eat anything that is fermented. Like cheese and bread and wine, all of those are off the plate, right? And the reason being is because you have the inability to break down that dietary tyramine that you're consuming, and those fermented foods have high levels of tyramine, and that can cause what is called a hypertensive crisis.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 9","sec":"Specific Sympathomimetic Drugs — Tyramine","t":"In patients treated with MAO inhibitors, particularly those inhibiting the MAO-A isoform, the sympathomimetic effect of tyramine can be greatly intensified, with marked increases in blood pressure. This follows from greater bioavailability of tyramine and larger neuronal stores of catecholamines, so these patients should avoid tyramine-containing foods such as aged cheese, cured meats and pickled food."}],"cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 15; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-031', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":2,"ladder":"mao-inhibitor","ladderName":"MAO inhibitor, tyramine and the hypertensive crisis","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-mao","skill":"tell","concept":"no-cheese-effect-mao-b","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which monoamine oxidase inhibitor carries no “cheese effect” with dietary tyramine?","options":[{"t":"Selegiline","correct":true,"why":"Selective MAO-B inhibition acts in the brain only, so gut tyramine is still broken down: no cheese effect."},{"t":"Phenelzine","correct":false,"why":"Phenelzine also blocks gut MAO-A, so tyramine causes a hypertensive crisis."},{"t":"Both phenelzine and selegiline","correct":false,"why":"Only the non-selective drug causes the cheese effect."},{"t":"Neither drug","correct":false,"why":"Selegiline is the one without it."}],"teach":"No cheese effect for selective MAO-B inhibitors: selegiline blocks monoamine oxidase B in the brain and leaves gut MAO-A free to break down dietary tyramine. So, when cost is not an issue, the selective MAO-B inhibitor is preferred.","quote":"So, if we need to choose one and your patient, money is not an issue, I'd rather pick the selective MOAB because that only blocks in the brain and you don't have to worry about the cheese effect associated with your patients, OK?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 15; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-032', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"ladder":"mao-inhibitor","ladderName":"MAO inhibitor, tyramine and the hypertensive crisis","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-mao","skill":"apply","concept":"maoi-more-ne-stored","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Blocking monoamine oxidase (MAO) is most likely to increase norepinephrine release by:","options":[{"t":"Leaving more stored in the vesicles","correct":true,"why":"With less breakdown, more norepinephrine is stored, so each release puts more into the synapse."},{"t":"Blocking the α2 autoreceptor","correct":false,"why":"Blocking the α2 autoreceptor (Gi) removes the brake on release; that is mirtazapine’s mechanism, and an MAO inhibitor leaves α2 alone."},{"t":"Making the transporter pump more out","correct":false,"why":"The transporter (NET) pumps norepinephrine back in, it does not push it out; an MAO inhibitor acts on the breakdown enzyme inside the terminal and leaves the transporter alone."},{"t":"Stopping the synthesis from tyrosine","correct":false,"why":"Synthesis continues; it is breakdown that is reduced."}],"teach":"Monoamine oxidase breaks down norepinephrine, dopamine and epinephrine. Blocking it means more norepinephrine is stored in the vesicles, so more is released into the synapse. Raising brain catecholamines this way is how the MAO inhibitors, the oldest antidepressants, decrease depression.","quote":"As such, if I block the enzyme, I can make an assumption that I'm gonna have a lot more norepinephrine being stored in the vesicles, which means that when I release, I have more norepinephrine being released.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 13–14; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-033', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a1","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"alpha1-sites","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which are major sites of action of α1 receptors? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Eye (pupil)","correct":true,"why":"“I see”: α1 dilates the pupil."},{"t":"Blood vessels of the nose","correct":true,"why":"“and smell”: α1 constricts nasal vessels (decongestion)."},{"t":"Arteries and veins","correct":true,"why":"“bloody”: α1 sets afterload and preload."},{"t":"Urethra (bladder sphincter, prostate vessels)","correct":true,"why":"“urine”: α1 on the sphincter and the blood vessels around the prostate."},{"t":"Sinoatrial node","correct":false,"why":"The heart is run by β1."},{"t":"Bronchial smooth muscle","correct":false,"why":"The lungs have M3 (parasympathetic only) and β2; α1 is not a lung receptor."}],"teach":"Memory aid for α1 sites: “I see and smell bloody urine”: eye (pupil dilation), nose (nasal blood vessels), blood vessels (systemic arteries for afterload, veins for preload) and urethra (bladder sphincter and the blood vessels of the prostate). α1 is Gq: phospholipase C, IP3, more Ca++.","quote":"So, the way I remember when I was in school is I see and smell bloody urine, right? ... So think about, uh, those sites as our major sites of action.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 16–17; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-034', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a1","skill":"apply","concept":"phenylephrine-eye","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Phenylephrine drops are placed in the eye. Which effect is most likely?","options":[{"t":"Mydriasis","correct":true,"why":"α1 on the radial (dilator) fibers contracts and pulls the pupil open: pupil dilation."},{"t":"Miosis","correct":false,"why":"Miosis is the M3 effect on the circular muscle."},{"t":"Lacrimation","correct":false,"why":"Lacrimation is a muscarinic (DUMBBELSS) effect."},{"t":"No effect on the pupil","correct":false,"why":"α1 is one of the two receptors controlling pupil size."}],"teach":"The pupil has dual innervation: M3 on the circular muscle constricts it, α1 on the radial muscle dilates it. Phenylephrine, a selective α1 agonist (Gq, ↑Ca++), contracts the radial fibers and causes mydriasis (pupil dilation). Burning and blurred vision are its eye side effects.","quote":"So if activate the alpha 1 is in the eyes, they are in these transverse fibers, so we're going to pull them apart by constricting and you're going to get pupil dilation, also known as madriasis, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 18; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-035', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a1","skill":"apply","concept":"phenylephrine-preload-afterload","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Phenylephrine reaches the systemic arteries and veins. Which change is most likely?","options":[{"t":"Preload and afterload both rise","correct":true,"why":"α1 in arterial smooth muscle raises afterload; α1 in venous smooth muscle raises preload; blood pressure goes up."},{"t":"Preload and afterload both fall","correct":false,"why":"Both falling is what an α1 antagonist (prazosin) does: without Gq signaling in arteries and veins, afterload and preload drop. Phenylephrine activates α1 and raises both."},{"t":"Only preload rises","correct":false,"why":"The arteries also constrict, so afterload rises too."},{"t":"Only afterload rises","correct":false,"why":"The veins also constrict, so preload rises too."}],"teach":"Phenylephrine activates α1 (Gq, ↑Ca++) in vascular smooth muscle: arterial constriction raises afterload, venous constriction raises preload, and blood pressure rises. That is why it is used carefully in patients with hypertension; long use can itself keep blood pressure high.","quote":"So increase calcium. In the blood vessels, it's going to increase both preload and after load, so it's going to increase your blood pressure. And we're going to be careful with patients with hypertension.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 18; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-036', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a1","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"phenylephrine-adr","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which adverse effects of phenylephrine should a patient be warned about? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Burning","correct":true,"why":"Anything put in the eye or nose can burn; burning is a listed adverse reaction."},{"t":"Rebound congestion","correct":true,"why":"Used for more than 3 days, the nasal decongestant gives rebound congestion."},{"t":"Blurred vision","correct":true,"why":"Used in the eye, α1 activation dilates the pupil, and vision blurs."},{"t":"Higher blood pressure","correct":true,"why":"Careful in patients with hypertension: α1 raises preload and afterload."},{"t":"Dry mouth","correct":false,"why":"Dry mouth is the complaint with clonidine."},{"t":"Orthostatic hypotension","correct":false,"why":"That is the α1 antagonists (prazosin); phenylephrine raises pressure."}],"teach":"Phenylephrine's adverse reactions: burning, blurred vision and rebound congestion (do not use for more than 3 days), and higher blood pressure, so care is needed in patients with hypertension. Each follows from α1 activation at its sites.","quote":"And then there are some side effects associated with that, which of course if you put anything in the eye, it's going to cause some burning, blurred vision, uh, you may have rebound congestion. As such, if you read in the back of the box, it tells you you don't use for more than 3 days.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 18; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-037', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a1","skill":"tell","concept":"oxymetazoline-less-constriction","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following statements about oxymetazoline, a nasal decongestant, is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"Its α2 (Gi) action offsets part of the α1 effect","correct":true,"why":"It is non-selective: α1 (Gq) constricts, α2 (Gi) relaxes, so the two partly offset."},{"t":"It blocks α1 receptors in the nasal vessels","correct":false,"why":"Oxymetazoline activates α1 (Gq, Ca++ up, constriction); a drug that blocked α1 would dilate the nasal vessels and worsen congestion."},{"t":"It activates β2 receptors in the nasal vessels","correct":false,"why":"β2 (Gs) would relax the vessels; oxymetazoline binds α1 and α2 only."},{"t":"It acts indirectly, by releasing norepinephrine from the nerve","correct":false,"why":"It binds and activates the α receptors itself; an indirect drug (amphetamine, cocaine) would work through the nerve’s own norepinephrine."}],"teach":"Oxymetazoline is a non-selective α1 and α2 agonist acting on nasal smooth muscle. α1 (Gq) constricts and α2 (Gi) relaxes, so the two receptors “play with each other” and the vessels constrict less than with a pure α1 agonist.","quote":"This is a non-selective alpha 1, alpha 2, so they're gonna kind of, you know, play with each other. Remember, alpha 1s are Q constriction. Alpha 2 is I relaxing, right? So you don't have as much constriction of those blood vessels that you would expect.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 19; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-038', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a1","skill":"tell","concept":"alpha1-vs-alpha2-messenger","tags":["in-class"],"source":"both","stem":"Which statement about α1 and α2 activation is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"α1 raises Ca++; α2 lowers cAMP","correct":true,"why":"α1 is Gq (calcium goes up, excitation); α2 is Gi (cAMP goes down, inhibition)."},{"t":"α1 lowers cAMP; α2 raises Ca++","correct":false,"why":"This swaps the G proteins: α1 is Gq (Ca++ up, constriction), α2 is Gi (cAMP down, inhibition)."},{"t":"Both raise cAMP","correct":false,"why":"Raising cAMP is Gs, the β receptors; neither α receptor is Gs."},{"t":"Both are Gq","correct":false,"why":"α2 is coupled to Gi (cAMP down, inhibition); only α1 is Gq (Ca++ up). If both were Gq, clonidine could not lower sympathetic output."}],"teach":"A student asked why α1 and α2 agonists seem to contradict each other. They do: α1 is Gαq and excitatory (Ca++ up, constriction), α2 is Gαi and inhibitory (cyclic AMP, cAMP, down; relaxation and less norepinephrine release).","quote":"They do. Because alpha 1 agonist constrict, alpha 2 relax because one is exciting, the other one is inhibiting. Remember, alpha 2 is G alpha I, alpha 1 is G alpha Q. So calcium goes up with alpha 1s, scamp goes down with the alpha 2s.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 17, 21; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-039', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a2","skill":"recall","concept":"alpha2-location-cns","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Where are the α2 receptors that clonidine acts on primarily located?","options":[{"t":"Nerve endings in the CNS","correct":true,"why":"α2 is primarily in the central nervous system (CNS), presynaptically, modulating norepinephrine release."},{"t":"Ventricular muscle of the heart","correct":false,"why":"The heart is run by β1."},{"t":"Detrusor muscle of the bladder","correct":false,"why":"The bladder's adrenergic receptor stressed is β3."},{"t":"Bronchial smooth muscle","correct":false,"why":"The lungs have M3 and β2."},{"t":"Radial muscle of the iris","correct":false,"why":"The iris dilator is α1."}],"teach":"α2 receptors are located at nerve endings, primarily in the central nervous system (CNS), and also in the gastrointestinal tract and on pancreatic β cells. Presynaptically they modulate how much norepinephrine is released; they are Gi (less cAMP), so they are inhibitory.","quote":"So alpha chews are going to be primary locations going to be the CNS. That's what we're going to be thinking about those guys. They are going to be pre-synaptically, so they're modulating the levels of norepinephrine release.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 20–21; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-040', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a2","skill":"tell","concept":"clonidine-agonist-inhibits","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Clonidine:","options":[{"t":"Agonist at an inhibitory Gi-coupled receptor","correct":true,"why":"α2 is Gi: activating it suppresses norepinephrine release, so the sympathetic system is turned down."},{"t":"Antagonist at α1 receptors","correct":false,"why":"An α1 antagonist (prazosin) lowers pressure by removing Gq constriction in the vessels; clonidine binds α2 and activates it."},{"t":"Blocker of the norepinephrine transporter","correct":false,"why":"Blocking the norepinephrine transporter (cocaine) leaves more norepinephrine in the synapse and raises sympathetic activity, the opposite of clonidine."},{"t":"Irreversible inhibitor of monoamine oxidase (MAO)","correct":false,"why":"Irreversible MAO block (phenelzine, selegiline) lets more norepinephrine be stored and released, raising sympathetic activity; clonidine does not touch the enzyme."}],"teach":"Clonidine is a selective α2 agonist. Because α2 is coupled to Gαi, activating it is inhibitory: less cAMP, less norepinephrine release and less sympathetic outflow. It behaves like an antagonist of the sympathetic system but is an agonist at its receptor.","quote":"So clonidine is an agonist that activates the inhibitory pathway. So he's behaving like an antagonist, but he's actually an agonist.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 21, 23–24; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-041', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"ladder":"clonidine","ladderName":"Clonidine: α2, an add-on, and stopping it","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a2","skill":"apply","multi":true,"concept":"clonidine-effects","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which effects does clonidine produce? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Decreased sympathetic tone","correct":true,"why":"Central α2 (Gi) activation suppresses norepinephrine release."},{"t":"Vasodilation","correct":true,"why":"Less sympathetic input to α1 in arteries and veins: they relax (less afterload and preload)."},{"t":"Bradycardia","correct":true,"why":"Less sympathetic drive to the heart lowers heart rate and contractile force."},{"t":"Tachycardia","correct":false,"why":"Heart rate falls, not rises."},{"t":"More norepinephrine release","correct":false,"why":"More release comes from blocking the α2 autoreceptor (mirtazapine); clonidine activates it, and Gi then suppresses release."},{"t":"Higher preload","correct":false,"why":"Venous sympathetic tone falls, so preload falls."}],"teach":"Clonidine's effects are decrease of sympathetic tone, vasodilation and bradycardia. Suppressing sympathetic input to the heart lowers heart rate and force (cardiac output); to the arteries, afterload; to the veins, preload.","quote":"So, if I suppress sympathetic to the heart, I'm going to decrease the heart rate and the contractile force. If I suppress the sympathetics to the arteries, I'm going to decrease the afterload. If I suppress the sympathetics to the veins, I'm going to decrease the preload, plus everything else.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 9","sec":"Cardiovascular System — Blood Vessels","t":"Selective α2 agonists such as clonidine act in the central nervous system to reduce sympathetic activity and are therefore called central sympatholytics; they are used to treat hypertension."}],"cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 23–24; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-042', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a2","skill":"apply","concept":"clonidine-dry-mouth","tags":["in-class"],"source":"both","stem":"Which is one of the major complaints of patients taking clonidine?","options":[{"t":"Dry mouth","correct":true,"why":"Sympathetic input to the salivary glands is suppressed; about 40% of patients complain of it."},{"t":"Diarrhea","correct":false,"why":"Clonidine causes constipation (less acetylcholine release in the gut)."},{"t":"Tachycardia","correct":false,"why":"Clonidine causes bradycardia."},{"t":"Insomnia","correct":false,"why":"Clonidine causes sedation."},{"t":"Rebound nasal congestion","correct":false,"why":"Rebound congestion follows an α1 agonist decongestant (phenylephrine) used too long; clonidine does not act on the nasal α1 receptors."}],"teach":"Clonidine suppresses sympathetic outflow everywhere, including to the salivary glands, so dry mouth (“cotton mouth”) is very common, in about 40% of patients. The effect is predicted from the mechanism of action, not memorized.","quote":"Do you know what is one of the major complaints of patients taking clonidine? Dry mouth. Because you suppress the sympathetic to the salivary gland, and they're going to get dry mouth. About 40% of them complain of dry mouth.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 9","sec":"Other Organ Systems","t":"The salivary glands contain adrenoceptors that regulate secretion of amylase and water, and α2 agonists such as clonidine produce dry mouth; central nervous system effects are likely responsible for this side effect."}],"cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 26; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-043', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":3,"ladder":"clonidine","ladderName":"Clonidine: α2, an add-on, and stopping it","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a2","skill":"apply","concept":"clonidine-withdrawal-crisis","tags":["exam-cue"],"source":"both","stem":"A patient stops clonidine abruptly after months of use. Which is most likely, and why?","options":[{"t":"Hypertensive crisis from up-regulated receptors","correct":true,"why":"Chronic suppression of sympathetic tone makes the body add receptors; without the drug, the released norepinephrine acts on many more receptors."},{"t":"Hypotension from down-regulated α1 receptors","correct":false,"why":"Chronic suppression up-regulates receptors, and the result is high, not low, pressure."},{"t":"Bradycardia from extra parasympathetic tone","correct":false,"why":"Stopping the drug ends the suppression of the sympathetic system, so heart rate rises rather than falls."},{"t":"Bronchoconstriction from unopposed parasympathetic tone","correct":false,"why":"Withdrawal unleashes sympathetic activity; it does not constrict the airways."}],"teach":"Months of suppressed sympathetic tone cause receptor up-regulation. Stopping clonidine cold turkey leaves many receptors and a surge of norepinephrine: a hypertensive crisis. The major counseling point: patients must be weaned off, not stopped abruptly.","quote":"We're gonna make more receptors, right? We're gonna cause up regulation. So what is the one education point that you have to make to your patients? You got to wean yourself off because if you go cold turkey, you got all these receptors, now norepinephrine is flowing like there's no tomorrow, and you go into a hypertensive crisis, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 26; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-044', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a2","skill":"apply","concept":"clonidine-constipation","tags":["in-class"],"source":"both","stem":"Clonidine is most likely to cause constipation by:","options":[{"t":"Letting less acetylcholine reach gut M3","correct":true,"why":"α2 on the cholinergic nerve slows acetylcholine secretion, so M3 is not activated and motility and tone fall."},{"t":"Blocking gut M3 receptors","correct":false,"why":"Clonidine has no affinity for M3; it acts one step earlier, on the α2 (Gi) receptor of the parasympathetic nerve, so less acetylcholine is released onto M3."},{"t":"Activating gut α1 receptors","correct":false,"why":"Clonidine binds α2, not α1; α1 (Gq) is not the receptor that sets gut motility."},{"t":"Raising norepinephrine release in the gut wall","correct":false,"why":"Activating α2 (Gi) on the sympathetic terminal lowers norepinephrine release; the constipation comes from less acetylcholine, not more norepinephrine."}],"teach":"The gut is one of the dual-innervation exceptions: α2 receptors slow the release of acetylcholine. Activating them with clonidine means less acetylcholine, no M3 activation, less motility and tone, and constipation.","quote":"What would you predict that would happen in the gut? Well, it was kind of list their constipation, right? But why? Because you're slowing down. Because you're slowing down the secretion of acetylcholine, this is one of our dual innervation exception, right? That by suppressing acetylcholine release, we don't activate the M3s","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 26; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-045', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a2","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"clonidine-adr","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which adverse effects are expected with clonidine? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Sedation","correct":true,"why":"Central nervous system inhibition; CNS side effects are the most common."},{"t":"Hypotension","correct":true,"why":"Too much of the wanted effect; with bradycardia, it raises the risk of falls."},{"t":"Sexual dysfunction","correct":true,"why":"Sexual function needs both divisions working together; clonidine suppresses the sympathetic side."},{"t":"Nightmares and depression","correct":true,"why":"Central nervous system (CNS) inhibition also brings nightmares and depression."},{"t":"Tachycardia","correct":false,"why":"Clonidine causes bradycardia."},{"t":"Diarrhea","correct":false,"why":"Clonidine causes constipation."}],"teach":"Clonidine's adverse reactions: CNS effects (sedation, nightmares, depression), dry mouth and constipation, hypotension and bradycardia (risk of falls), sexual dysfunction, and a hypertensive crisis on abrupt withdrawal from receptor up-regulation. Each follows from suppressed sympathetic outflow; the constipation comes from less acetylcholine released onto gut M3.","quote":"So when it comes to side effects, the most common side effects with this drug can be sedation because you're inhibiting the CNS. Patients may complain of nightmares and maybe depression. Dry mouth is very common. ... so hypertension and bradycardia can be severe if the dose is too high. It may cause sexual dysfunction.","note":"The transcript has “hypertension and bradycardia” where the slide says “Hypotension & Bradycardia”; in context (“lower blood pressure, lower heart rate”) it is hypotension. Keyed to the slide.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 26; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-046', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a2","skill":"tell","concept":"idine-osin-names","tags":["alpha"],"source":"both","stem":"A drug name ends in “-idine” (for example, brimonidine or tizanidine). Which mechanism should you predict?","options":[{"t":"α2 agonist","correct":true,"why":"Most drugs ending in -idine are α2 agonists like clonidine."},{"t":"Selective α1 antagonist","correct":false,"why":"The α1 antagonists end in -osin (prazosin, terazosin)."},{"t":"Muscarinic antagonist","correct":false,"why":"Atropine-like drugs carry “trop”."},{"t":"Cholinesterase inhibitor","correct":false,"why":"Those end in -stigmine."},{"t":"α1 agonist","correct":false,"why":"Phenylephrine “sounds like” epinephrine and norepinephrine."}],"teach":"Name cues: -idine (clonidine, brimonidine, tizanidine) → α2 agonist; -osin (prazosin, terazosin, doxazosin) → selective α1 antagonist; phenylephrine sounds like epinephrine and norepinephrine → α1 agonist. Clonidine is the prototype α2 agonist.","quote":"It doesn't matter if it's clonidine or brimonidine or, you know, tizanidine. They all have this edine with it, and it's gonna tell you where they're gonna fall, OK? So for our purpose on exam one, we're gonna focus on clonidine because that's our prototypical drug.","note":"“exam one” in the quote is a slip for Exam 2, the exam this lecture belongs to.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 17, 22; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-047', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a2","skill":"apply","concept":"clonidine-everywhere","tags":["in-class"],"source":"both","stem":"If your patient on clonidine has a lower blood pressure and a dry mouth, both effects come from:","options":[{"t":"Less sympathetic outflow","correct":true,"why":"One central action reduces sympathetic input to every organ: salivary glands, heart, arteries and veins."},{"t":"M3 block in the glands and vessels","correct":false,"why":"Clonidine has no affinity for M3; the salivary glands dry because their sympathetic input (which, with the parasympathetic, drives salivation) is suppressed."},{"t":"α1 activation in the glands and vessels","correct":false,"why":"Activating α1 would constrict vessels and raise pressure."},{"t":"More norepinephrine at every synapse","correct":false,"why":"Activating the α2 (Gi) autoreceptor lowers norepinephrine release; more norepinephrine would raise pressure."}],"teach":"A student asked how clonidine can do both. Answer: the sympathetic system is one-to-many, so suppressing it centrally suppresses salivation, pupil dilation, heart rate and force, and vessel tone at once. The arteries and veins have only sympathetic innervation, so without it they relax and blood pressure falls.","quote":"Because it's suppressed sympathetic to all over the body. So remember the sympathetic is one too many, right? So by suppressing the sympathetic outflow, you're gonna suppress the salivation. ... There's only sympathetic to the arteries and the veins, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 23–24, 26; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-048', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a1ant","skill":"recall","concept":"alpha-blocker-irreversible","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which α-receptor antagonist is irreversible?","options":[{"t":"Phenoxybenzamine","correct":true,"why":"Phenoxybenzamine is the non-selective (α1 and α2) irreversible antagonist; all the others are reversible."},{"t":"Prazosin","correct":false,"why":"Prazosin is a reversible, selective α1 antagonist."},{"t":"Terazosin","correct":false,"why":"An -osin: reversible, selective α1."},{"t":"None; all are reversible","correct":false,"why":"Phenoxybenzamine is the irreversible one."},{"t":"Mirtazapine","correct":false,"why":"Mirtazapine is a reversible α2 (and H1, muscarinic, α1) antagonist."}],"teach":"The α antagonists: irreversible, non-selective α1 and α2: phenoxybenzamine. Reversible, α1 selective: prazosin, terazosin, doxazosin, alfuzosin, tamsulosin, silodosin (the -osins). Reversible, α2 non-selective: mirtazapine (α2, H1, muscarinic, α1).","quote":"So all the other drugs are gonna be reversible and then we have here alpha-1 selectives all the zosin or Osin, right?","note":"He described phenoxybenzamine aloud (“a non-selective alpha 1 and alpha 2 irreversible antagonists”) while answering a student question, then said “we're gonna get a little bit ahead of us, so just wait”; its own slides (34–38) were not taught on 10/6.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 27; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-049', prof:'Gottlieb', "dupOf":"gtap-PE2-065","lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a1ant","skill":"tell","concept":"prazosin-moa","tags":["exam-cue"],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following describes prazosin?","options":[{"t":"Reversible, selective α1 antagonist","correct":true,"why":"Prazosin is about a thousandfold more selective for α1 and reversibly blocks catecholamines from binding it."},{"t":"Irreversible α1 and α2 antagonist","correct":false,"why":"Covalent, irreversible block of both α1 and α2 is phenoxybenzamine; prazosin is reversible and leaves α2 alone."},{"t":"Selective α2 antagonist","correct":false,"why":"An α2 antagonist (mirtazapine) removes the Gi brake on norepinephrine release and raises sympathetic activity; prazosin binds α1, and about 1000-fold less to α2."},{"t":"Selective α1 agonist","correct":false,"why":"A selective α1 agonist (phenylephrine) activates Gq and constricts; prazosin binds α1 with affinity and no efficacy, so the vessels dilate."},{"t":"Reversible, non-selective α antagonist","correct":false,"why":"Prazosin binds α1 about 1000 times more than α2, so it is selective; a non-selective reversible α blocker would also remove the α2 brake on norepinephrine release."}],"teach":"Prazosin is a reversible, selective α1 antagonist (about 1000-fold): it blocks norepinephrine and epinephrine from binding α1. Every drug ending in -osin (terazosin, doxazosin, tamsulosin, silodosin) is the same for the exam: a selective α1 antagonist acting at the α1 sites (eye, nose, blood vessels, urethra).","quote":"For our exam purpose, all I need to know if you see a drug with OC is a selective alpha-1 antagonist. And where the alpha ones are in the nose, in the eye, in the blood vessels, in the urethra, OK? Those are gonna be your sites of actions.","reading":[{"src":"Katzung 16e, Ch. 10","sec":"Specific Agents — Prazosin, terazosin, doxazosin","t":"Prazosin, terazosin and doxazosin are highly selective for α1 receptors. They dilate both arterial and venous smooth muscle, and smooth muscle in the prostate, by blocking α1 receptors; they are used as second-line treatment for hypertension and for urinary symptoms of benign prostatic hyperplasia."}],"cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 27–28; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-050', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a1ant","skill":"apply","concept":"alpha1-block-vasodilation","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Blocking α1 receptors in a patient with hypertension lowers blood pressure through:","options":[{"t":"Less Gq signaling → less Ca++ → vasodilation","correct":true,"why":"Norepinephrine cannot bind α1, so less Gq, PLC and IP3, less calcium, and the vessels relax: lower TPR."},{"t":"More Gi signaling → less cAMP → vasodilation","correct":false,"why":"Gi lowering cyclic AMP (cAMP) describes α2 activation, not α1 blockade."},{"t":"Less β1 signaling → lower heart rate","correct":false,"why":"Prazosin does not block β1; heart rate actually rises by reflex."},{"t":"More nitric oxide from the endothelium → vasodilation","correct":false,"why":"Nitric oxide comes from endothelial M3; α1 block works on smooth-muscle calcium."}],"teach":"In the arteries and veins, α1 is the main receptor and the sympathetic system the only (and so the primary physiological) tone: they are always constricting through Gq → PLC → IP3 → Ca++. Blocking α1 lowers that calcium, the vessels dilate, and systemic vascular resistance (total peripheral resistance, TPR) falls.","quote":"if I block it, norepinephrine can't bind to that receptor. As such, I have less GQ. If I have less GQ, I'm gonna have less calcium, and if I have less calcium, I have more vasodilation.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 29; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-051', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a1ant","skill":"apply","concept":"alpha1-block-vein-orthostatic","tags":["in-class"],"source":"both","stem":"What would you think would be a side effect of taking a drug that antagonizes the alpha one receptor in the vein?","options":[{"t":"Orthostatic hypotension and syncope","correct":true,"why":"Relaxed veins let blood pool in the feet; it does not reach the heart or brain, and the patient falls on standing."},{"t":"Rebound nasal congestion","correct":false,"why":"Rebound congestion follows an α1 agonist (phenylephrine) in the nose; an α1 antagonist in the vein relaxes the vessel and does not touch the nose."},{"t":"Bradycardia from a baroreceptor reflex","correct":false,"why":"Falling pressure raises, not lowers, heart rate by reflex."},{"t":"Bronchial constriction","correct":false,"why":"The airway smooth muscle carries M3 and β2, not α1; blocking α1 changes nothing in the bronchi."},{"t":"Higher preload","correct":false,"why":"Venous relaxation lowers preload."}],"teach":"First ask what the vein does: return blood to the heart (preload). An α1 antagonist relaxes venous tone, blood pools in the feet, does not reach the heart or the brain, and the patient falls: orthostatic hypotension and syncope. The side effect is predicted from mechanism and site, not memorized.","quote":"So if it's an alpha one antagonist in the veins, what that's gonna do to the vein, the venous tone? Is it gonna be constricted or relaxed? Relax. Relax. So if blood is, if your blood is pooling in my feet, what is not gonna go? It's not gonna go to the heart, it's not gonna go to the brain. So, you're gonna Fall off, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 29–30; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-052', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"ladder":"prazosin","ladderName":"Prazosin, norepinephrine and phenoxybenzamine","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a1ant","skill":"apply","concept":"alpha1-block-reflex-tachycardia","tags":["in-class"],"source":"both","stem":"Prazosin dilates the resistance arteries. Which response follows through the baroreceptors?","options":[{"t":"Reflex tachycardia (palpitations)","correct":true,"why":"The baroreceptors sense low pressure and raise sympathetic drive: reflex tachycardia, felt as palpitations."},{"t":"Reflex bradycardia","correct":false,"why":"Bradycardia is the reflex to a rise in pressure."},{"t":"Miosis","correct":false,"why":"Miosis is not a baroreflex response."},{"t":"Less sympathetic drive to the heart","correct":false,"why":"Low pressure increases sympathetic drive."}],"teach":"Dilating the arteries lowers blood pressure; the baroreceptors sense it and increase sympathetic outflow, giving reflex tachycardia (palpitations). Because of this, prazosin is not a good monotherapy; a β blocker may be added to block the tachycardia.","quote":"What about if it's the alpha ones in the arteries? If you vasodilate the arteries, now your barrels are gonna sense my blood pressure is too low. What is it gonna do? I'm gonna increase the sympathetic, I'm gonna get reflex tachycardia, so palpitations, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 30; transcript 10/6","note":"The teach keeps his aside that prazosin is not a good monotherapy and that a β blocker is added for the tachycardia; it is in his 10/6 words: “So it’s not a really drug, a really good drug to use as a monotherapy, but you’re gonna have to add like a beta blocker to block the tachycardia.”"},
{id:'gtap-L10-053', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a1ant","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"prazosin-adr","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which adverse effects are expected when a patient starts prazosin? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"First-dose orthostatic hypotension","correct":true,"why":"First dose effect: orthostatic hypotension and syncope (30–90 min)."},{"t":"Reflex tachycardia","correct":true,"why":"Baroreceptors respond to the fall in pressure."},{"t":"Blurred vision","correct":true,"why":"α1 in the eye is blocked, so the pupil cannot dilate properly."},{"t":"Headache","correct":true,"why":"A common adverse reaction for vasodilators."},{"t":"Bradycardia","correct":false,"why":"Heart rate rises by reflex."},{"t":"Hypertensive crisis on withdrawal","correct":false,"why":"A hypertensive crisis on withdrawal follows an α2 agonist (clonidine): the receptors up-regulate and a stop lets norepinephrine hit them all. Stopping prazosin removes a block, not an agonist."},{"t":"Rebound congestion","correct":false,"why":"Rebound congestion follows an α1 agonist (phenylephrine) in the nose; prazosin blocks α1 and causes stuffiness, not rebound."}],"teach":"Prazosin's adverse reactions: the first-dose effect (orthostatic hypotension and syncope, 30–90 minutes), reflex tachycardia (palpitations), dizziness, blurred vision and headache. Headache and flushing are common to vasodilators.","quote":"So we have that first dose effect that is gonna cause the reflex tachycardia because your barrels are gonna sense that your blood pressure is too low. ... We have alpha ones in the eyes, so we won't be able to dilate very properly, so you get the blurred vision.","note":"The transcript says “first dose orthostatic hypertension” and “orthostatic hypertension, also known as syncope”; the slide says “Orthostatic Hypotension & Syncope”, and he describes patients passing out. Keyed to the slide (hypotension).","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 30; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-054', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a1ant","skill":"apply","concept":"vasodilator-headache","tags":["prazosin"],"source":"both","stem":"A vasodilator such as prazosin is most likely to cause headache through:","options":[{"t":"Raised pressure inside the skull","correct":true,"why":"The brain is enclosed by the rigid skull; dilating its vessels raises pressure in the head (with flushing)."},{"t":"α2 block in the brain","correct":false,"why":"Prazosin binds α1, not α2, and the headache is a pressure effect of dilated vessels inside the skull, not a receptor effect in the brain."},{"t":"Constriction of the vessels in the brain","correct":false,"why":"An α1 blocker dilates; constriction (α1 activation) would lower the pressure in the head, not raise it."},{"t":"Less acetylcholine in the brain","correct":false,"why":"Prazosin has no affinity for cholinergic receptors or the enzyme; the headache is from dilated vessels in a rigid skull."}],"teach":"A general rule: a vasodilator most likely causes headache. Vessels in the face and brain dilate, and the brain sits inside the hard skull with little room, so pressure in the head rises; flushing goes with it.","quote":"So when you vasodilate those blood vessels, you increase the pressure in the head and you get the flushing and the headache associated with that. So one of the good general rules is if you are a vasodilator, most likely you're gonna get a headache for your patient, OK?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 30; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-055', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a1ant","skill":"apply","concept":"alpha1-antagonist-nose","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"What would an α1 antagonist do to nasal congestion?","options":[{"t":"Make it worse","correct":true,"why":"Blocking α1 relaxes the nasal blood vessels, which adds to the congestion."},{"t":"Clear it","correct":false,"why":"Clearing congestion needs vessel constriction, an α1 agonist (phenylephrine)."},{"t":"Cause rebound congestion only after 3 days","correct":false,"why":"Rebound congestion after days of use is the phenylephrine warning."},{"t":"Have no effect on the nose","correct":false,"why":"The nose is one of the α1 sites (“smell”)."}],"teach":"The nose is an α1 site. An α1 agonist (phenylephrine) constricts nasal vessels and relieves congestion; an α1 antagonist (prazosin) relaxes them and makes congestion worse, so α1 antagonists have no use as decongestants.","quote":"And then, uh, we don't really use alpha one antagonist to decongestant because it's gonna make it worse. It's gonna relax the blood vessels in the nose and it's gonna cause even more congestion for those patients, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 28; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-056', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a1ant","skill":"recall","concept":"arteries-veins-sympathetic-only","tags":["alpha1"],"source":"both","stem":"Which statement about the arteries and veins is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"They have only sympathetic innervation","correct":true,"why":"Sympathetic is the only (primary physiological) tone, so α1 always keeps some constriction."},{"t":"They have only parasympathetic innervation","correct":false,"why":"Parasympathetic-only is the lungs, not the vessels."},{"t":"Their main receptor is β1","correct":false,"why":"Their main receptor is α1."},{"t":"They are relaxed unless a drug is given","correct":false,"why":"They are always constricting to some degree to maintain blood pressure."}],"teach":"The arteries and veins are one of the autonomic exceptions: sympathetic innervation only, with α1 as the predominant receptor. They are always constricting; the question is how much. Removing that sympathetic tone (clonidine centrally, prazosin at the receptor) relaxes them.","quote":"There's only sympathetic to the arteries and the veins, right? So, you're always constricting. The question is how much you're constricting. So, are you constricting a lot or not a lot. You have to constrict somehow in order to maintain your blood pressure. But if you don't have sympathetics, relax","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 29; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-057', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-mirt","skill":"apply","concept":"mirtazapine-inhibit-inhibitor","tags":["in-class"],"source":"both","stem":"Mirtazapine, a presynaptic α2 antagonist, increases norepinephrine release by:","options":[{"t":"Removing the inhibitor of release","correct":true,"why":"α2 is the inhibitory (Gi) autoreceptor; blocking the inhibitor gives a positive effect: more norepinephrine."},{"t":"Blocking the norepinephrine transporter","correct":false,"why":"Blocking the transporter (cocaine) keeps released norepinephrine in the synapse longer; mirtazapine acts on the receptor that decides how much is released."},{"t":"Activating α1 on the nerve terminal","correct":false,"why":"Mirtazapine binds with affinity and no efficacy: it blocks α2, α1, H1 and muscarinic receptors and activates none of them."},{"t":"Inhibiting monoamine oxidase (MAO)","correct":false,"why":"Inhibiting MAO (phenelzine, selegiline) lets more norepinephrine be stored; mirtazapine does not touch the enzyme, it removes the Gi brake on release."}],"teach":"α2 autoreceptors are inhibitory (Gαi) and suppress norepinephrine release. Mirtazapine antagonizes them: inhibiting the inhibitor gives a positive effect, more norepinephrine (and serotonin) release, which helps decrease depression.","quote":"So what do you think is gonna happen to the levels of norepinephrine if I inhibit the alpha 2s? What happens if I inhibit the inhibitor? I get positive effect. So by inhibiting the alpha 2s in the CNS, I'm gonna get less inhibition from the G alphai pathway.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 31–32; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-058', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-mirt","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"mirtazapine-receptors","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which receptors does mirtazapine block? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"α2","correct":true,"why":"Its main action: presynaptic α2 autoreceptor antagonist."},{"t":"H1","correct":true,"why":"Histamine 1 receptors (sedation)."},{"t":"Muscarinic","correct":true,"why":"Muscarinic receptors (sedation)."},{"t":"α1","correct":true,"why":"It can block α1 as well."},{"t":"β1","correct":false,"why":"No β action was given for mirtazapine."},{"t":"Muscle nicotinic (Nm)","correct":false,"why":"Nm is the neuromuscular receptor; mirtazapine does not cause paralysis."}],"teach":"Mirtazapine is marketed as an α2 antagonist but also blocks histamine H1, muscarinic and α1 receptors. α2 block raises norepinephrine (antidepressant); H1 and muscarinic block cause sedation; α1 block adds its own effects.","quote":"Mirtazapine is our alpha-2 antagonist, but also can block H1s, it can block muscarinics, and it can block alpha-1s. So that's gonna be a fun drug to take, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 27, 31–32; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-059', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-mirt","skill":"apply","concept":"mirtazapine-sedation","tags":["in-class"],"source":"both","stem":"Mirtazapine helps depressed patients who also have insomnia fall asleep. Which receptor blockade accounts for this?","options":[{"t":"Histamine H1 and muscarinic","correct":true,"why":"Blocking histamine and muscarinic receptors in the brain causes sedation."},{"t":"Presynaptic α2 only","correct":false,"why":"α2 block raises norepinephrine, the antidepressant effect, not sleep."},{"t":"α1 and β1","correct":false,"why":"β1 is not one of its receptors, and sedation is tied to H1 and muscarinic block."},{"t":"The norepinephrine transporter","correct":false,"why":"Mirtazapine does not block the transporter."}],"teach":"Mirtazapine is adjunct therapy for major depressive disorder with insomnia. α2 block raises norepinephrine (less depression); H1 and muscarinic block in the brain causes sedation (sleep). The pharmacology explains the use.","quote":"What is the side effect of having a drug that blocks the muscarinic and the histamine receptors? Sedation, right? You're gonna put them to sleep. So this drug not only helps with the depression by increasing repinephrine, but also helps them to fall asleep","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 31–32; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-060', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-mirt","skill":"tell","concept":"clonidine-vs-mirtazapine","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following statements about the α2 drugs in the central nervous system is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"Clonidine lowers norepinephrine release","correct":true,"why":"Clonidine activates the inhibitory (Gi) α2 pathway: less norepinephrine release, less sympathetic outflow (↓CNS)."},{"t":"Mirtazapine lowers norepinephrine release","correct":false,"why":"Mirtazapine blocks α2 and enhances release (↑CNS)."},{"t":"Clonidine raises norepinephrine release","correct":false,"why":"Activating α2 suppresses release."},{"t":"Mirtazapine activates α2 receptors","correct":false,"why":"Mirtazapine is the α2 antagonist."}],"teach":"Same receptor, opposite drugs. Clonidine (α2 agonist) activates the Gi pathway: less norepinephrine, less sympathetic (↓CNS). Mirtazapine (α2 antagonist) removes that inhibition: more norepinephrine, more sympathetic (↑CNS).","quote":"So if you think about the CNS, clonidine is going to activate the inhibitory pathway, I get less sympathetic, as mirtazapine is going to enhance the sympathetic.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 21; transcript 10/6"},
{id:'gtap-L10-061', prof:'Gottlieb', "lecture":"gtap-L10","tier":"new","level":2,"ladder":"clonidine","ladderName":"Clonidine: α2, an add-on, and stopping it","topic":"gtap-L10","sub":"gtap-a2","skill":"apply","concept":"alpha2-agonist-plus-stimulant","tags":["ddi"],"source":"both","stem":"A boy with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) needs a high dose of methylphenidate and has lost his appetite. Guanfacine, an α2 agonist similar to clonidine, is added, and the methylphenidate dose is lowered. Which of the following statements is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"The two drugs have an additive effect on attention","correct":true,"why":"The α2 agonist adds to the stimulant, so attention is kept with less methylphenidate and less loss of appetite."},{"t":"Guanfacine blocks the norepinephrine transporter","correct":false,"why":"Blocking the norepinephrine transporter (NET) is cocaine's mechanism; guanfacine is an α2 agonist."},{"t":"Guanfacine raises the release of norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"An α2 agonist acts on an inhibitory (Gi) receptor and suppresses norepinephrine release."},{"t":"Guanfacine slows the breakdown of methylphenidate","correct":false,"why":"Guanfacine acts on the α2 receptor, not on how methylphenidate is broken down."},{"t":"Guanfacine is an α1 agonist that raises blood pressure","correct":false,"why":"Guanfacine is an α2 agonist; like clonidine it lowers sympathetic tone."}],"teach":"Methylphenidate is a stimulant: it mainly releases norepinephrine and dopamine, which improves focus but decreases appetite. Clonidine and guanfacine are α2 agonists, and both are used for ADHD. Adding guanfacine to the stimulant gave an additive effect, so the stimulant dose could come down and attention was kept without the loss of appetite: a good drug–drug interaction.","quote":"He was in such a high dose of Ritalin that we had to lower the dose and add a clonidine, so we didn't really add, we add the Intuniv, which is a similar drug to it, so that way they have this additive effect and he could maintain his attention without affecting his appetite.","note":"Taken from his own example on 10/6 (methylphenidate with guanfacine, which he called similar to clonidine). The -idine rule does not fit guanfacine; slide 22 lists it with the α2 agonists.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 11–12, 22; transcript 10/6"},
);
